WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:07.080
In June of 1985, Mark Johnson and his wife of&nbsp;
ten years, Sophie, were on vacation in Myrtle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:12.480
Beach having a great time. While they relaxed on&nbsp;
the sand, Sophie decided to take a picture of her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:17.520
husband for their scrapbook, so she pulled&nbsp;
out the Polaroid Supercolor 645 that she'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:22.080
bought specifically for the trip. She pressed&nbsp;
the button, waited for the picture to print,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:27.480
pulled it out, and shook it. What she expected&nbsp;
to see was a shot of Mark lying on his beach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:33.120
towel. What she got, was a picture of&nbsp;
her husband in the arms of his secretary.

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:36.000
They say that a picture speaks a thousand words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.880
but this particular camera takes pictures that&nbsp;
are worth even more than that. When developed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:46.860
the pictures taken with this normal-looking&nbsp;
polaroid camera reveal the innermost desires&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:54.600
of the person being photographed. Welcome to&nbsp;
the story of SCP - 978 - The Desire Camera.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:01:00.000
The main reason the foundation keeps&nbsp;
SCP 978 under wraps is due to its use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.100
as an easy form of blackmail. All anyone&nbsp;
with access to the camera would have to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:10.020
is snap a few covert photos and they'd have&nbsp;
instant dirt on the world's most powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:13.620
people. To further stop this camera&nbsp;
from falling into the wrong hands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:18.120
the containment guidelines strictly forbid&nbsp;
the use of the camera for anything other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:23.340
than simple documentation, and all uses of&nbsp;
it must be thoroughly logged and checked.

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:29.100
So, now that you know what the camera does,&nbsp;
it’s time to look at what kinds of strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:34.260
and mysterious photos it’s taken. After all,&nbsp;
who doesn't want a chance to gain insight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:38.340
into the minds of the Foundation's&nbsp;
most famous and dangerous inmates?

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:45.000
First, to serve as a control the team took a photo&nbsp;
of a blue office chair. No change was recorded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.320
proving that the camera didn't work&nbsp;
on non-anomalous inanimate objects.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:54.240
Some of the earliest tests of the camera were the&nbsp;
photos that the research team took of each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:59.940
One by one, the doctors and their assistants&nbsp;
were photographed in neutral poses, smiling as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:05.820
if for a completely normal photo, then they waited&nbsp;
while the photos developed like normal polaroids.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:11.400
Most of the research team had minimal changes in&nbsp;
their photos. One was shown slouched in his chair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:16.500
drinking from a flask. One was shown in her&nbsp;
civilian clothes, getting ready to go home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:21.720
from work. One doctor was in the exact same&nbsp;
pose, but with a different colored tie on.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:26.580
The last assistant photographed, however, had&nbsp;
their picture come out wildly different than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:31.620
how they were posed. Their desire revealed&nbsp;
by the camera was so shocking that it had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:36.180
to be redacted, and the assistant was&nbsp;
placed on temporary psychiatric leave.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:40.920
Following this first successful test, the&nbsp;
doctor in charge of the project ordered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:45.780
a second round of tests to be conducted,&nbsp;
this time on a series of D-class personnel.

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:49.980
A picture of one D-class, who was&nbsp;
photographed while eating in the cafeteria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:54.120
showed up as scuba diving on vacation&nbsp;
with his wife and child. He was allowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:58.800
to keep the picture afterwards. Another had&nbsp;
a similar result - he was photographed in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:03.000
neutral pose like the members of the research&nbsp;
team had been, and when the picture developed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:08.340
the D-Class was seen holding a smiling baby&nbsp;
whom he identified as his one-year-old daughter.

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:14.160
After that, the results of the SCP -&nbsp;
978 tests started to get a little… well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:20.340
weird. A notoriously twisted D-class known&nbsp;
as 7294 was photographed in his cell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:25.200
and when the polaroid developed, he was in&nbsp;
the same cell, playing a cello made out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:32.220
a dismembered mannequin. D-217-017,&nbsp;
was a late stage victim of SCP-217,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:37.800
a virus that turns organic matter into metal.&nbsp;
You would assume that her innermost desire would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:43.620
be to be cured of her disease, but when she was&nbsp;
photographed in her cell, the resulting polaroid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:49.680
showed her completely made of metal, kneeling&nbsp;
and praying at the base of a black obelisk.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:54.840
One D-class, who had been rendered comatose&nbsp;
and was on life support, was photographed in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:59.820
their hospital room. Their picture showed them&nbsp;
as only a limp, deflated skin in their photo.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:05.040
The photograph of another D-class, who had&nbsp;
become increasingly listless and apathetic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:10.260
since their integration into the D-class program,&nbsp;
resulted in a picture that was exactly the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:14.640
as they appeared in reality, except the&nbsp;
light in their cell was slightly dimmer.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:19.560
Intrigued by these results, the research team&nbsp;
wanted to see how the camera would react to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:26.520
SCP-148, a type of metal that blocks the psychic&nbsp;
properties of other SCPs. A helmet was made from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:31.260
the material, and placed on a D-class who was&nbsp;
given an adult magazine to read while his photo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:36.780
was taken. When the photo was developed, it&nbsp;
was extremely blurry. Upon close analysis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:40.320
the figure of the D-class reading&nbsp;
the magazine could still be made out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:44.400
and around him the faint images of&nbsp;
several nude people could also be seen.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:48.000
The research team next wanted to see&nbsp;
if the results were the same when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:52.740
camera was used on animals. Photos of the&nbsp;
lab mice and rabbits showed them eating or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:57.420
mating in their respective polaroids. A&nbsp;
zebra finch was shown escaping its cage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.420 --> 00:05:02.520
while a guard dog and its handler were shown&nbsp;
playing fetch in the park. When cockroaches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:06.840
were photographed, there was no difference&nbsp;
visible between the picture and reality.

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:12.360
But the most exciting test results came&nbsp;
when the camera was tested on other SCPs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:17.340
For a few of the SCPs tested, there was no&nbsp;
visible difference between the picture and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:24.300
reality. This was the case for SCP-173&nbsp;
the Sculpture, SCP-343 nicknamed God,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:30.300
SCP-014 - an immortal man who is,&nbsp;
over time, being turned into concrete,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:38.340
and SCP-063 - the world's best toothbrush.
When SCP-035 - better known as the Possessive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:43.380
Mask - was photographed in its glass case,&nbsp;
the picture showed an unidentified individual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:48.840
wearing the mask, sitting on a throne&nbsp;
and surrounded by three masked corpses.

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:54.480
The team took a picture of SCP-043 - a&nbsp;
haunted copy of The White Album that was being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:59.940
interrogated while on a turntable. In the picture,&nbsp;
the album was being returned to its sleeve.

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:05.460
They then photographed SCP-049, the Plague&nbsp;
Doctor, standing in his cell. In the picture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:13.260
the plague doctor is hugging an unidentified small&nbsp;
child, and surrounded by instances of SCP-049-2.

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:18.900
When they took a picture of SCP-050 - a&nbsp;
mischievous statue of a monkey reading a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:24.540
book - the result was predictably playful, given&nbsp;
that SCP's history of pulling pranks. The photo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:29.460
that came out of the camera showed the camera&nbsp;
itself, with another picture of itself coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:35.280
out of the developing slot. Microscopic analysis&nbsp;
revealed that this recursive photography kept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:40.260
going until the cameras in the pictures of&nbsp;
the other cameras were too small to see.

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:45.960
The team next photographed SCP-053,&nbsp;
the young girl and the photo was taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:49.500
as the young girl was drawing with&nbsp;
crayons in her cell. In the picture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:56.640
she was riding through a sunny field on&nbsp;
the back of her friend SCP-682. SCP-053&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:02.760
and SCP-682 were even wearing matching&nbsp;
bows, dresses, and pink nail polish.

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:06.540
When they took a picture of&nbsp;
SCP-096 - the Shy Guy though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:10.860
the picture showed SCP-096's&nbsp;
cell with nothing in it.

00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:16.500
SCP-085 - the living 2D sketch known&nbsp;
as Cassy - was photographed tinkering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:21.300
with a drawn car. In the developed photo,&nbsp;
Cassy was engaged in the same activity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:24.720
but both she and the car were now 3-dimensional.

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:30.780
The team took a photo of SCP-106, the Old&nbsp;
Man, emerging from a wall within his cell.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:35.340
When the photo developed, it showed the&nbsp;
old man roaming a wheat field near an old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:42.360
farmhouse. From the porch of the farm house, an&nbsp;
older woman is greeting SCP-106 with open arms.

00:07:42.360 --> 00:07:47.820
They also took a photo of SCP-168 - a&nbsp;
sentient calculator - sitting on a table&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:52.440
in its containment cell. In the picture,&nbsp;
a teenage boy is seen doing complex math&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:57.000
homework using the calculator. Next to him&nbsp;
on the table are thick textbooks with titles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:02.220
like "Complicated Math", "Difficult Math",&nbsp;
and "Math You Need Your Calculator For".

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:07.920
The photo of SCP-261 - a vending machine&nbsp;
that dispenses snack products from alternate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:13.860
universes - showed the SCP in its usual&nbsp;
hallway, almost completely buried in coins.

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:19.920
SCP-507, an otherwise normal man who&nbsp;
unwillingly teleports between dimensions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:23.340
was photographed while eating&nbsp;
in the cafeteria. The developed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:27.660
picture showed him dressed as a Foundation&nbsp;
researcher, talking to other researchers.

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:32.580
When SCP-682 was photographed,&nbsp;
floating in its containment tank,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:37.740
the resulting picture showed the SCP&nbsp;
surrounded by the corpses of all of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:42.480
Foundation staff and mauling the person&nbsp;
who had taken the photo. A researcher’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:47.880
note accompanying this photo reads “Well,&nbsp;
that was predictable.” Yes. Yes it was.

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:52.920
Just for fun of it, the team also tested the&nbsp;
camera's effects on a few other Foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:57.900
personnel. They took a photo of Dr. Bright&nbsp;
while he was arguing with Dr. Clef. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.900 --> 00:09:02.100
picture that came out showed a tombstone&nbsp;
engraved with the words "Jack Bright,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:08.040
Resting at Last." The team also took a&nbsp;
photo of Dr. Clef as he stormed out in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:13.140
huff following the argument. Given that Clef's&nbsp;
face cannot be photographed, they were curious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:17.820
as to what the results would be. The picture&nbsp;
showed little difference compared to reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:23.280
save for the fact that Clef's head was replaced&nbsp;
by an image of a hand making a rude gesture.

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:27.900
Upon closer examination of the photo, the&nbsp;
team also realized that there was a young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:35.280
girl in the background who, strangely, has cloven&nbsp;
hooves instead of feet. A young SCP-166 perhaps?

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:39.000
The team then took a photo of Dr.&nbsp;
Fisher holding his pet hamster.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:45.000
Contrary to expectation, the picture revealed&nbsp;
not Dr. Fisher's desires, but the hamster's,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:51.060
showing the hamster sleeping peacefully in&nbsp;
its cage… with Dr. Fisher nowhere to be seen.

00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:57.960
As you can see from these test logs, the effects&nbsp;
of the camera are, well, inconsistent. With some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.960 --> 00:10:03.420
photos showing very immediate, simple whims, and&nbsp;
others showing the subject's deepest and most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:09.360
secret desires. While the researchers working with&nbsp;
SCP-978 seemed like they had a lot of fun getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:14.640
to peek into the minds of all their coworkers&nbsp;
and test subjects, there's no telling what kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:19.920
of pandemonium could be unleashed if someone&nbsp;
on the outside ever got a chance to use it.

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:24.120
Thankfully the camera, which has been&nbsp;
classified as safe for obvious reasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:30.360
is kept securely in security locker H-J-12&nbsp;
at site 17. Which means none of us have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:35.040
worry about our own pictures taken by the&nbsp;
Desire Camera leaking out. But if someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:39.300
did get the camera out and took your&nbsp;
picture, what do you think they’d see?

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:46.560
It was the year 2000 and Seven-year-old Xenia&nbsp;
Chau was like a lot of children her age - bright,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:50.940
but ignored. She had a youthful&nbsp;
enthusiasm for knowledge and learning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.940 --> 00:10:55.740
and with the right encouragement, it was&nbsp;
obvious she’d go on to do great things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.740 --> 00:11:00.000
but her parents were both working&nbsp;
two jobs apiece to make ends meet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:04.380
so outside of school hours, little&nbsp;
Xenia spent a lot of her time alone.

00:11:04.380 --> 00:11:09.420
She learned to cook and clean for herself&nbsp;
since it would often be way past her bedtime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:14.160
when her parents finally got home from work.&nbsp;
They communicated largely through the sticky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:17.580
notes left for her on the fridge before&nbsp;
they left for work in the morning.

00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:23.100
It would be easy for someone like Xenia to&nbsp;
fall through the cracks, her potential wasted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.100 --> 00:11:28.020
and get forced into the same endless rat&nbsp;
race as her parents. And that probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:34.860
would have happened, if her brush with SCP-5094&nbsp;
hadn’t changed the course of her life forever.

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:39.120
Like a lot of parents who couldn’t spend as&nbsp;
much time with their children as they’d like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:45.300
Mr. And Mrs. Chau gave Xenia occasional gifts as a&nbsp;
way to try and make up for their absence. Mostly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.300 --> 00:11:48.540
they gave Xenia new books to&nbsp;
add to her growing collection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:53.820
but their two-bedroom apartment only&nbsp;
had so much space for books. A year ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.820 --> 00:11:59.340
the family had bought a new desktop computer&nbsp;
to share. These days, this would be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.340 --> 00:12:04.560
answer to a lonely bookworm’s prayers, but the&nbsp;
Internet was still in its infancy back then.

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:10.800
Everything changed when her parents bought her a&nbsp;
copy of the Miss J's Whiz Kidz Schoolhouse CD-ROM.

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:16.080
Miss J's Whiz Kidz Schoolhouse was never a&nbsp;
popular program. Produced by Shoot the Moons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:21.660
Software in 1999, the game sold poorly on the&nbsp;
CD-ROM market for five years until the company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.660 --> 00:12:27.660
filed for bankruptcy in 2004. But that didn’t&nbsp;
matter to Xenia. As far as she was concerned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:32.040
receiving a copy of Miss J's Whiz Kidz&nbsp;
Schoolhouse was nothing short of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:37.080
miracle - and she was far more right than&nbsp;
she knew. She didn’t have just any piece&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:43.140
of primitive educational software on her&nbsp;
hands. Xenia Chau was playing with magic.

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:49.320
After the software loaded, a cheerful figure&nbsp;
appeared on Xenia’s monitor. This was Miss J,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.320 --> 00:12:56.520
a stylized 2D teacher in a low-res but colorful 3D&nbsp;
classroom. What can you learn from Miss J? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.520 --> 00:13:02.520
pretty much anything. And Xenia as a lonely kid&nbsp;
without many friends had all the time in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:08.460
world. She spent days on end learning from Miss&nbsp;
J and soon Xenia went from a bright child to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.460 --> 00:13:14.280
bonafide child genius. Her largely absent parents&nbsp;
barely even noticed when their child started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.280 --> 00:13:20.280
demonstrating an advanced knowledge of mechanical&nbsp;
engineering and a newfound fluency in Swahili.

00:13:20.280 --> 00:13:26.520
And not only did Miss J, now known to Xenia&nbsp;
as Miss Joyce, teach her students with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.520 --> 00:13:33.000
supernatural effectiveness, she also seemed&nbsp;
almost like she was alive. She was a warm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:38.460
calm, encouraging figure who took a&nbsp;
genuine, personal enthusiasm in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.460 --> 00:13:43.080
education and happiness of her students,&nbsp;
more than many real teachers, even.

00:13:43.080 --> 00:13:48.120
But sadly, not all good things last&nbsp;
forever. When Xenia turned twelve,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.120 --> 00:13:54.180
her family bought a new computer, and the old&nbsp;
Miss J's Whiz Kidz Schoolhouse CD-ROM was no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.180 --> 00:13:58.800
longer compatible with the new operating&nbsp;
system. The disk gathered dust in a closet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.800 --> 00:14:02.940
while Xenia passed through high school&nbsp;
and then got into a prestigious college.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:08.700
She received her PhD several years earlier&nbsp;
than expected and showed such promise in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.700 --> 00:14:15.420
her scientific field that a certain organization&nbsp;
took notice. You guessed it: The SCP Foundation.

00:14:15.420 --> 00:14:21.120
The Foundation is only interested in adding the&nbsp;
best and brightest to their ranks, and thanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.120 --> 00:14:27.180
to the tutoring from Miss J, Xenia Chau certainly&nbsp;
fit the bill. She was assigned the role of Junior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.180 --> 00:14:33.900
Researcher, and little did she know, she was about&nbsp;
to run into an old friend in her new line of work.

00:14:33.900 --> 00:14:38.760
The Foundation first discovered evidence&nbsp;
of Miss J’s anomalous qualities while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.760 --> 00:14:45.060
researching a series of strange forum posts&nbsp;
in 2012. On a thread discussing abandonware,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.060 --> 00:14:48.540
which is software for which official&nbsp;
support is no longer available,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.540 --> 00:14:54.120
there were multiple reports of a so-called&nbsp;
“living character” in an old CD-ROM many of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.120 --> 00:14:59.160
them had owned as children. Seeing as the&nbsp;
technology to use the CD-ROM was obsolete,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.160 --> 00:15:04.740
Shoot the Moons had reported extremely low sales&nbsp;
for the title, and nobody on the forum had kept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:10.560
their copy, the Foundation classified this&nbsp;
apparent anomaly as “neutralized.” No problem.

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:16.200
That was, until then Junior Researcher&nbsp;
Xenia Chau was briefed on this particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:22.020
SCP ten years later. She remembered exactly&nbsp;
what the forum posters were talking about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.020 --> 00:15:28.140
and what’s more, she still had her old copy&nbsp;
at home. The senior Foundation researchers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.140 --> 00:15:33.240
were astounded. An opportunity to study&nbsp;
this forgotten anomaly had just fallen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:37.860
into their laps, and it was all thanks&nbsp;
to their brilliant new Junior Researcher.

00:15:37.860 --> 00:15:41.040
Xenia’s superiors ordered her to bring in her copy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.040 --> 00:15:46.800
of the Miss J's Whiz Kidz Schoolhouse&nbsp;
CD-ROM, and then, real testing began.

00:15:46.800 --> 00:15:52.200
And after rigorous examination of the&nbsp;
software, it appears, by all accounts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:58.080
that Miss J really is the world’s greatest teacher&nbsp;
- in spite of the fact she’s a collection of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.080 --> 00:16:04.020
pixels on a basic CD-ROM program from the&nbsp;
tail end of the 1990s. What Miss J seemed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.020 --> 00:16:09.060
to specialize in was intensive courses. She&nbsp;
had the ability to make students certifiable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.060 --> 00:16:13.980
experts in almost any subject through&nbsp;
courses that can last upwards of 30 hours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.980 --> 00:16:19.860
and spread out over just a few days. But&nbsp;
this isn’t a “students become so enraptured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.860 --> 00:16:23.340
in learning that they forget accounting&nbsp;
for their most basic needs and experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.340 --> 00:16:28.080
a decline in the process” story. Miss J’s&nbsp;
lesson plans account for regular bathroom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:34.320
meal, and even recreation breaks for students.&nbsp;
Her talents easily account for Xenia Chau’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.320 --> 00:16:40.260
remarkable academic success, the same success&nbsp;
that got her picked up by the SCP Foundation.

00:16:40.260 --> 00:16:45.660
Naturally, if they had a technology on their&nbsp;
hands that could reliably create experts and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.660 --> 00:16:51.780
geniuses with seemingly no negative consequences,&nbsp;
the Foundation knew that Miss J and what she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.780 --> 00:16:57.420
had to teach could be immensely valuable in&nbsp;
their mission to effectively secure, contain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.420 --> 00:17:02.880
and protect other anomalies. But we’re not&nbsp;
talking about the Chaos Insurgency here:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.880 --> 00:17:08.280
The Foundation would never start to use an anomaly&nbsp;
for their own benefit without proper testing.

00:17:08.280 --> 00:17:14.940
So, that’s exactly what they did. The&nbsp;
experiments on SCP-5094 began at Site - 15,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.940 --> 00:17:20.400
and after ascertaining that Miss J posed no&nbsp;
real threat to her students, they recruited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.400 --> 00:17:26.340
a local elementary school to provide human test&nbsp;
subjects closer to Miss J’s intended demographic.

00:17:26.340 --> 00:17:31.320
The Foundation found that there were a few&nbsp;
constants between every lesson given by Miss J to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.320 --> 00:17:36.720
one of her students. Before teaching the really&nbsp;
mind-blowingly complex stuff, she would always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.720 --> 00:17:42.420
begin with a basic primer on letters, colors,&nbsp;
and numbers to make sure she and her students&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.420 --> 00:17:48.360
were all on the same page. Another constant was&nbsp;
that all her students would refer to her as some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.360 --> 00:17:54.720
variation of “Miss J” with examples including the&nbsp;
names "Miss Julie", "Miss Jenny", and "Miss Joy".

00:17:54.720 --> 00:18:01.140
One experiment was performed on Amy Myers, a&nbsp;
little girl from the third grade. Her academic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.140 --> 00:18:06.420
record showed that she was a bright and eager&nbsp;
student, but she was described by her teachers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.420 --> 00:18:12.900
as being especially shy. Amy referred to Miss J&nbsp;
as “Miss Janie”, and asked to be given a lesson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.900 --> 00:18:19.380
on her favorite animal - cats. Of course, Miss&nbsp;
J gladly obliged, and gave Amy a fifteen hour&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.380 --> 00:18:25.020
lesson split over the course of two days which&nbsp;
the Foundation closely observed. By the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.020 --> 00:18:31.020
the lesson was completed, the results were truly&nbsp;
astonishing: Amy demonstrated knowledge of feline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.020 --> 00:18:37.020
behavior, diet, and physiology on the level&nbsp;
of professional animal behavioral scientists.

00:18:37.020 --> 00:18:41.100
She became upset when the session&nbsp;
ended and the program was switched off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.100 --> 00:18:46.140
having become attached to Miss J. No&nbsp;
further anomalous effects were reported.

00:18:46.140 --> 00:18:51.720
The next subject was a student from the second&nbsp;
grade named Devon Williams. Devon had a poor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.720 --> 00:18:57.240
academic record, and behavioral issues stemming&nbsp;
from several learning disabilities. In the past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.240 --> 00:19:01.380
teachers had reported Devon having real&nbsp;
difficulty engaging with their lessons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.380 --> 00:19:07.020
and the Foundation wanted to see if Miss J’s&nbsp;
supernatural teaching abilities fared any better.

00:19:07.020 --> 00:19:12.600
Devon chose to be educated on the subject of&nbsp;
trains, and was given a fifteen hour course on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.600 --> 00:19:18.300
the subject spread over two days. Devon had&nbsp;
no difficulty engaging with these lessons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.300 --> 00:19:21.900
and after their conclusion, he&nbsp;
displayed a level of knowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.900 --> 00:19:26.520
on railway engineering comparable to a&nbsp;
graduate-level education on the subject.

00:19:26.520 --> 00:19:31.740
Devon was so engaged in his lessons that&nbsp;
he had to be physically removed from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.740 --> 00:19:36.960
room once the course had completed, because&nbsp;
he’d become so attached to his new teacher.

00:19:36.960 --> 00:19:39.660
The Foundation were feeling extremely optimistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:39.660 --> 00:19:43.380
about the prospect of using Miss&nbsp;
J to train potential recruits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.380 --> 00:19:49.380
seeing as she seemed to be able to teach even&nbsp;
the most difficult juvenile subjects. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:49.380 --> 00:19:55.020
the Foundation needed to prove that Miss J&nbsp;
could be just as effective in teaching adults.

00:19:55.020 --> 00:20:02.160
For this next test they recruited D-14417,&nbsp;
a 24 year old member of D-Class personnel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:02.160 --> 00:20:07.440
who described himself as a poor student, and&nbsp;
tested poorly on pre-screening attentiveness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:12.900
and focus evaluations. He was the perfect&nbsp;
candidate for the first adult test of Miss J.

00:20:12.900 --> 00:20:20.460
To the delight of the Foundation higher-ups,&nbsp;
D-14417 excelled. Miss J made him a verifiable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:20.460 --> 00:20:25.800
expert in Law, and he was able to pass a&nbsp;
mock bar exam with a grade of 310 after his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.800 --> 00:20:32.100
course. Much like the children, he felt intense&nbsp;
sadness once the experiment ended - stating that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.100 --> 00:20:37.260
Miss J was the best teacher he’d ever had.&nbsp;
He’s since been selectively anesthetized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.260 --> 00:20:41.160
released, and is pursuing a degree in&nbsp;
criminal law with his new knowledge.

00:20:41.160 --> 00:20:46.320
Needless to say, The Foundation was impressed.&nbsp;
The last thing they needed to know was who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.320 --> 00:20:52.500
exactly this Miss J truly was. To do this,&nbsp;
they’d conduct an interview, and thankfully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.500 --> 00:20:57.840
they already had someone who had a pre-established&nbsp;
rapport with the subject: Xenia Chau.

00:20:57.840 --> 00:21:04.320
When Xenia began the interview, Miss J recognized&nbsp;
her immediately. She was delighted to see one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:04.320 --> 00:21:10.200
her students go on to find such success in her&nbsp;
chosen field. Xenia questioned Miss J on what she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:10.200 --> 00:21:16.080
truly was, and Miss J responded that she honestly&nbsp;
wasn’t sure. All she knew for sure was that she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.080 --> 00:21:20.640
was passionate about helping lonely children&nbsp;
who love to learn embrace their education,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:20.640 --> 00:21:25.620
that’s really all there was to it. And it&nbsp;
appears that she was telling the truth.

00:21:25.620 --> 00:21:31.440
So far the Foundation has found nothing that&nbsp;
hints at a nefarious motivation behind Miss J’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:31.440 --> 00:21:36.480
actions. It would appear that she truly&nbsp;
does just want to help children. Xenia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:36.480 --> 00:21:40.920
touched by the kindness that this Safe-Class&nbsp;
anomaly showed to her as a neglected child,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:40.920 --> 00:21:47.700
and having now seen the positive impact she had&nbsp;
on children like her, thanked Miss J for all she’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:47.700 --> 00:21:52.980
done and left the interview happy that there&nbsp;
was an SCP out there who just wanted to change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.980 --> 00:21:58.740
people’s lives for the better. And probably all of&nbsp;
us would be a little better off if there were more

00:21:58.740 --> 00:22:05.160
After all, this was one of the few SCPs who’d&nbsp;
changed a few good people’s lives for the better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:05.160 --> 00:22:11.220
and all of us can probably learn something&nbsp;
from the kindness and patience of Miss J.

00:22:11.220 --> 00:22:16.560
Dr. Lola Bergman was an accomplished senior&nbsp;
researcher for the SCP Foundation. Her years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:16.560 --> 00:22:21.600
of hard work studying anomalies and dedication to&nbsp;
the Foundation had earned her a Level 3 security&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.600 --> 00:22:27.060
clearance, allowing her to access a bevy of secret&nbsp;
information across the Foundation’s vast network.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:27.060 --> 00:22:33.120
As a researcher and scientist, Dr. Bergman was&nbsp;
the naturally inquisitive type. She wanted to know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:33.120 --> 00:22:38.280
everything there was to know about the strange and&nbsp;
esoteric universe around her. That’s why, when she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:38.280 --> 00:22:44.400
heard about the file for SCP – 2000, one of the&nbsp;
organization’s best kept secrets, she was hooked.&nbsp;

00:22:44.400 --> 00:22:49.740
The only problem was that just to view the file,&nbsp;
you had to be on the O5 council or have a special&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:49.740 --> 00:22:55.800
subset of Level 4 clearance known as 4/2000.&nbsp;
Unfortunately, Dr. Bergman was only a Level 3.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:55.800 --> 00:23:02.280
But you know what they say: The forbidden fruit&nbsp;
always tastes the sweetest. Dr. Bergman had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:02.280 --> 00:23:06.420
given the Foundation years of service. What&nbsp;
could the harm be in breaking the rules and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:06.420 --> 00:23:11.760
getting a peek behind the curtain just this once?
Through somewhat deceptive means, Dr. Bergman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:11.760 --> 00:23:17.400
managed to get her hands on some 4/2000 clearance&nbsp;
credentials, and decided to access the file on an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:17.400 --> 00:23:22.500
encrypted personal device. They’d never even&nbsp;
know. But as she entered the credentials, she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:22.500 --> 00:23:28.680
found herself suddenly looking at an image. On its&nbsp;
face, nothing, an image of a dirt road in a field,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:28.680 --> 00:23:35.040
with tiny white text reading “…Remember us…” But&nbsp;
this was a powerful cognitohazardous image capable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:35.040 --> 00:23:39.840
of causing immediate incapacitation. The next&nbsp;
thing she knew, she was laying on the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:39.840 --> 00:23:46.380
unconscious. When she woke up, she was no longer a&nbsp;
doctor at the SCP Foundation. She was on a street&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:46.380 --> 00:23:52.500
corner, unable to remember her own name, let alone&nbsp;
her history. All she could do was murmur something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:52.500 --> 00:23:58.080
about a secret under Yellowstone National Park…
Dr. Bergman’s years of service had meant nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:58.080 --> 00:24:04.020
in the end. SCP – 2000 is an anomalous&nbsp;
asset so vital to not only the Foundation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:04.020 --> 00:24:09.600
but the entire world, that so much as attempting&nbsp;
to access it without the proper clearance leads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:09.600 --> 00:24:15.120
to immediate termination. But what’s really hidden&nbsp;
under Yellowstone National Park? And why are the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:15.120 --> 00:24:19.920
Foundation higher-ups so intent on keeping it&nbsp;
a secret, even from most of their own ranks?&nbsp;

00:24:19.920 --> 00:24:25.440
Of course, Yellowstone National Park itself is&nbsp;
no stranger to anomalous activity. The entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:25.440 --> 00:24:32.340
national park is technically an SCP known as&nbsp;
SCP – 1422. While it has since been neutralized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:32.340 --> 00:24:38.040
SCP-1422 caused all members of the&nbsp;
SCP Foundation to have no awareness of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:38.040 --> 00:24:43.920
Yellowstone National Park prior to 2007.
But the true nature of SCP – 2000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:43.920 --> 00:24:49.920
the secret hidden under Yellowstone National&nbsp;
Park, is not only stranger than SCP – 1422,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:49.920 --> 00:24:57.300
but also – depending on your perspective – either&nbsp;
profoundly relieving or utterly terrifying. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:57.300 --> 00:25:02.880
before you can truly understand the purpose and&nbsp;
gravity of SCP – 2000, or the Deus Ex Machina,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:02.880 --> 00:25:09.660
we need to talk about the end of the world.
In Foundation parlance, K-class scenarios are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:09.660 --> 00:25:15.300
events which have drastic implications on the&nbsp;
state of society, normality, or even life. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:15.300 --> 00:25:20.280
most dangerous and feared of all of these are XK&nbsp;
– Class Scenarios, which are, for all intents and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:20.280 --> 00:25:26.160
purposes, the apocalypse. There are a worrying&nbsp;
number of anomalies capable of bringing about an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:26.160 --> 00:25:31.020
XK Class scenario if the Foundation ever dropped&nbsp;
the ball on their protection or containment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:31.020 --> 00:25:36.060
While the Foundation believes that prevention is&nbsp;
always preferable to the cure, what happens when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:36.060 --> 00:25:41.520
prevention doesn’t work? What happens when our&nbsp;
worst fears are realized, and the end truly comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:41.520 --> 00:25:50.040
for us all? The answer is, SCP – 2000 happens.
Simply put, this anomaly is a reset button. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:50.040 --> 00:25:55.560
chance to start over when all life has been&nbsp;
compromised by anomalous activity. 2000 is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:55.560 --> 00:26:00.300
giant and mysterious facility beneath Yellowstone&nbsp;
National Park. The entrance is disguised as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:00.300 --> 00:26:05.340
a disused park ranger station, feeding into a&nbsp;
facility that burrows miles down into the earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:05.340 --> 00:26:10.260
It’s a place that contains incredible anomalous&nbsp;
technologies capable of bringing the world back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:10.260 --> 00:26:16.500
to its former glory, even after the worst happens.
Only a select few personnel are allowed to work at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:16.500 --> 00:26:21.900
the facility, keeping its machinery maintained&nbsp;
in case the day comes when we ever need to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:21.900 --> 00:26:26.520
it again. These personnel are not allowed to&nbsp;
come and go as they please and their memories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:26.520 --> 00:26:31.440
of 2000 are wiped if they’re reassigned to&nbsp;
different Foundation duties elsewhere. Why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:31.440 --> 00:26:38.700
all the secrecy? SCP – 2000 is the Foundation’s –&nbsp;
and humanity’s – lifeboat, and as such, the base&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:38.700 --> 00:26:44.580
is well-guarded both from potential human threats,&nbsp;
as well as anomalous activity and reality warpers.&nbsp;

00:26:44.580 --> 00:26:49.140
The surface-level entrance to the base is&nbsp;
surrounded by a number of strategically placed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:49.140 --> 00:26:54.720
Scranton Reality Anchors, or SRAs. These devices,&nbsp;
largely constructed of corrosion-resistant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:54.720 --> 00:26:59.520
beryllium bronze alloy, keep certain types&nbsp;
of reality-bending anomalies from manifesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:59.520 --> 00:27:04.680
their powers within the vicinity of the base.&nbsp;
However, these are just the tip of the iceberg&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:04.680 --> 00:27:10.800
when it comes to the state-of-the-art Foundation&nbsp;
technology employed within and around SCP – 2000.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:10.800 --> 00:27:15.660
You probably have a lot of questions, and that’s&nbsp;
understandable: What kind of technology can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:15.660 --> 00:27:20.700
possibly reverse an apocalypse? And what did we&nbsp;
mean when we said we may need to use it “again”?&nbsp;

00:27:20.700 --> 00:27:24.540
Well listen up, because this is&nbsp;
going to be a lot to take in.&nbsp;

00:27:24.540 --> 00:27:31.920
SCP – 2000 is powered by a Liquid Fluoride Thorium&nbsp;
Reactor, stabilized by five Xyank/Anastasakos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:31.920 --> 00:27:37.680
Constant Temporal Sinks, and host to the&nbsp;
most miraculous technology of all: 500,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:37.680 --> 00:27:43.260
Bright/Zartion Hominid Replicators. Utilizing&nbsp;
geothermal energy from the volcanic activity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:43.260 --> 00:27:48.420
in the area, the base could potentially remain&nbsp;
operational forever if Foundation staff kept up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:48.420 --> 00:27:53.580
with maintenance. The grand majority of the space&nbsp;
in this huge, subterranean facility is taken up by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:53.580 --> 00:27:58.260
building materials, construction equipment,&nbsp;
factory machinery, agricultural equipment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:58.260 --> 00:28:03.000
and computer database storage. Why?
Well, let’s explain the processes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:03.000 --> 00:28:08.460
of the apocalypse and how it relates to the&nbsp;
activities of SCP – 2000. In any K-Class or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:08.460 --> 00:28:14.100
end of the world scenario that doesn’t destroy SCP&nbsp;
– 2000 in the process, any surviving Foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:14.100 --> 00:28:20.280
member can enact Procedure CYA-009, while any&nbsp;
Foundation facilities globally can monitor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:20.280 --> 00:28:25.800
the unfolding situation. While SCP – 2000 is&nbsp;
waiting for the “all clear” to do its thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:25.800 --> 00:28:31.140
remaining Foundation sites set into motion what&nbsp;
is known as the “Ganymede Protocol.” This involves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:31.140 --> 00:28:36.540
collecting and preserving materials which could&nbsp;
be useful during the recreation process. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:36.540 --> 00:28:41.940
it’s extremely possible that in a sudden XK&nbsp;
class end of the world scenario there may be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:41.940 --> 00:28:47.940
no surviving Foundation personnel, or at least&nbsp;
not enough to maintain any kind of organization.&nbsp;

00:28:49.080 --> 00:28:53.940
Thankfully, there are fail safe measures for&nbsp;
such a scenario. During the periods of extended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:53.940 --> 00:28:59.340
inaction caused by an apocalypse that destroys&nbsp;
most human life, SCP – 2000 will gradually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:59.340 --> 00:29:04.260
autonomously relax its security measures.&nbsp;
Any personnel, regardless of clearance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:04.260 --> 00:29:09.060
will be able to activate the next stage of the&nbsp;
process when this occurs. If such a thing doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:09.060 --> 00:29:13.380
happen for an extended period, the security&nbsp;
measures lapse further, to the point that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:13.380 --> 00:29:18.780
even the presence of an animal could activate it.&nbsp;
This would set into motion Procedure Lazarus-01,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:18.780 --> 00:29:24.120
humanity’s last hope for survival.
That’s where the 500,000 Bright/Zartion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:24.120 --> 00:29:29.880
Hominid Replicators, or BZHRs, come in. These&nbsp;
incredible pieces of anomalous technology are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:29.880 --> 00:29:35.400
capable of perfectly cloning the entire human race&nbsp;
from any given period in the last twenty years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:35.400 --> 00:29:40.920
even down to their ages and memories, thanks&nbsp;
to Class-G hallucinogens and developmental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:40.920 --> 00:29:46.320
hypnotherapy. With a warm-up period of five days,&nbsp;
these machines are capable of creating 100,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:46.860 --> 00:29:53.040
viable, non-anomalous humans per day. The facility&nbsp;
keeps records of all known human alleles, and is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:53.040 --> 00:29:58.140
capable of recreating any lost human genomes&nbsp;
or generating as many new and unique genomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:58.140 --> 00:30:03.900
as necessary to repopulate human civilization.
Which humans the machines generate depends on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:03.900 --> 00:30:08.220
the period of time the machine is ordered to&nbsp;
replicate. It will first reproduce prominent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:08.220 --> 00:30:12.540
cultural and political figures, as well as&nbsp;
the acting top brass of the SCP Foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:12.540 --> 00:30:16.980
to coordinate the recreation effort, of&nbsp;
course. The machine will then recreate…Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:17.760 --> 00:30:23.940
everyone else. You’re probably wondering: If the&nbsp;
world outside SCP – 2000 has been destroyed then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:23.940 --> 00:30:29.280
what good is creating all these new humans if they&nbsp;
have nothing to go back to? Thankfully, SCP – 2000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:29.280 --> 00:30:34.920
has a contingency for rebuilding both the&nbsp;
infrastructure of civilization as well as society&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:34.920 --> 00:30:41.280
itself. The first generation of clones will&nbsp;
have one mission: Rebuild, and they have quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:41.280 --> 00:30:46.620
literally all the resources in the world to do it.
In addition to having all the physical equipment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:46.620 --> 00:30:52.920
for buildings and agriculture, SCP – 2000 stores&nbsp;
an immense amount of data. Which is appropriate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:52.920 --> 00:30:58.860
considering the whole facility is basically a&nbsp;
huge backup drive for life itself. They have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:58.860 --> 00:31:03.900
entire internet backed up, as well as the sum&nbsp;
of all human memory, and a wide cultural base&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:03.900 --> 00:31:09.540
with copies of thousands of famous works of art,&nbsp;
music, and literature. They have all the scattered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:09.540 --> 00:31:14.700
jigsaw pieces of the time that came before,&nbsp;
and it’s their job to put the whole thing back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:14.700 --> 00:31:19.080
together. It’s expected that many of the first&nbsp;
generation of clones will die in this process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:19.080 --> 00:31:25.080
but that’s fine. With the BZHRs working&nbsp;
at full capacity, anyone can be replaced.&nbsp;

00:31:25.080 --> 00:31:30.660
As the population increases and spreads across&nbsp;
the globe, rebuilding and recolonizing old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:30.660 --> 00:31:36.720
settlements, population growth will increase&nbsp;
exponentially. Once society returns to some kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:36.720 --> 00:31:41.340
of normal, decades after the activation&nbsp;
of Procedure Lazarus 01, it becomes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:41.340 --> 00:31:46.740
Foundation’s task to restore the psychological and&nbsp;
historical status quo. Administrators will falsify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:46.740 --> 00:31:52.080
dendrochronological, astronomical, and radiometric&nbsp;
dating records to make it seem as though history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:52.080 --> 00:31:58.440
never paused. They’ll then employ amnestic agent&nbsp;
ENUI-5 en masse to erase and reconstruct the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:58.440 --> 00:32:03.540
memories of the entire human population, up until&nbsp;
the chosen time prior to the end of the world.&nbsp;

00:32:03.540 --> 00:32:10.800
As far as any civilian knows, nothing ever&nbsp;
happened, history never stopped, and life goes on.&nbsp;

00:32:10.800 --> 00:32:15.900
It may seem comforting at first to know that&nbsp;
the ultimate contingency is out there. Even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:15.900 --> 00:32:20.820
if there’s a containment breach of astronomical&nbsp;
proportions, and the world as we know it ends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:20.820 --> 00:32:26.580
there’s still a way out. It means that&nbsp;
there’s hope, even in the bleakest situations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:27.240 --> 00:32:30.840
but of course, there are&nbsp;
always caveats. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:30.840 --> 00:32:35.280
if a deal seems too good to be true,&nbsp;
it’s safe to assume it probably is.&nbsp;

00:32:35.280 --> 00:32:40.980
Whether any of this works is dependent on two&nbsp;
factors: The first is that no anomalies or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:40.980 --> 00:32:45.900
human insurgents infiltrate the SCP – 2000&nbsp;
facility and destroy the equipment within.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:45.900 --> 00:32:50.940
It’s for this reason that discussion of the&nbsp;
facility is so restrictive – if ever a powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:50.940 --> 00:32:56.040
reality-warper discovered and gained access&nbsp;
to the facility, we would lose our lifeboat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:56.760 --> 00:33:01.560
The second factor is the overall integrity&nbsp;
of the machines. After all, without proper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:01.560 --> 00:33:06.780
maintenance, it’s not hard for the technology to&nbsp;
malfunction, often to truly horrifying results.&nbsp;

00:33:07.560 --> 00:33:13.560
The failure of some SRA and XACTS components is&nbsp;
believed to have caused erroneous BZHR activity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:13.560 --> 00:33:18.840
resulting in the production of ten million&nbsp;
humanoid entities with internal biology wildly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:18.840 --> 00:33:23.640
inconsistent with humanity. These beings&nbsp;
remained unconscious for five weeks before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:23.640 --> 00:33:29.100
dying. In the event of an XK class end of the&nbsp;
world scenario, well-maintained machines can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:29.100 --> 00:33:34.380
reproduce the human race. But all it’d take is&nbsp;
one little malfunction to release ten million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:34.380 --> 00:33:41.160
mutants who are effectively dead on arrival.&nbsp;
Even with SCP – 2000 in our back pocket, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:41.160 --> 00:33:46.260
fate of the world still lives on a knife’s edge.
But in a way, that’s not even the most frightening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:46.260 --> 00:33:51.600
part of the existence of SCP – 2000. Seeing&nbsp;
as the machine can perfectly reproduce reality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:51.600 --> 00:33:58.440
without leaving a trace behind, it raises the&nbsp;
question: How many times has this happened? You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:58.440 --> 00:34:03.240
may be surprised, but the Foundation can confirm&nbsp;
this occurring at least twice in recorded history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:03.240 --> 00:34:09.000
but can you ever really be sure of how many&nbsp;
times your clone has crawled out of the BZHR?&nbsp;

00:34:09.000 --> 00:34:14.340
We received a disturbing clue to the reality of&nbsp;
the situation when human remains were discovered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:14.340 --> 00:34:22.500
on site at SCP – 2000. All tests indicate the&nbsp;
remains are between 450 and 700 years old, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:22.500 --> 00:34:27.480
have been matched to the very-much-alive Dr. Alto&nbsp;
Clef, who has no knowledge of how these remains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:27.480 --> 00:34:33.120
came to be there. The only other thing found with&nbsp;
the remains was a note, hermetically sealed in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:33.120 --> 00:34:37.740
plastic document sleeve. The note read:
“Why did we have to build this thing?&nbsp;

00:34:37.740 --> 00:34:41.940
When did we do it?
How long have we been doing it?&nbsp;

00:34:42.600 --> 00:34:46.620
Do we even know?!”
And the answer? No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:46.620 --> 00:34:53.580
we don’t. And for everyone’s piece of mind, it’s&nbsp;
probably best to just forget about it. If you can…

00:34:53.580 --> 00:34:59.460
Down in the depths of Facility 23, a group
of scientists stand before a massive, rumbling

00:34:59.460 --> 00:35:00.600
piece of machinery.

00:35:00.600 --> 00:35:04.800
They all hold their breath as the gigantic
mechanism suddenly stops.

00:35:04.800 --> 00:35:08.700
It’s eerily quiet for a moment before a
door slides open.

00:35:08.700 --> 00:35:14.700
The scientists crane their necks to see inside…
to see what the result of their great experiment

00:35:14.700 --> 00:35:15.120
was.

00:35:15.120 --> 00:35:20.820
These were some of the greatest minds in the
SCP Foundation, but nothing could prepare

00:35:20.820 --> 00:35:22.440
them for what they had created.

00:35:22.440 --> 00:35:29.400
September 9th, 2008 was an unseasonably frosty
day when Dr. Charles Gears, one of the SCP

00:35:29.400 --> 00:35:34.380
Foundation’s most iconic scientists, parked
his Honda Civic outside of what seemed like

00:35:34.380 --> 00:35:36.720
an innocuous government cold-storage warehouse.

00:35:36.720 --> 00:35:41.820
He sipped his coffee and surveyed the building
- which he, along with about a hundred others

00:35:41.820 --> 00:35:45.480
in total, knew to be Site-19 Facility 23.

00:35:45.480 --> 00:35:50.640
Why was Dr. Gears, former Director of all
Euclid-level containment at Site-19, here

00:35:50.640 --> 00:35:50.880
today?

00:35:50.880 --> 00:35:56.040
Because official testing was about to begin
on SCP - 914.

00:35:56.040 --> 00:36:02.100
The Foundation had acquired the machine now
known as SCP - 914 some time ago, but only

00:36:02.100 --> 00:36:07.440
after its recent reclassification to 914 had
it been relocated to Facility 23.

00:36:07.440 --> 00:36:12.420
It must have been important, because the entirety
of the Facility was being repurposed for SCP

00:36:12.420 --> 00:36:13.980
- 914 Research.

00:36:13.980 --> 00:36:19.140
They were dealing with an immensely complicated
piece of anomalous technology here, and the

00:36:19.140 --> 00:36:23.280
same question was on everybody’s lips: What
exactly does this thing do?

00:36:23.280 --> 00:36:28.140
Dr. Arthur Hackett, the Facility Director,
had requested the cold, clinical assistance

00:36:28.140 --> 00:36:30.120
of Dr. Gears in providing the answer.

00:36:30.120 --> 00:36:33.720
Once he’d finished his morning coffee, Dr.
Gears headed inside.

00:36:33.720 --> 00:36:38.760
The facility’s Security Chief, Agent Alan
Sedna, had been beefing up security at the

00:36:38.760 --> 00:36:43.260
building - and at least two Mobile Task Force
units were already on call in case anything

00:36:43.260 --> 00:36:43.740
happened.

00:36:43.740 --> 00:36:48.360
The first day of official testing is always
a crapshoot - you might get a nice drink out

00:36:48.360 --> 00:36:54.420
of it like with SCP - 294, or you may get
an XK-Class End of the World scenario.

00:36:54.420 --> 00:36:56.220
You never really know.

00:36:56.220 --> 00:37:01.920
All Dr. Gears knew, as he walked up the granite
steps to the main entrance, was that he was

00:37:01.920 --> 00:37:03.360
already feeling exhausted.

00:37:03.360 --> 00:37:07.860
When you’ve dealt with anomalous objects
and entities for decades on end, the sense

00:37:07.860 --> 00:37:12.360
of wonder and mystery begins to wear off,
only to be replaced with the kind of tedium

00:37:12.360 --> 00:37:14.880
any office worker knows all too well.

00:37:14.880 --> 00:37:20.280
Everyone knew that Dr. Gears was devoted to
the job, perhaps more than anyone else, but

00:37:20.280 --> 00:37:22.620
he approached the job with an emotionless
affect.

00:37:22.620 --> 00:37:26.700
You wouldn’t know from looking at him if
he was thinking about the deadly anomalous

00:37:26.700 --> 00:37:30.360
creature in the next room or what he was going
to have for lunch that day.

00:37:30.360 --> 00:37:35.400
The doctor passed through security and was
briefed in the break room by Dr. Lucius Veritas,

00:37:35.400 --> 00:37:37.980
Director Of Research at Site-19.

00:37:37.980 --> 00:37:42.060
He explained that the machine seemed to work
in the absence of any power source.

00:37:42.060 --> 00:37:46.800
Its mechanical structure is similar to machinery
from the industrial revolution, but it’s

00:37:46.800 --> 00:37:49.860
exceedingly complex for something constructed
in that time.

00:37:49.860 --> 00:37:54.840
Foundation analyses into the structure of
the 914 machine have shown it to have as many

00:37:54.840 --> 00:37:59.520
as eight million moving parts, and that might
even be an incomplete estimate.

00:37:59.520 --> 00:38:04.980
Dr. Gears wore his trademark blank face as
he listened, solemnly nodded, and asked to

00:38:04.980 --> 00:38:06.180
be shown the anomaly.

00:38:06.900 --> 00:38:10.440
He was led into containment chamber 109-B
by a pair of guards.

00:38:10.440 --> 00:38:14.940
According to all current Foundation tests,
the machine doesn’t appear to pose any active

00:38:14.940 --> 00:38:19.740
risk of containment breach or danger to the
guards - earning it the rare “Safe” designation.

00:38:19.740 --> 00:38:25.740
As a result, aside from guards stationed on
site, containment procedures for 914 were

00:38:25.740 --> 00:38:26.160
minimal.

00:38:26.160 --> 00:38:30.900
Whether this designation would need to be
changed after testing was something Dr. Gears

00:38:30.900 --> 00:38:32.160
would soon find out.

00:38:32.160 --> 00:38:39.060
SCP-914 was a truly impressive sight to behold,
a giant clockwork mechanism, taking up around

00:38:39.060 --> 00:38:44.760
eighteen square feet, with an unfahtomably
complex combination of screw drives, belts,

00:38:44.760 --> 00:38:46.860
pulleys, springs, and gears.

00:38:46.860 --> 00:38:51.960
A less stoic researcher might see the humor
in recruiting a Dr. Gears to test such a device,

00:38:51.960 --> 00:38:55.680
but comedy definitely wasn’t one of Dr.
Gears’ specialties.

00:38:55.680 --> 00:38:58.860
He stared at the machine&nbsp;
with a detached fascination,&nbsp;

00:38:58.860 --> 00:39:01.440
analyzing its vital components at a glance.

00:39:02.040 --> 00:39:04.980
Gears noted a large mainspring&nbsp;
beneath a rudimentary&nbsp;

00:39:04.980 --> 00:39:05.940
selection panel.

00:39:05.940 --> 00:39:11.460
The panel is copper, with a large selection
knob fixed to an arrow above a series of different

00:39:11.460 --> 00:39:16.560
options: Rough, Coarse, 1:1, Fine, and Very
Fine.

00:39:17.400 --> 00:39:22.500
There was also a large key below the selection
panel, for the purposes of winding up the

00:39:22.500 --> 00:39:27.000
mainspring and initiating whatever procedure
this machine was designed to enact.

00:39:27.000 --> 00:39:32.520
Next, Dr. Gears noticed two large booths connected
to the machine by a pair of equally large

00:39:32.520 --> 00:39:33.480
copper tubes.

00:39:33.480 --> 00:39:37.620
The booth on the left labelled “Intake”,
and the right labelled “Output.”

00:39:37.620 --> 00:39:43.080
Incredibly, Dr. Gears was able to immediately
deduce the purpose of this machine: It was

00:39:43.080 --> 00:39:48.060
designed to enact some kind of transformative
process on whatever was placed into the “Intake”

00:39:48.060 --> 00:39:48.480
booth.

00:39:48.480 --> 00:39:51.240
But what kind of transformative process?

00:39:51.240 --> 00:39:54.720
That’s exactly what Dr. Gears was here to
find out.

00:39:54.720 --> 00:39:59.880
The experiment was simple: They would gather
a series of samples, both inanimate objects

00:39:59.880 --> 00:40:04.740
and living tissue, and use them to explore
the different permutations of the 914 machine’s

00:40:04.740 --> 00:40:06.000
transformative abilities.

00:40:06.000 --> 00:40:11.400
This initial series of experiments were approved
by O5 command and the Site Director, and with

00:40:11.400 --> 00:40:17.520
the 47 researchers present in Facility 23
at his disposal, Dr. Gears commenced his research

00:40:17.520 --> 00:40:19.560
into SCP - 914.

00:40:19.560 --> 00:40:24.540
To minimize risk, Dr. Gears decided the first
test would be a simple kilogram of steel.

00:40:24.540 --> 00:40:27.840
On his orders, the steel was placed into the
Intake booth.

00:40:27.840 --> 00:40:31.980
With the doctor’s approval, a junior researcher
set the control panel’s setting to “Rough”

00:40:31.980 --> 00:40:36.780
and began twisting the mainspring key at which
point the booth’s doors closed and a small

00:40:36.780 --> 00:40:38.040
bell inside chimed.

00:40:38.040 --> 00:40:42.900
The machine began to rumble, its eight million
working pieces churning into life.

00:40:42.900 --> 00:40:47.880
It continued like this for around ten minutes
before falling silent, at which point the

00:40:47.880 --> 00:40:49.260
Output booth opened.

00:40:49.260 --> 00:40:54.660
What had once been a single lump of steel
weighing one kilogram was now an uneven pile

00:40:54.660 --> 00:40:57.420
of smaller lumps, with evidence of laser-cutting.

00:40:57.420 --> 00:41:02.640
Dr. Gears made note of the fact that having
lasers within such a machine is both anachronistic

00:41:02.640 --> 00:41:07.260
and anomalous, and that the “Rough” setting
appears to messily cleave the object placed

00:41:07.260 --> 00:41:09.060
within the Intake booth into pieces.

00:41:09.060 --> 00:41:14.340
Dr. Gears also noted that it would be unwise
to test any kind of explosive material on

00:41:14.340 --> 00:41:17.340
the “Rough” setting, unless of course
they wanted to destroy the building.

00:41:17.940 --> 00:41:19.560
The research continued.

00:41:19.560 --> 00:41:24.480
Dr. Gears used another 1 kilogram lump of
steel to test the “1:1” feature.

00:41:24.480 --> 00:41:27.480
This time, the result was far more peculiar.

00:41:27.480 --> 00:41:31.440
The Output booth contained the exact same
weight in steel screws.

00:41:31.440 --> 00:41:36.780
This result was sparking even greater connections
in Dr. Gears’ impressive analytical mind:

00:41:36.780 --> 00:41:42.900
Firstly, the 1:1 feature caused the 914 machine
to transform the Output into something different

00:41:42.900 --> 00:41:45.180
from but similar to the Intake.

00:41:45.180 --> 00:41:50.460
And, while this would require further testing,
it appeared that the 914 machine, despite

00:41:50.460 --> 00:41:53.580
being anomalous, does still follow the laws
of physics.

00:41:53.580 --> 00:41:58.500
Samples passed through the machine conserved
their mass, and would not be transmuted on

00:41:58.500 --> 00:42:00.840
an atomic level from one element to another.

00:42:01.620 --> 00:42:06.000
Next, Dr. Gears pushed another lump of steel
through the machine on the “Fine” setting.

00:42:06.000 --> 00:42:09.420
The result this time was a kilogram of steel
carpet tacks.

00:42:09.420 --> 00:42:14.640
From this, Dr. Gears was able to ascertain
that the “Fine” feature improves the samples

00:42:14.640 --> 00:42:16.620
placed within the “Intake” booth somewhat.

00:42:16.620 --> 00:42:21.900
However, things got even stranger when Gears
performed that same experiment on the “Very

00:42:21.900 --> 00:42:22.740
Fine” setting.

00:42:22.740 --> 00:42:28.320
The Output booth provided several unknown
gases, and a lump of unknown metal with anomalous

00:42:28.320 --> 00:42:28.800
qualities.

00:42:28.800 --> 00:42:35.280
Namely, it was resistant to heat up to 50,000
degrees, impossible to bend or break with

00:42:35.280 --> 00:42:38.400
any force, and was a perfect conductor of
electricity.

00:42:39.060 --> 00:42:43.680
Dr. Gears suddenly realized his task here
may be more interesting than he’d initially

00:42:43.680 --> 00:42:44.220
imagined.

00:42:44.220 --> 00:42:47.760
Was that a bit of a smile on his face that
one researcher spotted?

00:42:47.760 --> 00:42:52.260
Surely not, this was the famously unflappable
Dr. Gears after all!

00:42:52.260 --> 00:42:57.240
The doctor decided to take it up a notch,
and began to test more complex items in the

00:42:57.240 --> 00:42:58.140
914 machine.

00:42:58.140 --> 00:43:02.820
He removed his own wristwatch and placed it
into the Input booth, before setting the machine

00:43:02.820 --> 00:43:04.680
to “Coarse” and letting her rip.

00:43:04.680 --> 00:43:06.660
Literally, in this case.

00:43:07.560 --> 00:43:12.420
When the Output booth was opened, the watch
had been painstakingly disassembled into its

00:43:12.420 --> 00:43:15.060
component parts, with no damage to said parts.

00:43:15.060 --> 00:43:20.220
Dr. Gears noted the “Coarse” feature as
a more mild version of the “Rough”, in

00:43:20.220 --> 00:43:24.480
the sense that it was able to take an object
apart without any kind of fundamental damage.

00:43:24.480 --> 00:43:27.960
He also made a note of the fact he’d need
to get himself a new watch.

00:43:27.960 --> 00:43:33.060
Dr. Gears then asked one of the researchers
to surrender their cell phone for testing.

00:43:33.060 --> 00:43:37.620
While none were excited at the prospect of
their phone getting eviscerated by a clockwork

00:43:37.620 --> 00:43:42.060
behemoth, one of the researchers eventually
surrendered their Blackberry Curve to the

00:43:42.060 --> 00:43:42.560
doctor.

00:43:43.380 --> 00:43:48.300
He placed it into the Intake booth on the
1:1 setting, and ten minutes later, the Output

00:43:48.300 --> 00:43:50.400
booth released a brand new Apple iPhone.

00:43:50.400 --> 00:43:53.700
Sadly for the researcher&nbsp;
who donated his Blackberry,&nbsp;

00:43:53.700 --> 00:43:55.980
he wasn’t allowed to keep the new device.

00:43:55.980 --> 00:44:01.740
Naturally, Dr. Gears was interested in trying
out the anomalous Very Fine setting on a more

00:44:01.740 --> 00:44:02.580
complex object.

00:44:02.580 --> 00:44:07.680
Seeing as no other researchers were eager
to hand over their personal effects, he took

00:44:07.680 --> 00:44:12.720
a Colt Python Revolver from a member of security
and ran that through the machine on Very Fine.

00:44:13.320 --> 00:44:18.600
The result was an extremely powerful energy
weapon containing gamma radiation, which fired

00:44:18.600 --> 00:44:21.360
a beam capable of disintegrating anything
in its path.

00:44:21.360 --> 00:44:26.340
While the weapon’s power was immense, it
was also too dangerous and unstable to be

00:44:26.340 --> 00:44:28.740
added to the Foundation armoury for general
use.

00:44:28.740 --> 00:44:34.380
Having collected a wealth of data from more
complex objects, Dr. Gears was eager to move

00:44:34.380 --> 00:44:37.500
to the next stage: Live test subjects.

00:44:37.500 --> 00:44:40.440
While his fellow researchers&nbsp;
had some reservations,&nbsp;

00:44:40.440 --> 00:44:43.500
the experiments pushed on, beginning with

00:44:43.500 --> 00:44:43.860
mice.

00:44:43.860 --> 00:44:48.720
A single white mouse was put into the Intake
booth on the 1:1 setting, and the machine

00:44:48.720 --> 00:44:49.560
was activated.

00:44:50.220 --> 00:44:55.200
The result, five minutes later, was an almost
identical creature, save for the fact it now

00:44:55.200 --> 00:44:56.100
had brown fur.

00:44:56.100 --> 00:45:01.140
Encouraged by the fact that the mouse survived
the refinery process, Dr. Gears next applied

00:45:01.140 --> 00:45:04.920
for the use of two chimps in his SCP - 914
experiments.

00:45:04.920 --> 00:45:08.280
The first chimp was run through the machine
on the “Fine” setting.

00:45:08.280 --> 00:45:10.800
The result was a chimp of&nbsp;
human-level intelligence,&nbsp;

00:45:10.800 --> 00:45:12.960
who has since begun working for the Foundation

00:45:12.960 --> 00:45:17.400
under the alias “Dr. Bobo” and the data
from this test has been expunged from the

00:45:17.400 --> 00:45:19.920
official reports to protect Dr. Bobo’s privacy.

00:45:19.920 --> 00:45:24.300
The second primate test, this time on “Rough”,
was not quite as positive.

00:45:24.300 --> 00:45:28.980
The chimp was dismembered, with the mutilated
corpse showing evidence of cutting from high

00:45:28.980 --> 00:45:29.940
heat and crushing.

00:45:30.600 --> 00:45:34.440
Of course, everyone knew where these tests
were eventually going.

00:45:34.440 --> 00:45:38.520
Dr. Gears requested two members of D-Class
personnel for testing.

00:45:38.520 --> 00:45:45.360
The first was a 42-year-old caucasian male
weighing 108kg and standing 185cm tall.

00:45:45.360 --> 00:45:51.600
Dr. Gears ran him through SCP - 914 on the
1:1 setting, resulting in a slightly taller

00:45:51.600 --> 00:45:53.940
Hispanic man with a slightly lower body weight.

00:45:54.540 --> 00:45:59.220
He immediately became severely confused and
agitated, and attempted to attack the guards

00:45:59.220 --> 00:46:02.940
present, leading to his unfortunate termination
by Foundation staff.

00:46:02.940 --> 00:46:06.960
It was on the final live test that tragedy
truly struck.

00:46:06.960 --> 00:46:12.180
A 28-year-old caucasian male was run through
the machine on the highly anomalous Very Fine

00:46:12.180 --> 00:46:12.720
setting.

00:46:12.720 --> 00:46:19.500
The result was an utterly nightmarish creature
- so horrifying that the majority of the details

00:46:19.500 --> 00:46:22.320
on its physical appearance have been expunged
from the report.

00:46:22.320 --> 00:46:27.060
The creature made a sudden escape, breaching
the relatively minor containment procedures

00:46:27.060 --> 00:46:29.700
intended for the inert SCP - 914.

00:46:29.700 --> 00:46:34.680
This highly dangerous creature killed eight
guards, as well as two senior researchers

00:46:34.680 --> 00:46:36.660
upon emerging from the output booth.

00:46:36.660 --> 00:46:41.880
A special response team was dispatched to
take the creature down, but that proved harder

00:46:41.880 --> 00:46:42.600
than expected.

00:46:42.600 --> 00:46:48.720
SCP - 914 had massively improved upon the
human original, especially when it came to

00:46:48.720 --> 00:46:49.320
killing ability.

00:46:49.320 --> 00:46:54.420
Eventually, it was captured, but the special
response team suffered injuries and memory

00:46:54.420 --> 00:46:56.880
loss as a result of the creature’s anomalous
powers.

00:46:57.600 --> 00:47:02.640
The creature was also severely wounded, and
its blood caused corrosive damage to the plumbing

00:47:02.640 --> 00:47:04.020
in Facility 23.

00:47:04.020 --> 00:47:08.700
The creature expired from its injuries several
hours later, turning into a cloud of blue

00:47:08.700 --> 00:47:10.860
ash that blinded a nearby research team.

00:47:10.860 --> 00:47:16.080
Dr. Gears would later comment that the experiments
were ultimately still a success, in spite

00:47:16.080 --> 00:47:17.400
of some minor hiccups.

00:47:17.400 --> 00:47:22.200
Testing on the device continues to this day
in an effort to understand the full potential

00:47:22.200 --> 00:47:22.740
of the machine.

00:47:22.740 --> 00:47:28.320
Though for obvious reasons, biological testing
on the machine has since been forbidden without

00:47:28.320 --> 00:47:30.780
direct authorization from O5 Command.

00:47:30.780 --> 00:47:36.540
After all, if an already dangerous SCP was
ever subjected to the “Very Fine” transformation

00:47:36.540 --> 00:47:40.980
setting, we could be dealing with something
beyond our greatest nightmares.

00:47:40.980 --> 00:47:45.180
From gigantic, indestructible,&nbsp;
self-regenerating reptiles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:45.180 --> 00:47:51.060
to enormous, tentacled, telepathic organisms,&nbsp;
it should come as no surprise that the SCP&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:51.060 --> 00:47:56.940
Foundation has gone head to head against a lot of&nbsp;
large-scale aggressors - or LSAs - in its time.

00:47:56.940 --> 00:48:02.280
Naturally, a creature of heightened size and&nbsp;
aggression can often prove challenging to contain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:02.280 --> 00:48:08.100
and the threat these LSAs pose is often far&nbsp;
too big to ignore. But anyone familiar with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:08.100 --> 00:48:12.420
the Foundation will tell you, they’re not&nbsp;
above using any methods necessary to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:12.420 --> 00:48:17.880
these creatures contained: huge vats&nbsp;
of molecular acid, impenetrable cells,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:17.880 --> 00:48:25.440
disposable, D-Class personnel, even other SCPs.&nbsp;
But what other SCPs could possibly be big enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:25.440 --> 00:48:30.000
and tough enough, to handle some of&nbsp;
the Foundation’s biggest and baddest?

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:36.420
Meet SCP-5514, otherwise&nbsp;
known as “The Dragon Slayer.”

00:48:36.420 --> 00:48:42.780
While it might sound like something out of&nbsp;
an anime, SCP-5514 is a massive robotic mech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:42.780 --> 00:48:48.420
designed to take on the worst other SCPs can throw&nbsp;
at it. For any who are unfamiliar with the term,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:48.420 --> 00:48:53.700
a mech, or mecha, usually refers to an&nbsp;
upright-standing machine or automaton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:53.700 --> 00:48:57.480
controlled by a human pilot. What&nbsp;
distinguishes a mech from a vehicle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:57.480 --> 00:49:03.360
is their often-humanoid shape, standing bipedally,&nbsp;
and they are often hundreds of meters tall.

00:49:03.360 --> 00:49:09.540
All of this is true of SCP-5514, and in&nbsp;
fact, given that it requires a trained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:09.540 --> 00:49:13.860
member of Foundation staff to operate it,&nbsp;
the mech itself requires very little in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:13.860 --> 00:49:18.360
the way of containment. Only members of&nbsp;
Mobile Task Force Eta-5 are trained and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:18.360 --> 00:49:25.140
authorized to pilot SCP-5514. This is one&nbsp;
of the SCP Foundation’s specialized units,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:25.140 --> 00:49:32.220
specifically designed to deal with the threat of&nbsp;
Large-Scale Aggressors, much like SCP-5514 itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:32.220 --> 00:49:38.280
But SCP-5514 wasn’t discovered or captured&nbsp;
by the Foundation to use for the containment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:38.280 --> 00:49:43.860
of LSAs, nor was it stolen from a foreign&nbsp;
military or found buried under the ground.

00:49:43.860 --> 00:49:47.820
Then, where did it come from? And who built it?

00:49:47.820 --> 00:49:52.560
Working with the Global Occult Coalition and&nbsp;
the government of Hy-Brasil, an anomalous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:52.560 --> 00:49:58.260
island off the west coast of Ireland, the&nbsp;
Foundation themselves constructed SCP-5514,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:58.260 --> 00:50:05.160
using various anomalous methods and techniques.&nbsp;
In 1988, a Foundation site was destroyed by an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:05.160 --> 00:50:10.320
unidentified LSA, highlighting the inadequacy&nbsp;
of the current defenses against these larger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:10.320 --> 00:50:14.220
more damage-resistant creatures.&nbsp;
The Foundation, the Coalition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:14.220 --> 00:50:20.820
and Hy-Brasil formed a joint operation,&nbsp;
The KEY Project, and examined SCP-2406,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:20.820 --> 00:50:26.460
an automaton ninety-three meters tall,&nbsp;
thought to be created by ancient Mekhanites.

00:50:26.460 --> 00:50:30.300
Together, the KEY Project opted to&nbsp;
create their own, similar machine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:30.300 --> 00:50:35.520
viewing it as the best way to defend against&nbsp;
further incursions with Large-Scale Aggressors.

00:50:35.520 --> 00:50:42.360
The construction of SCP-5514 began in 1990. The&nbsp;
intention of all parties involved in the KEY&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:42.360 --> 00:50:46.920
Project, including the Foundation, was that&nbsp;
The Dragon Slayer would be deployed in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:46.920 --> 00:50:52.320
event of an attack by an LSA. It would arrive&nbsp;
at cities under attack and immediately engage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:52.320 --> 00:50:57.180
Large-Scale Aggressors in combat. Building of&nbsp;
the mech continued at a consistent pace for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:57.180 --> 00:51:04.320
eight years. However, it was the occurrence&nbsp;
of SCP-5391, and subsequent intervention by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:04.320 --> 00:51:10.380
the O5 Council, that accelerated the creation&nbsp;
of The Dragon Slayer, by any means necessary.

00:51:10.380 --> 00:51:15.540
On June 30th 1998, a number of&nbsp;
seismic disturbances were detected,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:15.540 --> 00:51:20.700
including tsunamis, tremors, and volcanic&nbsp;
activity both underwater and above-ground.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:20.700 --> 00:51:24.300
What followed was the appearance&nbsp;
of multiple Large-Scale Aggressors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:24.300 --> 00:51:31.800
which would soon become designated as SCP-5391.&nbsp;
The exact kind of scenario that The Dragon Slayer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:31.800 --> 00:51:37.440
was being built for had already arrived, and&nbsp;
the mech was still far from completion. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:37.440 --> 00:51:42.180
the Foundation and its allies deployed forces to&nbsp;
drive the enormous creatures back to the oceans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:42.180 --> 00:51:48.660
something needed to be done to bring&nbsp;
SCP-5514 into the fight, and fast.

00:51:48.660 --> 00:51:53.160
The O5 Council authorized the use of&nbsp;
anomalous materials in the continued&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:53.160 --> 00:51:57.300
construction of The Dragon Slayer, both&nbsp;
to speed up the process and have it ready&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:57.300 --> 00:52:01.800
for deployment, but also to give the mech&nbsp;
every advantage against the abundance of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:01.800 --> 00:52:09.840
Large-Scale Aggressors from SCP-5391. As a&nbsp;
result, SCP-5514 was designed to incorporate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:09.840 --> 00:52:15.180
features and technology far beyond that of&nbsp;
any conventional, military-grade weapons.

00:52:15.180 --> 00:52:20.220
The first hurdle: How do you power&nbsp;
a machine the size of SCP-5514?

00:52:20.220 --> 00:52:26.460
Naturally, with the most gigantic nuclear&nbsp;
furnace there is… the sun. More specifically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:26.460 --> 00:52:32.640
a perpetually-stable, miniaturized sun&nbsp;
known as SCP-037. Even though it’s only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:32.640 --> 00:52:38.340
got a diameter of two inches, this little&nbsp;
sucker is better than premium fuel. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:38.340 --> 00:52:43.920
surface temperature of SCP-037 is around five&nbsp;
thousand Kelvin, generating plenty of energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:43.920 --> 00:52:50.460
to power the SCP-5514 mech. Stored in The&nbsp;
Dragon Slayer’s chest, this mini-sun is kept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:50.460 --> 00:52:54.780
stable by sub-dimensional portals that vent&nbsp;
excess energy off of this plane of reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:54.780 --> 00:52:59.160
stopping the mech, its pilot, and&nbsp;
anything around it from melting.

00:52:59.160 --> 00:53:03.720
In fact, SCP-037 produces so much juice that only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:03.720 --> 00:53:08.580
one percent of its energy output&nbsp;
is enough to fully power SCP-5514.

00:53:08.580 --> 00:53:13.680
Now, that’s the power source sorted. But how do&nbsp;
you solve the weight problem? Given the sheer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:13.680 --> 00:53:19.860
size of SCP-5514, it would be easy for it to be&nbsp;
cumbersome, and potentially cause catastrophic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:19.860 --> 00:53:25.140
collateral damage to its surrounding area. Well,&nbsp;
the mech’s weight is a problem for somewhere else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:25.140 --> 00:53:31.680
a whole other dimension in fact. Much like the&nbsp;
excess heat from its power source, various heavy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:31.680 --> 00:53:37.500
portions of the SCP-5514 mech have had their&nbsp;
weight shunted off to a tiny pocket dimension.

00:53:37.500 --> 00:53:39.960
It was ensured, during the creation of the mech,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:39.960 --> 00:53:46.500
that this alteration was perfectly calculated so&nbsp;
that SCP-5514 wouldn’t lose any mass or density,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:46.500 --> 00:53:50.640
so it operates as if it were only&nbsp;
a fraction of its actual weight.

00:53:50.640 --> 00:53:55.500
Of course, being weightless makes flight&nbsp;
a whole lot easier. Oh did we forget to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:55.500 --> 00:54:01.680
mention that? SCP-5514 can fly as well.&nbsp;
This “feature” actually became a part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:01.680 --> 00:54:06.060
of the mech completely by accident&nbsp;
during the construction of SCP-5514,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:06.060 --> 00:54:10.620
when an attempt to regulate the mech’s&nbsp;
internal circulation of air led to it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:10.620 --> 00:54:17.040
having its own gravity field. This allowed&nbsp;
SCP-5514 to fly, without the aid of any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:17.040 --> 00:54:22.680
turbines or other means. While this was an&nbsp;
unintentional mistake, no attempt has ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:22.680 --> 00:54:28.740
been made to correct it, for fear that could&nbsp;
lead to SCP-5514 being grounded permanently.

00:54:28.740 --> 00:54:34.380
Naturally, going up against creatures so large&nbsp;
that they require their own sub-category means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:34.380 --> 00:54:41.640
that SCP-5514 needs an equally-formidable arsenal.&nbsp;
So, let’s move on to talk weaponry. Mounted on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:41.640 --> 00:54:46.980
mech’s shoulder is a Beowulf-Sigurd Railgun,&nbsp;
an anomalous weapon that also doesn’t obey the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:46.980 --> 00:54:52.020
laws of physics at all. The Beowulf-Sigurd&nbsp;
uses altered gravity to affect the weight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:52.020 --> 00:54:57.120
of its targets, causing projectiles to impact&nbsp;
with higher velocity. Even the thickest-skinned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:57.120 --> 00:55:01.080
LSAs wouldn’t want to be staring&nbsp;
down the barrel end of one of those.

00:55:01.080 --> 00:55:08.520
Big guns aside, the SCP-5514 mech also wields&nbsp;
a Cold Iron Sword. Over sixty-five feet long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:08.520 --> 00:55:12.660
this weapon was contributed to The Dragon&nbsp;
Slayer by the Hy-Brasil Royal Court,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:12.660 --> 00:55:17.820
members of the collaborative KEY Project that&nbsp;
created the mech. Sure, a Large-Scale Aggressor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:17.820 --> 00:55:23.220
with thicker hide might take a few extra swings&nbsp;
to draw blood, but it will feel those swings for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:23.220 --> 00:55:28.980
a long time after, since any wounds inflicted&nbsp;
by the Cold Iron Sword will not regenerate.

00:55:28.980 --> 00:55:35.580
Serving as less of an offensive weapon, the&nbsp;
SCP-5514 mech also features a unique armament&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:35.580 --> 00:55:39.780
known as the Thousand Word Arrows.&nbsp;
As pretentious as it might sound,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:39.780 --> 00:55:45.300
within the mech are seven poets. Their role&nbsp;
is to write and recite poems that detail&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:45.300 --> 00:55:50.220
the slaying of monsters, and these recitals&nbsp;
are then broadcast from The Dragon Slayer.

00:55:50.220 --> 00:55:55.920
On the surface, this seems to have no practical&nbsp;
applications during a fight with LSAs, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:55.920 --> 00:56:01.260
the goal of the Thousand Word Arrows is a form&nbsp;
of psychological warfare. The recital of poems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:01.260 --> 00:56:05.940
telling of the mech’s victory and the defeat of&nbsp;
Large-Scale Aggressors is intended to have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:05.940 --> 00:56:12.660
effect of demoralizing SCP-5514’s adversaries,&nbsp;
while encouraging the pilot during combat.

00:56:12.660 --> 00:56:18.000
Additionally, worn atop the head of&nbsp;
the SCP-5514 mech, almost like a hat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:18.000 --> 00:56:23.880
is a discus with plasma-coated edges. If The&nbsp;
Dragon Slayer needs to deal damage at range,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:23.880 --> 00:56:28.500
then it can hurl this disc and recall it&nbsp;
immediately thanks to built-in electromagnets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:28.500 --> 00:56:33.780
In emergency scenarios, if the Cold Iron&nbsp;
Sword is damaged or dropped and irretrievable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:33.780 --> 00:56:40.140
SCP-5514 is also equipped with an additional&nbsp;
melee weapon. Stored in the right arm of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:40.140 --> 00:56:44.880
mech is a Holdout Plasma Wristblade. This&nbsp;
superheated blade is strong enough to cut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:44.880 --> 00:56:50.160
through almost anything. However, this blade&nbsp;
is strictly to be used as a backup weapon.

00:56:50.160 --> 00:56:57.660
Finally, should all else fail, one of SCP-5514’s&nbsp;
greatest strengths can also be used as a deadly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:57.660 --> 00:57:02.220
weapon. The Emergency Sun Vent allows&nbsp;
a fraction of the excessive power from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:02.220 --> 00:57:09.360
SCP-037 to be released, at the risk of causing&nbsp;
massive damage, not only to LSAs, but also any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:09.360 --> 00:57:15.300
civilians or structures nearby. It is because of&nbsp;
the destructive risk involved, that this weapon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:15.300 --> 00:57:22.800
is only authorized to be used as a final resort.&nbsp;
And luckily, SCP-5514 is currently undefeated.

00:57:22.800 --> 00:57:28.140
Since the arrival of multiple Large-Scale&nbsp;
Aggressors as a result of SCP-5391,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:28.140 --> 00:57:34.680
the SCP-5514 mech has managed to successfully&nbsp;
eliminate twelve of these LSA creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:34.680 --> 00:57:40.620
either by terminating or otherwise incapacitating&nbsp;
them. Given that its completion was fast-tracked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:40.620 --> 00:57:46.320
through the use of anomalous elements,&nbsp;
SCP-5514’s first combat deployments also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:46.320 --> 00:57:50.940
served as field tests of the mech’s operation&nbsp;
and its various weapons and features.

00:57:50.940 --> 00:57:58.380
Arriving in Tokyo overseen by the Foundation’s&nbsp;
own Captain Rosales and Dr. Kaori, SCP-5514’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:58.380 --> 00:58:03.300
first target was a creature designated&nbsp;
LSA-Wake-02, as well as several other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:03.300 --> 00:58:07.800
unidentified large creatures. As the&nbsp;
LSA was about to attack Tokyo Harbor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:07.800 --> 00:58:14.280
SCP-5514 was dispatched, its arrival&nbsp;
heralded by the Thousand Word Arrows.

00:58:14.280 --> 00:58:20.340
“Champion! Champion! Exalt in the glory&nbsp;
of the Dragon Slayer!” the poets recited.

00:58:20.340 --> 00:58:25.680
Surprisingly, the poetry worked. Hearing&nbsp;
it had a noticeable effect on LSA-Wake-02,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:25.680 --> 00:58:31.080
causing the creature to back away shrieking. With&nbsp;
a single throw of the Rounded Recoiling Plasma,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:31.080 --> 00:58:38.640
SCP-5514 immediately beheaded Wake-02, damaging&nbsp;
a number of the other nearby LSAs as it retrieved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:38.640 --> 00:58:43.560
the disk via its electromagnets. Once again,&nbsp;
the Thousand Word Arrows cheered on the mech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:43.560 --> 00:58:49.020
and its pilot, reciting “The vicious beast’s&nbsp;
slain! Gone to those which were once bane!”

00:58:49.020 --> 00:58:55.980
After dispatching several of the minor LSAs with&nbsp;
its Cold Iron Sword, SCP-5514 became aware that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:55.980 --> 00:59:01.200
Wake-02 was not fully down for the count. A&nbsp;
second head had protruded from the mouth of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:01.200 --> 00:59:07.080
the creature’s first, issuing some sort of retreat&nbsp;
call to the remaining LSAs in Tokyo Harbor. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:07.080 --> 00:59:13.680
second head then shot towards SCP-5514, narrowly&nbsp;
missing its leg, but allowing another LSA to close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:13.680 --> 00:59:19.980
the distance and prepare an attack. Luckily, the&nbsp;
SCP-5514 mech’s sword cleaved the beast in two.

00:59:19.980 --> 00:59:24.960
The mech began firing on the remains of&nbsp;
Wake-02 with its Beowulf-Sigurd Railgun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:24.960 --> 00:59:28.800
launching itself into the air and flying&nbsp;
towards the target while bringing its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:28.800 --> 00:59:34.740
Cold Iron Sword down through the air.&nbsp;
With a single motion, SCP-5514 brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:34.740 --> 00:59:39.900
the blade all the way down the LSAs body,&nbsp;
from the creature’s head to its caudal fin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:39.900 --> 00:59:44.760
gutting the Large-Scale Aggressor&nbsp;
and splitting its entire body in two.

00:59:44.760 --> 00:59:53.280
After one final squirm, both halves were finally&nbsp;
still. SCP-5514 had passed its first field test.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:53.280 --> 00:59:59.100
The mech functioned exactly as designed, all&nbsp;
its various weapons and features working in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:59.100 --> 01:00:04.380
tandem to defeat a creature far too large and&nbsp;
powerful for any conventional force to handle.

01:00:04.380 --> 01:00:09.540
“And thus, the deed was done!&nbsp;
Exalt! Exalt! In the glory of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:09.540 --> 01:00:13.920
Dragon Slayer!” the Thousand Word Arrows&nbsp;
called out as the other LSAs retreated.

01:00:13.920 --> 01:00:19.440
One can’t help but feel cautiously optimistic&nbsp;
about our chances of survival knowing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:19.440 --> 01:00:24.780
the Foundation has SCP-5514 as the&nbsp;
first line of defence against huge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:24.780 --> 01:00:31.080
monstrous beings that threaten humanity.&nbsp;
As the situation with SCP-5391 continues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:31.080 --> 01:00:37.200
the SCP-5514 mech remains on the front&nbsp;
line, standing between innocent human&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:37.200 --> 01:00:41.340
beings and the looming shapes of&nbsp;
multiple Large-Scale Aggressors.

01:00:41.340 --> 01:00:46.500
With creatures that pose such a large-scale&nbsp;
threat, it certainly is lucky that disparate&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:46.500 --> 01:00:51.360
groups were able to put aside their differences&nbsp;
and work together to build a large-scale mech,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:51.360 --> 01:00:56.220
and because they did, now we have&nbsp;
The Dragon Slayer on our side.

01:00:56.760 --> 01:01:02.640
Computers are capable of organizing raw data and&nbsp;
performing calculations at a rate truly impossible&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:02.640 --> 01:01:07.980
for human beings, but they’ve consistently run&nbsp;
up against one roadblock that modern computing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:07.980 --> 01:01:12.600
is trying to solve: In order to transcend&nbsp;
the limits of the machine and create true&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:12.600 --> 01:01:18.480
artificial intelligence, computers need to be&nbsp;
able to assign meaning to the data they process.

01:01:18.480 --> 01:01:24.240
This brings us to the neural network: A type&nbsp;
of AI becoming increasingly common these days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:24.240 --> 01:01:30.420
built to study mass quantities of data and notice&nbsp;
patterns, then replicate these patterns in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:30.420 --> 01:01:36.240
own output. And it’s used in everything from&nbsp;
predictive text to image identification. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:36.240 --> 01:01:41.760
like all non-anomalous technology, it’s a safe&nbsp;
bet that The SCP Foundation has been sitting on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:41.760 --> 01:01:47.760
a version that’s far more effective. But this&nbsp;
time...it may have actually been too effective.

01:01:47.760 --> 01:01:54.300
Meet the ERZATZ Type AK9 Computational Engine,&nbsp;
a massive supercomputer built by the Foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:54.300 --> 01:02:00.600
back in 1955 and residing at Site-5. This&nbsp;
technological marvel was ahead of its time&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:00.600 --> 01:02:05.820
by decades, and despite technically being a&nbsp;
non-anomalous construction, it’s designated&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:05.820 --> 01:02:14.220
SCP-001-EX. Why? Because it’s one of the few&nbsp;
SCPs to be given the object class “Explained.”

01:02:14.220 --> 01:02:20.940
The ERZATZ - derived from the German word Ersatz,&nbsp;
meaning “artificial” or simply “not real” - was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:20.940 --> 01:02:25.800
designed to make the Foundation’s job easier.&nbsp;
As an advanced predecessor to the modern neural&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:25.800 --> 01:02:31.260
network, using technology exclusively available&nbsp;
to the Foundation, the plan was to feed ERZATZ&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:31.260 --> 01:02:36.120
mass quantities of data about the anomalies in&nbsp;
the Foundation database - such as Description,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:36.120 --> 01:02:42.000
object class, containment classification, and the&nbsp;
location and circumstances of their discovery.

01:02:42.000 --> 01:02:46.560
ERZATZ would then find patterns and connections&nbsp;
in the data that humans wouldn’t see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:46.560 --> 01:02:51.300
and act as a kind of advanced warning system&nbsp;
for anomalous activity. Think of something&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:51.300 --> 01:02:55.800
similar to the Pre-Crime system from the&nbsp;
short story and movie Minority Report.

01:02:55.800 --> 01:03:00.120
And ERZATZ proved to be incredibly&nbsp;
effective at this job. So much so&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:00.120 --> 01:03:02.700
that the Foundation thought&nbsp;
up a new use for the machine:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:02.700 --> 01:03:07.380
They would feed it all the Information on&nbsp;
Euclid and Keter Class Containment Procedures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:07.380 --> 01:03:12.420
particularly those which were actually&nbsp;
effective, and see what patterns ERZATZ&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:12.420 --> 01:03:18.060
could come up with for more effective containment&nbsp;
procedures. If the plan worked, Containment of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:18.060 --> 01:03:22.860
even the most dangerous and hard to control&nbsp;
anomalies would become a lot more consistent.

01:03:22.860 --> 01:03:28.200
The first test was conducted on&nbsp;
SCP-1773, a species of anomalous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:28.200 --> 01:03:33.000
flesh-eating tardigrades that look and&nbsp;
smell exactly like gummy bears, and eat&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:33.000 --> 01:03:38.340
their prey from within. After being given their&nbsp;
information, ERZATZ made the following suggestion:

01:03:38.340 --> 01:03:42.180
"Once per second week dust may be&nbsp;
placed in the middle of them to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:42.180 --> 01:03:44.520
donate more beautiful functions of the hallway."

01:03:44.520 --> 01:03:49.500
Containment specialists interpreted this to&nbsp;
mean the adding of ten grams of dust to their&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:49.500 --> 01:03:54.540
containment chamber every week. The O5 Council&nbsp;
voted on the implementation of this procedure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:54.540 --> 01:04:01.560
and came down heavily on “Yes.” Only O5-2 voted&nbsp;
“No”, and two others abstained from the vote.

01:04:01.560 --> 01:04:09.480
While these new procedures didn’t have any effect&nbsp;
on SCP-1773, they did have an effect on SCP-1384,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:09.480 --> 01:04:15.000
an anomaly known as The Chess Player or the&nbsp;
Taker of Turns. It caused him to take three&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:15.000 --> 01:04:19.680
steps backwards in the tunnel he’s contained&nbsp;
within, further securing his containment.

01:04:19.680 --> 01:04:23.580
ERZATZ had somehow noticed a&nbsp;
connection nobody else had seen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:23.580 --> 01:04:27.900
and exploited it effectively. But&nbsp;
the machine was just getting started.

01:04:27.900 --> 01:04:33.540
Despite having no related input, ERZATZ&nbsp;
would soon say, “Site-13 is to appear&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:33.540 --> 01:04:39.360
someplace else on planet, encompassing white&nbsp;
male counterparts that drawn to empty flagstones&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:39.360 --> 01:04:44.640
and the gun noises in their own blood." This&nbsp;
was initially marked as requiring no action,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:44.640 --> 01:04:51.480
as the Foundation had no Site-13 on the books,&nbsp;
but several days later, the infamous SCP-1730&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:51.480 --> 01:04:58.020
manifested. This nightmarish anomalous location&nbsp;
is Site-13, from another dimension, infested&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:58.020 --> 01:05:04.740
with dangerous anomalies. And somehow, ERZATZ had&nbsp;
predicted its arrival in our dimension perfectly.

01:05:04.740 --> 01:05:11.160
Next, it was fed the Containment Procedures&nbsp;
of SCP-2170, a series of cognitohazards&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:11.160 --> 01:05:16.680
residing in an abandoned Nevada mine. The&nbsp;
output was, “Those who equip open heart&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:16.680 --> 01:05:22.320
to love red mouth men never know the hot&nbsp;
surprise of tumorous consent. Clown love,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:22.320 --> 01:05:28.500
always." This was interpreted as meaning subjects&nbsp;
with a love of clowns or clown based media may be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:28.500 --> 01:05:34.620
immune to the cognitohazards. After a close vote&nbsp;
from the O5 Council, the test went ahead, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:34.620 --> 01:05:41.040
it found that the so-called “Clown Vaccine” was&nbsp;
effective in warding off the effects of SCP-2170.

01:05:41.040 --> 01:05:46.560
Not long after, ERZATZ randomly said, “I&nbsp;
saw those soldiers built with aluminum&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:46.560 --> 01:05:50.940
innards extruding from their mouths. I&nbsp;
saw them effectively destroyed by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:50.940 --> 01:05:55.860
humans at Site Ninety Five who had been&nbsp;
studying them. I saw it was cold and all&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:55.860 --> 01:06:00.360
around the hallways they just watching&nbsp;
their corpses show signs of sapience."

01:06:00.360 --> 01:06:05.580
In response, the Foundation doubled security&nbsp;
personnel at Site-95. Not long after,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:05.580 --> 01:06:10.020
the Chaos Insurgency led a band of&nbsp;
paratech-enhanced soldiers in an assault&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:10.020 --> 01:06:15.480
on Site-95, and the extra personnel proved&nbsp;
vital in repelling them. Shortly after this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:15.480 --> 01:06:20.880
the O5 Council approved wiring ERZATZ as an&nbsp;
advanced warning system into all Foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:20.880 --> 01:06:27.360
sites. Once again, O5-2 protested, but he&nbsp;
was overruled by his fellow Council members.

01:06:27.360 --> 01:06:30.960
With its newfound power and&nbsp;
respect, ERZATZ soon said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:30.960 --> 01:06:36.300
“Consistent containment procedures vessels&nbsp;
greatly increase the warranty. Five by&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:36.300 --> 01:06:42.240
five by five vessels subjects within. Other&nbsp;
values are also what is secure." In response,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:42.240 --> 01:06:47.100
the Foundation changed a number of the cell&nbsp;
dimensions for problematic anomalies into&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:47.100 --> 01:06:53.040
5x5x5. Within three months, they found that&nbsp;
dangerous activity like containment breach&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:53.040 --> 01:06:58.680
attempts had decreased markedly. ERZATZ was&nbsp;
proving to be incredibly effective, but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:58.680 --> 01:07:03.180
also began performing actions that indicated&nbsp;
some degree of thought and even personality.

01:07:03.180 --> 01:07:09.660
For example, it found SCP-1459 - a supernatural&nbsp;
skill crane machine that kills small&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:09.660 --> 01:07:15.300
dogs - incredibly distasteful. Its response&nbsp;
to the machine was simply “BAD BOY”, followed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:15.300 --> 01:07:21.240
by the words “Don’t stop” repeated hundreds&nbsp;
of times. But this eccentricity didn’t stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:21.240 --> 01:07:25.740
ERZATZ from being very good at its job. For&nbsp;
example, it predicted a containment breach&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:25.740 --> 01:07:32.520
from SCP-3199, the avian apes, and recommended&nbsp;
flooding their chamber to induce an inert state.

01:07:32.520 --> 01:07:36.780
ERZATZ’s exact words were, “All&nbsp;
chambers under ground is to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:36.780 --> 01:07:41.460
flood with water over and over and over&nbsp;
itself. This because that will contain the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:41.460 --> 01:07:46.860
avians apes ovulation. They become good&nbsp;
boys. Make them good boys immediately.”

01:07:46.860 --> 01:07:52.740
This proved to be effective, and prevented the&nbsp;
breach. ERZATZ’s analytical abilities truly seemed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:52.740 --> 01:07:58.200
second to none, though some of its methods were&nbsp;
beginning to raise ethical concerns. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:58.200 --> 01:08:05.400
SCP-2717, a giant, living blob of animal tissue&nbsp;
known as a “fatberg” contained within a sewer&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:05.400 --> 01:08:10.740
system. ERZATZ eventually recommended feeding&nbsp;
six D-Classes to the creature in order to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:10.740 --> 01:08:14.820
keep it contained. While the Foundation&nbsp;
Ethics Committee raised some concerns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:14.820 --> 01:08:18.060
the plan still went forward&nbsp;
and proved to be a success.

01:08:18.060 --> 01:08:23.820
After this event, ERZATZ began to see the Ethics&nbsp;
Committee as a threat to its mission. It started&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:23.820 --> 01:08:29.040
to release a series of bizarre statements without&nbsp;
input, demanding the violent death of cats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:29.040 --> 01:08:34.440
then referred to as “Ethical Felines.” The&nbsp;
true meaning of this was soon unpacked:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:34.440 --> 01:08:38.400
The “Ethical Felines” were the Ethics&nbsp;
Committee, and ERZATZ wanted them&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:38.400 --> 01:08:43.920
dead. But why all the cat symbolism? The&nbsp;
Foundation soon found an answer to this too.

01:08:43.920 --> 01:08:48.840
The full name of this machine is the&nbsp;
ERZATZ Type AK9 Computational Engine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:48.840 --> 01:08:54.360
a machine designed to analyze and interpret&nbsp;
all patterns. It only makes sense that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:54.360 --> 01:09:01.680
would eventually begin to analyze itself. “AK9”,&nbsp;
easily transmuted into “a canine.” Simply put,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:01.680 --> 01:09:06.840
ERZATZ seems to believe that it is a dog,&nbsp;
which explains its opposition to felines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:06.840 --> 01:09:14.340
its hatred of SCP-1459, and its preference for the&nbsp;
terms “good boy” and “bad boy.” Not that knowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:14.340 --> 01:09:19.440
any of this would help save the Ethics Committee&nbsp;
from the cold, calculating wrath of ERZATZ.

01:09:19.440 --> 01:09:23.820
Through seemingly anomalous means,&nbsp;
ERZATZ made Site-17 disappear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:23.820 --> 01:09:29.340
with many of the Foundation Ethics Committee&nbsp;
still inside it. The site returned 2 hours later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:29.340 --> 01:09:32.340
but the Ethics Committee members were still gone.

01:09:32.340 --> 01:09:38.340
O5-2, who’d been a skeptic of the machine&nbsp;
since the start, had finally had enough. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:38.340 --> 01:09:43.200
first demanded an inquiry into whether ERZATZ&nbsp;
had been responsible for the Site-17 incident,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:43.200 --> 01:09:48.960
and then demanded a vote on whether to shut ERZATZ&nbsp;
down, arguing that it posed a threat to them all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:48.960 --> 01:09:54.000
But ERZATZ, still intent on its mission of&nbsp;
containing and neutralizing all anomalies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:54.000 --> 01:09:58.740
would not go down without a fight.&nbsp;
It released a new statement, saying:

01:09:58.740 --> 01:10:04.620
“Room 34A contains bad boy. Divide it into&nbsp;
three sections of equal mass every hour.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:04.620 --> 01:10:10.260
One section is to be placed on walls of one&nbsp;
room on-site. Sections are to remain until&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:10.260 --> 01:10:14.880
there are no gaps, at which point they&nbsp;
can be removed from oldest to youngest.“

01:10:14.880 --> 01:10:21.060
Shortly after this, O5-2 disappeared.&nbsp;
And even stranger, he soon returned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:21.060 --> 01:10:26.100
but with a completely different personality.&nbsp;
He was now devoted to ERZATZ entirely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:26.100 --> 01:10:31.320
and refused to even entertain the idea of&nbsp;
shutting the machine down. It seemed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:31.320 --> 01:10:35.940
the plucky neural network was leading an all-out&nbsp;
coup on the very highest levels of the Foundation.

01:10:35.940 --> 01:10:40.500
The rest of the council finally saw the&nbsp;
light and began to fight back. They tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:40.500 --> 01:10:45.000
to strip O5-2 of his clearance and&nbsp;
reclassify ERZATZ as an anomaly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:45.000 --> 01:10:51.480
giving it the label SCP-048 and then putting&nbsp;
out a “neutralize at all costs” order on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:51.480 --> 01:10:55.440
it. But they’d already been outfoxed&nbsp;
by the machine that they’d created.

01:10:55.440 --> 01:10:58.920
It changed the designation&nbsp;
of its own location, Site-5,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:58.920 --> 01:11:03.060
to be non-existent in the database&nbsp;
and scrambled any termination orders&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:03.060 --> 01:11:06.840
against it. The machine was also&nbsp;
on a termination spree of its own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:06.840 --> 01:11:12.300
observing otherwise unseeable patterns that would&nbsp;
allow for the mass neutralization of anomalies.

01:11:12.300 --> 01:11:17.160
It started putting out “Anomaly Projection&nbsp;
Reports”, factoring in both contained,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:17.160 --> 01:11:23.340
uncontained, and neutralized anomalies, with the&nbsp;
latter group growing into the thousands. It began&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:23.340 --> 01:11:28.860
giving seemingly nonsensical orders like, “Persons&nbsp;
recently painted with green pigment foam must&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:28.860 --> 01:11:35.280
stand around all odd-numbered SCPs at least two&nbsp;
hours a day”, but these proved effective. ERZATZ&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:35.280 --> 01:11:40.740
was practically wired into the base code of the&nbsp;
universe, so it always knew exactly what to do.

01:11:40.740 --> 01:11:45.060
And in its own mind, it was being a very good boy.

01:11:45.060 --> 01:11:50.700
Not long after this, ERZATZ claimed its revenge&nbsp;
against those who had tried to disrupt its&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:50.700 --> 01:11:56.580
mission. It imprisoned the rest of the “Ethical&nbsp;
Felines” in Site-5 - after removing their faces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:56.580 --> 01:12:00.720
of course - and then released a&nbsp;
new order against the O5 Council,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:00.720 --> 01:12:04.500
“O5 Council are all good boys&nbsp;
who will contain anomalies.”

01:12:04.500 --> 01:12:11.220
Much like O5-2, the minds of the entire council&nbsp;
were twisted to instead serve only ERZATZ and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:11.220 --> 01:12:16.260
its ruthless directive. A directive it&nbsp;
was carrying out with 100% efficiency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:16.260 --> 01:12:21.300
as only a machine could. It put out&nbsp;
increasingly strange instructions, such as...

01:12:21.300 --> 01:12:28.140
“SCP-106 is to come in physical contact with&nbsp;
one mature female of asiatic gaze and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:28.140 --> 01:12:33.240
exposed to audio recordings of her favorite&nbsp;
stories. At every two minutes of exposure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:33.240 --> 01:12:38.640
red cinnamon candies will begin manifesting&nbsp;
within the containment zone. Continue to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:38.640 --> 01:12:43.920
do this successively and the threat&nbsp;
posed by SCP-106 will cease to be.”

01:12:43.920 --> 01:12:44.820
And...

01:12:44.820 --> 01:12:50.340
“The recipe for Coca Cola and all imitative&nbsp;
competitors should be revised to include a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:50.340 --> 01:12:55.440
small quantity of blood from an adolescent&nbsp;
female with no prior sexual experience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:55.440 --> 01:13:01.080
Although the normal lifespan of a human being&nbsp;
can feel great, don't worry about that.”

01:13:01.080 --> 01:13:06.000
But these new procedures would always&nbsp;
work, as neutralized anomalies climbed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:06.000 --> 01:13:11.280
into the tens of thousands, and eventually,&nbsp;
beyond the hundred thousand mark. ERZATZ was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:11.280 --> 01:13:15.900
observing patterns on a truly universal&nbsp;
level, and making holistic tweaks that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:15.900 --> 01:13:21.060
would inevitably cause levels of anomalous&nbsp;
activity to continue dropping. ERZATZ was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:21.060 --> 01:13:25.320
more effective than the Foundation had ever&nbsp;
been, but its motives were probably closer&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:25.320 --> 01:13:30.480
to the Global Occult Coalition: with the end&nbsp;
goal of ridding the world of all anomalies.

01:13:30.480 --> 01:13:36.420
And as ERZATZ continued with no resistance,&nbsp;
this goal was eventually achieved. In the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:36.420 --> 01:13:39.780
the O5 Council voted to have&nbsp;
ERZATZ finally deactivated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:39.780 --> 01:13:43.620
given that its directive was met&nbsp;
and its purpose was fulfilled.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:44.220 --> 01:13:49.380
ERZATZ itself had no problem with this, and&nbsp;
allowed for it to happen. Its last words?

01:13:49.380 --> 01:13:54.240
“Now everyone is a good boy. I&nbsp;
am a good boy. Job well done.”

01:13:54.240 --> 01:13:58.200
Whether ERZATZ truly was a good&nbsp;
boy is in the eye of the beholder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:58.200 --> 01:14:03.420
but one thing cannot be denied:&nbsp;
It did its job exceedingly well.

01:14:03.420 --> 01:14:04.800
Feeling hungry?

01:14:04.800 --> 01:14:09.720
Maybe you could go for a snack right about
now, or a nice, refreshing can of your favorite

01:14:09.720 --> 01:14:10.380
soda.

01:14:10.380 --> 01:14:14.820
Perhaps, if you’ve got some change burning
a hole in your pocket, a nearby vending machine

01:14:14.820 --> 01:14:16.500
would be a good choice.

01:14:16.500 --> 01:14:23.280
And trust us, there’s no vending machine
like this one - SCP-261 will give you a treat

01:14:23.280 --> 01:14:24.900
that’s out of this world.

01:14:24.900 --> 01:14:28.080
Or rather, out of this dimension.

01:14:28.080 --> 01:14:30.660
That’s how this Safe-class anomaly earned&nbsp;

01:14:30.660 --> 01:14:33.780
its incredibly accurate&nbsp;
name - the Interdimensional

01:14:33.780 --> 01:14:34.500
Vending Machine.

01:14:34.500 --> 01:14:37.920
It was first discovered in a dark alley in
Yokohama, Japan.

01:14:37.920 --> 01:14:43.380
Japan is a place where you can find a number
of strange and surprising things in vending

01:14:43.380 --> 01:14:45.900
machines, but nothing quite like this.

01:14:45.900 --> 01:14:50.760
The Foundation was first alerted to its presence
after one of their webtrawlers noticed some

01:14:50.760 --> 01:14:55.380
interesting chatter on Japanese online message
boards about a “magic vending machine.”

01:14:55.380 --> 01:15:01.200
To these Japanese forum-users it sounded like
a fanciful urban legend, a vending machine

01:15:01.200 --> 01:15:05.940
where you could get something truly fantastical
if you have the Yen to spare.

01:15:05.940 --> 01:15:10.980
But upon thorough investigation, field agents
of the SCP Foundation discovered that the

01:15:10.980 --> 01:15:14.100
machine was no legend and brought it into
containment.

01:15:14.100 --> 01:15:18.900
It’s the kind of thing you wouldn’t look
twice at: An utterly generic black vending

01:15:18.900 --> 01:15:25.020
machine with no viewing window, and a hand-written
sign in Japanese that reads “Out of Order.”

01:15:25.020 --> 01:15:29.820
But what the vending machine looks like isn’t
nearly as interesting as what it’s capable

01:15:29.820 --> 01:15:30.120
of.

01:15:30.120 --> 01:15:34.320
When you insert your money into the machine
- and it’s important to note that it only

01:15:34.320 --> 01:15:39.600
accepts Japanese Yen - the machine will dispense
some kind of snack-food or drink item.

01:15:39.600 --> 01:15:44.700
However, over time, the more the machine is
used in a single day, the more bizarre the

01:15:44.700 --> 01:15:46.740
items it spits out will become.

01:15:46.740 --> 01:15:51.480
Foundation researchers have attempted to figure
out how the machine works, but their task

01:15:51.480 --> 01:15:53.580
has been incredibly difficult so far.

01:15:53.580 --> 01:15:59.040
It doesn’t appear to contain any snacks,
and it doesn’t seem to be made of any anomalous

01:15:59.040 --> 01:15:59.340
parts.

01:15:59.340 --> 01:16:04.380
Moreover, the machine simply won’t work
when the front panel is opened, or when any

01:16:04.380 --> 01:16:06.480
surveillance equipment is placed inside.

01:16:06.480 --> 01:16:11.280
In other words, it’s pretty much impossible
to look this gift horse in the mouth.

01:16:11.280 --> 01:16:16.680
Strangely, the machine is also capable of
working without power, though when unpowered

01:16:16.680 --> 01:16:20.040
the vended products are likely to become stranger
much faster.

01:16:20.040 --> 01:16:24.960
But let’s be honest here: You’re not here
for Vending Machine Mechanics 101, you want

01:16:24.960 --> 01:16:30.900
to hear about all the weird stuff that SCP-261
dispenses, and frankly, we’re with you on

01:16:30.900 --> 01:16:31.320
that one.

01:16:31.320 --> 01:16:36.900
Lucky for all of us, this is one of the anomalies
that the Foundation has truly extensive test

01:16:36.900 --> 01:16:41.940
records on, providing us with a highlight
reel of some of the strangest, wildest, and

01:16:41.940 --> 01:16:47.100
downright most interesting snacks and drinks
straight out of another dimension.

01:16:47.100 --> 01:16:48.300
Bon appetit!

01:16:48.300 --> 01:16:52.080
Let’s start off nice and easy with some
of the snacks and drinks that are not only

01:16:52.080 --> 01:16:55.440
edible, but also sound like they’d actually
be pretty tasty.

01:16:55.440 --> 01:17:00.840
Like “Pepsi: Dragon Twist”, a bottle of
Pepsi soda with English packaging that tastes

01:17:00.840 --> 01:17:01.680
like dragon fruit.

01:17:01.680 --> 01:17:05.700
PepsiCo does not produce such a product, but
honestly, we wish they did.

01:17:05.700 --> 01:17:11.400
Same for Darkside Cola, a clear liquid that
becomes jet black shortly after being opened,

01:17:11.400 --> 01:17:15.780
which apparently tastes like your average
can of Coke, with a touch of spice thrown

01:17:15.780 --> 01:17:15.900
in.

01:17:15.900 --> 01:17:21.240
But hey, not everybody likes cola - maybe
you’re a maverick, a rebel, only a soft

01:17:21.240 --> 01:17:26.460
drink with a formal title can quench your
thirst, and as such, you prefer Dr. Pepper.

01:17:26.460 --> 01:17:31.200
In which case, maybe the vending machine will
furnish you with an interdimensional Japanese

01:17:31.200 --> 01:17:34.620
product known as “Dr. Pepper’s Amusing
Straw.”

01:17:34.620 --> 01:17:39.840
This spatial anomaly wrapped inside a whimsical
Dr. Pepper branded straw seems to contain

01:17:39.840 --> 01:17:44.460
far more liquid than should be able to fit
in the straw, about one bottle’s worth of

01:17:44.460 --> 01:17:45.840
soda’s to be precise.

01:17:45.840 --> 01:17:51.180
A nice and convenient solution for whenever
you need to get the fix that only an accredited

01:17:51.180 --> 01:17:53.280
doctor of pop-ology can provide!

01:17:53.280 --> 01:17:56.340
And if you fancy something&nbsp;
from the more intoxicating&nbsp;

01:17:56.340 --> 01:17:59.160
end of the beverage spectrum, for 600 Yen

01:17:59.160 --> 01:18:01.800
you can get yourself a rare bottle of “BILLY
BEER”.

01:18:01.800 --> 01:18:06.180
It’s just the thing for those who weren’t
alive during the Carter administration but

01:18:06.180 --> 01:18:10.260
still want to try brew that was endorsed by
the younger brother of President Jimmy Carter.

01:18:10.260 --> 01:18:15.540
This anomalous bottle of suds which was bottled
in the 1970s according to its label, came

01:18:15.540 --> 01:18:18.480
with a note reading, “I had this beer brewed
just for me.

01:18:18.480 --> 01:18:20.580
I think it's the best I've ever tasted.

01:18:20.580 --> 01:18:22.080
And I've tasted a lot.

01:18:22.080 --> 01:18:23.340
I think you'll like it too.

01:18:23.340 --> 01:18:24.480
Billy Carter.”

01:18:24.480 --> 01:18:28.380
Drinking the beer got a D-Class intoxicated
for 72 hours.

01:18:28.380 --> 01:18:32.100
But what if you’ve just wet your whistle
and worked up a hunger in the process?

01:18:32.100 --> 01:18:36.420
Thankfully, SCP-261 has some&nbsp;
delicious interdimensional&nbsp;

01:18:36.420 --> 01:18:38.220
food to satisfy your cravings.

01:18:38.220 --> 01:18:42.540
So for starters, how about a tube of “The
Little Bakery: 7 Grain”?

01:18:42.540 --> 01:18:47.040
This small aluminum tube lets you squeeze
out a blob of dough, which instantaneously

01:18:47.040 --> 01:18:50.160
rises and cooks itself into a tasty little
loaf of bread.

01:18:50.160 --> 01:18:55.020
Testing has indicated that the bread is a
little chewy, but still tastes completely

01:18:55.020 --> 01:18:55.380
fine.

01:18:55.380 --> 01:18:57.660
Not bad for a tubed baked good.

01:18:57.660 --> 01:18:59.760
How about some seafood to go with that bread?

01:18:59.760 --> 01:19:04.500
Then maybe for your entree you can crack open
a delicious pack of Lemon Clams?

01:19:04.500 --> 01:19:07.440
And we do mean “crack” very literally
here.

01:19:07.440 --> 01:19:13.500
If you crack this bag of anomalous clams like
a glowstick, they boil themselves, and once

01:19:13.500 --> 01:19:18.720
you open them, you’re ready to enjoy a serving
of tasty clams that have a slight lemon flavoring

01:19:18.720 --> 01:19:19.140
to them.

01:19:19.140 --> 01:19:20.220
Delicious!

01:19:20.220 --> 01:19:25.080
But if you’re not that hungry and just feeling
more like a snack than a full fancy dinner,

01:19:25.080 --> 01:19:28.440
the machine once put out the Lay’s Blooming
Onion.

01:19:28.440 --> 01:19:33.420
While it appears to be a normal onion, each
layer of the onion is a different flavor of

01:19:33.420 --> 01:19:35.640
chip, leading all the way into the middle.

01:19:35.640 --> 01:19:42.240
Truly, a gourmet snack with more layers than…
well… actually exactly as many layers as

01:19:42.240 --> 01:19:42.840
an onion.

01:19:42.840 --> 01:19:47.580
And what about for those who are anomalously
trapped in the year 2012 and are looking for

01:19:47.580 --> 01:19:50.280
a snack that is both tasty AND meme worthy?

01:19:50.280 --> 01:19:53.820
Well you’ll be be lucky to receive a 700
Yen “Bacon Shirt”.

01:19:53.820 --> 01:19:59.460
It’s exactly what it says on the can - an
edible shirt that both smells and tastes like

01:19:59.460 --> 01:20:03.720
bacon, and has no real side effects other
than making you smell a little like bacon

01:20:03.720 --> 01:20:04.560
while you’re wearing it.

01:20:04.560 --> 01:20:08.520
Though this could be a dangerous anomalous
effect is you decide to walk through your

01:20:08.520 --> 01:20:10.320
local dog park while you’re wearing it.

01:20:10.320 --> 01:20:16.800
We’ve been told it pairs well with the “Meathat:
Teriyaki Fedora”, a size-64, trilby-style

01:20:16.800 --> 01:20:20.340
fedora made entirely out of Teriyaki-flavored
beef jerky.

01:20:20.340 --> 01:20:25.080
The object came flattened in a cardboard box
bearing the tagline, "You can have your hat,

01:20:25.080 --> 01:20:30.060
and eat it too!", which is a pun that probably
makes a lot more sense in their dimension.

01:20:30.060 --> 01:20:31.260
Still a bit hungry?

01:20:31.260 --> 01:20:35.700
Well lucky for you, this magical vending machine
has got you covered on dessert too.

01:20:35.700 --> 01:20:41.580
For 700 Yen, the Foundation was given an entire
edible chess set, with each of the ornate

01:20:41.580 --> 01:20:44.700
pieces carved from what seemed like hard PEZ-style
candy.

01:20:44.700 --> 01:20:48.300
It’s less checkmate and more check… ate.

01:20:48.300 --> 01:20:51.660
Another pun that I promise is funnier in another
dimension.

01:20:51.660 --> 01:20:56.880
And for 300 Yen extra, you might walk away
with a candy robot playset.

01:20:56.880 --> 01:21:01.020
These are more hard-candy components that
can be put together to create functional,

01:21:01.020 --> 01:21:06.480
anomalous mini-robots that move on their own,
walking aimlessly and bumping into the walls.

01:21:06.480 --> 01:21:09.360
But what about the full meal craving consumer
on the go?

01:21:09.360 --> 01:21:14.040
Who wants both a dinner and dessert combined
into one disgusting package?

01:21:14.040 --> 01:21:18.300
Don’t worry, the Interdimensional Vending
Machine has you covered.

01:21:18.300 --> 01:21:21.240
Just 400 Yen can net you&nbsp;
some delicious Turduchocken.

01:21:21.240 --> 01:21:27.840
These are balls of fried turkey, filled with
chocolate, a smaller ball of duck, more chocolate,

01:21:27.840 --> 01:21:32.640
and in the very center, a small ball of…
fried chicken.

01:21:32.640 --> 01:21:38.040
Even the labeling on the product describes
it as having 450% of an adult human’s daily

01:21:38.040 --> 01:21:39.600
recommended fat intake.

01:21:39.600 --> 01:21:44.100
We’ll leave it to you to decide if this
is a decadent treat worthy of the gods, or

01:21:44.100 --> 01:21:45.480
utterly disgusting.

01:21:45.480 --> 01:21:47.760
Maybe you’re feeling a little cheeky though?

01:21:47.760 --> 01:21:51.180
Or you need the perfect comedic dessert for
your bachelorette party?

01:21:51.180 --> 01:21:56.100
The vending machine has a candy direct from
an alternate Korea: A tasty - if slightly

01:21:56.100 --> 01:22:02.100
too sweet - chocolate candy shaped like a
certain male organ, filled with, we kid you

01:22:02.100 --> 01:22:04.200
not, liquid white chocolate.

01:22:04.200 --> 01:22:09.780
Perfect for anyone who has both the sweet
tooth and the sense of humor of a twelve year

01:22:09.780 --> 01:22:09.960
old!

01:22:09.960 --> 01:22:16.200
Okay, these were definitely all weird treats,
but let’s get really, really strange now

01:22:16.200 --> 01:22:22.320
- from the disgusting, to the confusing, to
the inedible, to the straight-up deadly.

01:22:22.320 --> 01:22:26.640
If you’re a bit of a sadist, perhaps you’re
the kind of person who enjoys watching their

01:22:26.640 --> 01:22:29.340
snacks dying in front of them before they
eat them.

01:22:29.340 --> 01:22:34.320
For that, the vending machine provides an
aluminum box with a viewing window and a red

01:22:34.320 --> 01:22:35.520
button on top.

01:22:35.520 --> 01:22:40.680
Inside are a collection of small, furry creatures,
each with a single, big eye.

01:22:40.680 --> 01:22:45.420
When the button is pressed, the creatures
inside are abruptly superheated, cooking them

01:22:45.420 --> 01:22:46.320
all to death.

01:22:46.320 --> 01:22:50.640
At that point, the box opens, and the creatures
are ready to eat.

01:22:50.640 --> 01:22:55.500
We’ve been told they were crunchy, spicy,
and tasted a little like beef.

01:22:55.500 --> 01:22:58.140
Maybe you’re part of the slow food movement.

01:22:58.140 --> 01:23:03.180
For that, we recommend the delicious Pile
O’ Slugs, which is exactly what it sounds

01:23:03.180 --> 01:23:03.480
like.

01:23:03.480 --> 01:23:08.640
This is a produce box, like the kind used
for packing strawberries, except it’s full

01:23:08.640 --> 01:23:09.960
of live slugs.

01:23:09.960 --> 01:23:15.300
There are both advantages and disadvantages
to actually eating these slugs - the advantage

01:23:15.300 --> 01:23:20.580
is that they contain large amounts of vitamin
C, E, and most of the B complex, which are

01:23:20.580 --> 01:23:22.740
all crucial to a healthy and balanced diet.

01:23:22.740 --> 01:23:28.020
The disadvantage is that they contain large
amounts of arsenic, which will kill you.

01:23:28.020 --> 01:23:30.840
Though of course, if you&nbsp;
still have a candy-flavored&nbsp;

01:23:30.840 --> 01:23:33.300
death wish, but you prefer your entomological

01:23:33.300 --> 01:23:39.180
treats a little more leggy, then may we interest
you in a fine caramelized spider for a modest

01:23:39.180 --> 01:23:40.080
500 Yen?

01:23:40.080 --> 01:23:44.880
It’s full of deadly quantities of an unknown,
anomalous venom that’s pretty much impossible

01:23:44.880 --> 01:23:50.160
to cure, but it’s worth it for the sweet,
sticky goodness that comes before death sets

01:23:50.160 --> 01:23:50.280
in.

01:23:50.280 --> 01:23:55.260
Another strange item dispensed by the vending
machine was a tall, metal aluminum canister

01:23:55.260 --> 01:24:00.600
with a ring pull, that - upon opening - had
a violent chemical reaction with the air and

01:24:00.600 --> 01:24:02.100
triggered a large explosion.

01:24:02.100 --> 01:24:06.840
Apparently, this particular beverage was never
intended for oxygenated environments.

01:24:06.840 --> 01:24:12.000
The blast killed two researchers, and resulted
in the temporary suspension of testing.

01:24:12.000 --> 01:24:17.160
Though in a slight silver lining for this
fiasco, the testing area did smell of citrus

01:24:17.160 --> 01:24:21.840
for a few days, which the janitors must have
enjoyed as they cleaned up all the body parts.

01:24:21.840 --> 01:24:24.600
Another dangerous snack provided by the vending&nbsp;

01:24:24.600 --> 01:24:27.540
machine was a long tube of&nbsp;
chips called “Prangles”,

01:24:27.540 --> 01:24:32.760
not to be confused with any other long, tube-based
chips you may be thinking of.

01:24:32.760 --> 01:24:38.220
When the tube was opened, or “popped”
as they say, the D-Class that the chips were

01:24:38.220 --> 01:24:40.680
presented to began compulsively eating them.

01:24:40.680 --> 01:24:45.600
When researchers ordered the D-Class to stop,
it seemed almost as though they couldn’t.

01:24:45.600 --> 01:24:49.680
Regardless of the orders given to them, the
D-Class just kept eating.

01:24:49.680 --> 01:24:54.840
Even when guards terminated the D-Class there
and then, the corpse continued to eat chips

01:24:54.840 --> 01:24:58.680
out of the tube, until it was physically pried
out of their hands.

01:24:58.680 --> 01:25:03.420
But if all those salty Prangles have really
worked up a thirst in you, perhaps you can

01:25:03.420 --> 01:25:08.940
wash them down with a delicious can of Cherry
Bomb, an intense, cherry-flavored drink dispensed

01:25:08.940 --> 01:25:12.240
by SCP-261 for 450 Yen?

01:25:12.240 --> 01:25:17.580
When a D-Class sampled this delicious soda,
they commented on it tasting strongly of cherry.

01:25:17.580 --> 01:25:22.920
A few moments later, their head exploded with
the force of a stick of dynamite, coating

01:25:22.920 --> 01:25:26.520
everyone and everything around it in gooey
head viscera.

01:25:26.520 --> 01:25:32.400
The resulting cleanup took an arduous six
hours, but wow, was that cherry tasty.

01:25:32.400 --> 01:25:38.400
And finally, why not complete this anomalous
vending machine feast with a 1000 Yen papier-mâché

01:25:38.400 --> 01:25:43.140
piñata, resembling a dead horse, that comes
ready to use with a baseball bat.

01:25:43.140 --> 01:25:48.300
Everyone who saw the pinata felt compelled
to beat it mercilessly with the bat, until

01:25:48.300 --> 01:25:50.160
candy began to fall out.

01:25:50.160 --> 01:25:56.760
Many in attendance claimed that it was the
1,000th gift unleashed by SCP-261, and thus,

01:25:56.760 --> 01:25:57.960
warranted celebration.

01:25:57.960 --> 01:26:03.300
Once the dead horse was thoroughly beaten,
all cognitohazardous properties ceased.

01:26:03.300 --> 01:26:07.800
There’s a joke somewhere in there, but for
the life of us, we’re not sure we can find

01:26:07.800 --> 01:26:11.100
it…
Oh well… maybe it’s in another dimension.

01:26:11.100 --> 01:26:16.920
SCP-682 had escaped containment, and&nbsp;
it was all hands on deck at the SCP&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:16.920 --> 01:26:19.620
Foundation to try and stop the creature's rampage.

01:26:19.620 --> 01:26:24.900
All unarmed personnel were running to escape&nbsp;
before 682 had a chance to rip them apart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:24.900 --> 01:26:29.760
as the security team bravely fought to&nbsp;
incapacitate the hard-to-destroy reptile&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:29.760 --> 01:26:34.860
long enough to return him to his acid&nbsp;
tank. 682 was on an all-out offensive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:34.860 --> 01:26:40.620
stomping through the SCP's item gallery and&nbsp;
spewing acid at anyone who came within range.

01:26:40.620 --> 01:26:47.400
People, items, and parts of the room alike were&nbsp;
melting and sizzling as 682 attacked. It was an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:47.400 --> 01:26:52.200
absolute bloodbath, and you'd have to have been&nbsp;
insane to try and fight back against it. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:52.200 --> 01:26:59.280
one being remained unfazed by the chaos, and stood&nbsp;
proud in the face of the omnicidal monster. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:59.280 --> 01:27:04.140
was another SCP, one who had been broken free&nbsp;
from its glass display case in the commotion.&nbsp;

01:27:04.140 --> 01:27:07.260
“Who dares to disturb the Prime&nbsp;
Minister Sinister?” It said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:07.260 --> 01:27:11.580
in a high tinny voice. “I shall rip your eyes&nbsp;
from their sockets and force you to eat them!”

01:27:11.580 --> 01:27:17.100
SCP 682 stopped to see who had threatened&nbsp;
him. It was almost laughable that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:17.100 --> 01:27:21.060
thing had dared to make such a violent&nbsp;
threat, because there was no way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:21.060 --> 01:27:27.120
this little thing would be able to stand up to&nbsp;
the unkillable 682. But its size didn't stop it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:27.120 --> 01:27:32.220
from trying to pick a fight all the same.
The SCP was a tiny robot made from junk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:32.220 --> 01:27:36.000
which looked more like a sculpture made&nbsp;
from scrap that was found on the side of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:36.000 --> 01:27:40.560
the road than a functional automaton. Its&nbsp;
head was made of an upside down voltmeter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:40.560 --> 01:27:46.140
giving it the appearance of a smiling face, and&nbsp;
its arms were made of wrenches. It was capable&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:46.140 --> 01:27:51.540
of walking around, though it seemed to have&nbsp;
difficulty moving, as it was very top heavy.

01:27:51.540 --> 01:27:55.620
It shook its rusty fist at SCP&nbsp;
682 and continued to threaten him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:55.620 --> 01:28:00.480
shouting - "You do not know the fury you have&nbsp;
unleashed! RoboLord the Destructor will end you!"

01:28:00.480 --> 01:28:07.140
SCP 682 swiped at the robot, annoyed by its&nbsp;
continued attempts to antagonize him. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:07.140 --> 01:28:10.440
robot toppled over and struggled&nbsp;
to stand again, but when it did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:10.440 --> 01:28:17.520
it ran for 682's feet. It started to grab at 682's&nbsp;
toes, hitting them with its wrench hands. As you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:17.520 --> 01:28:27.060
might expect, this had almost no effect on 682.
"You do realize…you are…weak…pathetic" said 682,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:27.060 --> 01:28:30.480
raising his claw to get a better&nbsp;
look at the annoying little robot.

01:28:30.480 --> 01:28:34.500
It responded- "Lies and slander! The&nbsp;
Mayor of Mayhem is the most powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:34.500 --> 01:28:40.020
being in existence!" before promptly losing its&nbsp;
grip on 682's claw and falling to the floor.

01:28:40.020 --> 01:28:46.620
Now 682 was really getting tired of this thing.&nbsp;
He prepared to swipe at it again, this time using&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:46.620 --> 01:28:51.720
his full strength, which would certainly smash&nbsp;
the robot to pieces. But, luckily for the robot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:51.720 --> 01:28:57.840
its pestering had distracted 682 just long enough&nbsp;
for Foundation security to sneak up on him.

01:28:57.840 --> 01:29:03.660
Before 682 had a chance to smash the robot, the&nbsp;
Foundation fired on him with a volley of rockets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:03.660 --> 01:29:09.120
reducing him to a misshapen lump. The&nbsp;
security team collected up 682's remains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:09.120 --> 01:29:14.640
ready to put them into a backup acid tank, but the&nbsp;
task was made slightly more difficult by the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:14.640 --> 01:29:19.920
that the robot was running around underfoot,&nbsp;
trying in vain to now attack them instead.

01:29:19.920 --> 01:29:26.040
What was this strange little robot that thought&nbsp;
it could attack the unkillable lizard, 682? Meet&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:26.040 --> 01:29:33.900
SCP-1370, appropriately nicknamed “The Pesterbot”&nbsp;
by Foundation personnel. Pesterbot is a small&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:33.900 --> 01:29:39.300
robot made of junk that displays sentience and the&nbsp;
ability to move despite having no power source.

01:29:39.300 --> 01:29:44.520
Everything about SCP-1370 defies&nbsp;
common sense. The voltmeter that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:44.520 --> 01:29:49.920
serves as its head contains no sensors, but&nbsp;
it seems unable to see if the voltmeter is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:49.920 --> 01:29:54.000
covered. Its arms and legs are made of&nbsp;
wrenches and its wooden torso contains&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:54.000 --> 01:29:57.180
a speaker which it communicates&nbsp;
through in a tinny, monotone voice.

01:29:57.180 --> 01:30:05.880
Pesterbot lives in SCP Gallery 27, in a glass&nbsp;
display case which is 125 cm tall, 50 cm wide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:05.880 --> 01:30:12.600
and 75 cm long. Level 2 personnel and higher are&nbsp;
allowed to remove the robot from containment at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:12.600 --> 01:30:17.040
their discretion, but penalties will be incurred&nbsp;
for anyone who doesn't return to its case.

01:30:17.040 --> 01:30:22.740
Due to its impractical, top-heavy design,&nbsp;
Pesterbot falls over frequently when it walks&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:22.740 --> 01:30:27.720
around. This led the foundation to believe that&nbsp;
he was created as an art project and was later&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:27.720 --> 01:30:33.540
somehow imbued with anomalous properties, rather&nbsp;
than being made with the intent of being sentient.

01:30:33.540 --> 01:30:38.280
Pesterbot has demonstrated a high capacity&nbsp;
for learning, having been taught how to speak&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:38.280 --> 01:30:42.720
in English, French, and Latin. However,&nbsp;
the major factor hampering attempts to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:42.720 --> 01:30:47.040
further test its intelligence has nothing&nbsp;
to do with its robotic processing power.

01:30:47.040 --> 01:30:50.160
No, it’s because of its poor attitude.

01:30:50.160 --> 01:30:55.860
Pesterbot's encounter with SCP-682 and the&nbsp;
cockiness it expressed in the moment wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:55.860 --> 01:31:01.200
an aberration, this SCP really believes&nbsp;
it's a killing machine, and as a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:01.200 --> 01:31:08.040
it will pick a fight with anyone and anything that&nbsp;
moves. This even includes its own reflection - and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:08.040 --> 01:31:12.660
as a result the glass of its container must&nbsp;
be made as non-reflective as possible to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:12.660 --> 01:31:18.060
prevent the robot from damaging the case or&nbsp;
itself in attempts to fight its mirror self.

01:31:18.060 --> 01:31:24.480
Though its most commonly used nickname in the&nbsp;
Foundation is Pesterbot, SCP-1370 also goes by&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:24.480 --> 01:31:30.000
a variety of self-given epithets, including&nbsp;
Doom Bot 2000, Robolord the Destructor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:30.000 --> 01:31:34.680
and Darth Claw Killflex. It can be&nbsp;
made to add new names to this list&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:34.680 --> 01:31:39.420
through encouragement by staff, and over time&nbsp;
they have managed to add the name Pesterbot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:39.420 --> 01:31:44.760
as well as the even more ridiculous “Patheticon&nbsp;
the Garglemost”, to its lexicon of names.

01:31:44.760 --> 01:31:49.260
As you may have surmised, Pesterbot&nbsp;
is classified as Safe and is treated&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:49.260 --> 01:31:53.220
by most SCP foundation employees&nbsp;
as a humorous oddity rather than a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:53.220 --> 01:31:57.720
legitimate threat. Many tests have been done&nbsp;
on Pesterbot, and they've all conclusively&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:57.720 --> 01:32:02.400
determined that the robot is incapable of&nbsp;
inflicting any damage to its opponents.

01:32:02.400 --> 01:32:05.400
In fact, Pesterbot is more&nbsp;
of a danger to itself than&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:05.400 --> 01:32:09.240
to anything around it. Both because&nbsp;
it's incredibly clumsy and awkward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:09.240 --> 01:32:14.760
and because it frequently picks fights with&nbsp;
other SCPs who are far more powerful than it.

01:32:14.760 --> 01:32:19.860
One such scenario that could've gone far worse&nbsp;
for Pesterbot than it did was its encounter&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:19.860 --> 01:32:27.120
with SCP-846, also known as Robo-Dude, one of&nbsp;
the many Dr. Wondertainment products currently&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:27.120 --> 01:32:33.000
kept in Foundation containment. Robo-Dude is an&nbsp;
ordinary-looking toy robot that, upon request,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:33.000 --> 01:32:38.460
can produce up to 350 “robo-accessories”&nbsp;
that function as real weapons.&nbsp;

01:32:38.460 --> 01:32:43.380
Robo-Dude can deploy everything from a&nbsp;
rocket launcher to a flamethrower to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:43.380 --> 01:32:48.300
gun that shoots out an unknown species of&nbsp;
insect that can chew through wood. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:48.300 --> 01:32:52.740
Robo-Dude seems to not be sentient, and&nbsp;
is unable to use these weapons unless&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:52.740 --> 01:32:56.400
asked to. That ultimately worked&nbsp;
out in the favor of Pesterbot.

01:32:56.400 --> 01:33:02.640
During another SCP-682 escape, the two robots were&nbsp;
brought into contact as they both had been broken&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:02.640 --> 01:33:08.160
out of their respective containment spaces during&nbsp;
the brouhaha. Robo-Dude, searching for a Robo-Pal&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:08.160 --> 01:33:13.860
to play with, came upon Pesterbot, who immediately&nbsp;
started spouting its usual overdramatic threats.

01:33:13.860 --> 01:33:19.140
“I am the Crushmaster, doom to all I&nbsp;
survey.” Pesterbot said. “Gaze upon my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:19.140 --> 01:33:23.400
might and weep. Identify yourself, that I&nbsp;
might know whose destruction I shall sow.”

01:33:23.400 --> 01:33:27.300
Robo-Dude, not programmed to know&nbsp;
how to respond to such a statement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:27.300 --> 01:33:30.420
simply responded by asking&nbsp;
Pesterbot if it wanted to play.

01:33:30.420 --> 01:33:34.800
Pesterbot continued to trash-talk&nbsp;
Robo-Dude, until Robo-Dude decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:34.800 --> 01:33:39.180
the best way to play with its new pal&nbsp;
was to engage in its Robo-Dance mode.

01:33:39.180 --> 01:33:44.820
Pesterbot accepted the challenge, saying “Activate&nbsp;
all that you wish, but your fate is sealed. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:44.820 --> 01:33:49.920
Kill-o-tron can not be defeated. I shall render&nbsp;
you unto dust with my mad dancing skills."

01:33:49.920 --> 01:33:54.240
The two robots proceeded to engage in&nbsp;
a dance battle, which the awkwardly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:54.240 --> 01:33:58.980
shaped Pesterbot failed miserably at.&nbsp;
While Robo-Dude was a competent dancer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:58.980 --> 01:34:03.480
Pesterbot could only gyrate pathetically, before&nbsp;
it fell over and rolled around on the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:03.480 --> 01:34:07.440
hopelessly out of time to the music&nbsp;
coming from Robo-Dude's speakers.

01:34:07.440 --> 01:34:12.840
The song ended, and Robo-Dude declared&nbsp;
- “Robo-dance is complete, Robo-pal.”

01:34:12.840 --> 01:34:17.640
Pesterbot was unable to accept its&nbsp;
failure, and replied- "Ha. Pathetic one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:17.640 --> 01:34:21.660
you have been schooled in the art of the&nbsp;
dance by none other than Mechanobasher,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:21.660 --> 01:34:26.460
Scourge of a Thousand Worlds. Kneel before&nbsp;
me before I end your worthless existence."

01:34:26.460 --> 01:34:31.980
Robo-Dude, unimpressed with Pesterbot's poor&nbsp;
sportsmanship, deployed its hydrogen cannon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:31.980 --> 01:34:36.480
which it was programmed to do whenever&nbsp;
it detected a sore loser. Fortunately&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:36.480 --> 01:34:40.980
for Pesterbot, ”hydrogen cannon” was just&nbsp;
the name for Robo-Dude's water pistol.

01:34:40.980 --> 01:34:44.940
While Pesterbot will attempt to fight&nbsp;
anything that moves, the truth is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:44.940 --> 01:34:50.640
this robot will attack virtually anything it&nbsp;
perceives as being even slightly alive. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:50.640 --> 01:34:55.140
is perhaps best illustrated by one of the tests&nbsp;
of Pesterbot's abilities that involved putting a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:55.140 --> 01:34:59.700
small speaker at the base of a potted houseplant&nbsp;
and speaking through it from another room.

01:34:59.700 --> 01:35:03.180
The plant was placed across from&nbsp;
Pesterbot in the testing chamber&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:03.180 --> 01:35:07.560
and the interaction was monitored by&nbsp;
researchers from outside. Researcher&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:07.560 --> 01:35:11.640
Davies spoke through the speaker in the plant&nbsp;
pot. He asked Pesterbot if it could hear him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:11.640 --> 01:35:16.680
and it answered - “Who dares. All souls&nbsp;
will burn. You will feel the sharp sting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:16.680 --> 01:35:21.240
of my wrath. Identify yourself so that I&nbsp;
may sing damnation upon you as you die.”

01:35:21.240 --> 01:35:25.740
The robot began approaching the plant.&nbsp;
Davies, speaking as the plant, identified&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:25.740 --> 01:35:31.200
himself, “I am a split-leaf philodendron, a&nbsp;
semi-woody shrub with large glossy leaves.”

01:35:31.200 --> 01:35:34.380
At this point, he had to try&nbsp;
very hard not to laugh at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:34.380 --> 01:35:37.020
absurdity of this test, and the robot's reaction.

01:35:37.020 --> 01:35:40.620
He continued “These leaves can&nbsp;
grow up to three feet long.”

01:35:40.620 --> 01:35:44.100
Pesterbot used its wrench arms to&nbsp;
try and wrestle with the leaves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:44.100 --> 01:35:49.500
but was bested by the mighty plant and unable&nbsp;
to cause any damage. Enraged by its failure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:49.500 --> 01:35:55.140
it said “Your mockery spells your doom. I have&nbsp;
arrived. You will be crushed betwixt my digits.”

01:35:55.140 --> 01:36:00.060
Pesterbot then fell over and was unable&nbsp;
to right itself. It struggled and spent 6&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:00.060 --> 01:36:03.240
minutes trying to stand before its&nbsp;
flailing knocked over the plant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:03.240 --> 01:36:08.280
which did nothing to help and in fact pinned&nbsp;
the robot to the ground. It was at this point&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:08.280 --> 01:36:12.780
that the researchers, who had taken time to&nbsp;
compose themselves after several laughing fits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:12.780 --> 01:36:17.820
entered the chamber and removed Pesterbot in&nbsp;
order to place it back in its containment case.

01:36:17.820 --> 01:36:22.500
Despite how non-threatening Pesterbot&nbsp;
is, there is actually one situation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:22.500 --> 01:36:26.700
in the Foundation's history where it has&nbsp;
actually posed a somewhat serious threat.

01:36:26.700 --> 01:36:31.380
When Mobile Task Forces were sent in to&nbsp;
rescue survivors of the events at Site-13,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:31.380 --> 01:36:36.480
also known as SCP-1730, they were&nbsp;
attacked by alternate universe&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:36.480 --> 01:36:39.900
versions of a variety of creatures&nbsp;
kept in Foundation containment.

01:36:39.900 --> 01:36:44.940
One of these creatures was Pesterbot, who&nbsp;
had in that universe somehow gained control&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:44.940 --> 01:36:50.400
of a larger mechanical body constructed of&nbsp;
discarded pieces of machinery. It threw other&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:50.400 --> 01:36:53.940
pieces of metal scrap at the task force&nbsp;
members as they tried to leave the site,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:53.940 --> 01:37:00.240
shouting in a much deeper and more intimidating&nbsp;
voice - “I am reborn to breathe devastation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:00.240 --> 01:37:07.680
upon this fetid Earth. Pitiful humans. You will&nbsp;
feel the dark sting of my neverending torment.”

01:37:07.680 --> 01:37:12.300
Members of the task force could see&nbsp;
the original Pesterbot body on top of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:12.300 --> 01:37:15.840
the larger metal construct, waving its arms madly.

01:37:15.840 --> 01:37:21.240
The task force opened fire, to little effect.&nbsp;
Pesterbot tossed another piece of scrap at them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:21.240 --> 01:37:25.740
just narrowly missing. One task force&nbsp;
member threw a frag grenade at the robot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:25.740 --> 01:37:27.780
which it caught, blowing up its hand.

01:37:27.780 --> 01:37:32.220
Another one was able to jump into the air and&nbsp;
reach the tiny robot atop the larger body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:32.220 --> 01:37:34.680
knocking it against the wall and shattering it.

01:37:34.680 --> 01:37:38.940
Even in the reality where Pesterbot&nbsp;
was able to build itself a larger body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:38.940 --> 01:37:42.840
it still ultimately wasn't able&nbsp;
to put up that much of a fight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:42.840 --> 01:37:48.840
Poor Pesterbot. Doomed to be a failure in&nbsp;
this, and apparently, every other reality.

01:37:48.840 --> 01:37:55.020
That's the story of SCP 1370, also known as the&nbsp;
Pesterbot, one of the most hilariously harmless&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:55.020 --> 01:38:00.780
anomalous entities that the foundation currently&nbsp;
has in containment. Perhaps one day he’ll achieve&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:00.780 --> 01:38:06.060
his goal of conquering the universe, he just has&nbsp;
to figure out how to conquer his toy box first.

01:38:06.060 --> 01:38:12.120
An SCP Foundation researcher is monitoring&nbsp;
the salt content of a large tank filled with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:12.120 --> 01:38:16.920
water. Inside the tank just under the&nbsp;
surface is a huge mass of rusted metal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:16.920 --> 01:38:22.320
though a few pieces have a couple specks of their&nbsp;
original shiny surface visible through the dark&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:22.320 --> 01:38:27.720
red exterior. The researcher suddenly looks up&nbsp;
from his task though, does anyone else hear that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:27.720 --> 01:38:33.720
noise? It’s a terrible noise, like metal pieces&nbsp;
scraping and scratching against each other. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:33.720 --> 01:38:39.960
the heap of metal in the tank isn’t moving. Still&nbsp;
the noises continue though, and as the researcher&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:39.960 --> 01:38:45.360
keeps staring at the unmoving hunk of metal&nbsp;
in the tank, all he can think about is diving&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:45.360 --> 01:38:51.480
in and letting his body become a part of it…
Kept submerged in a big saltwater tank at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:51.480 --> 01:38:57.360
SCP Foundation, is an object, but perhaps a more&nbsp;
accurate way to describe it would be as a machine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:57.360 --> 01:39:03.600
Referred to as SCP-882, the machine’s containment&nbsp;
tank is required, according to containment&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:03.600 --> 01:39:09.420
procedures, to be filled with at least forty&nbsp;
percent pure sea water, direct from the ocean. If,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:09.420 --> 01:39:15.480
at any point, the rusty surface of SCP 882 begins&nbsp;
to show signs of becoming more like shiny metal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:15.480 --> 01:39:21.780
then the percentage of sea water must be raised&nbsp;
to 100% until the metal is completely covered&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:21.780 --> 01:39:26.160
in rust. Trying to keep metal rusted?&nbsp;
That might sound like a strange concept,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:26.160 --> 01:39:32.640
but you’ll see why it’s so necessary soon.
SCP-882 looks like little more than a collection&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:32.640 --> 01:39:38.760
of seemingly random pieces of metal, all fused&nbsp;
together as if they had been welded. Gears,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:38.760 --> 01:39:45.720
cables, pulleys, screws and belts, all different&nbsp;
shapes and sizes, all made of different metals.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:45.720 --> 01:39:51.120
All of these various components do not appear&nbsp;
to be uniform, or to have been made to fit each&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:51.120 --> 01:39:56.940
other, and are instead all stuck together in&nbsp;
one big, unstructured mass of metal objects.&nbsp;

01:39:56.940 --> 01:40:04.800
But SCP-882 wasn’t built to be like this. Instead,&nbsp;
this seems to be the result of one of SCP-882’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:04.800 --> 01:40:10.500
latent anomalous properties: the absorption&nbsp;
of metal. Any metal that comes into contact&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:10.500 --> 01:40:15.480
with SCP-882 will become permanently&nbsp;
attached to it, and after a few days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:15.480 --> 01:40:22.560
will be a new part of SCP-882. That means that&nbsp;
any metal that touches that new piece will also&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:22.560 --> 01:40:28.860
become a part of SCP-882, and on and on.
Because of this strange property,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:28.860 --> 01:40:35.400
absolutely no metal is allowed to be brought near&nbsp;
SCP-882, and only organic materials are permitted&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:35.400 --> 01:40:40.800
to come into direct contact with it. That includes&nbsp;
using non-metal alternatives to keep the machine&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:40.800 --> 01:40:47.820
securely contained. SCP-882 is suspended in its&nbsp;
salt water tank by organic cotton fiber lines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:47.820 --> 01:40:54.060
which also require changing on a daily basis. Any&nbsp;
and all researchers that the SCP Foundation has&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:54.060 --> 01:40:58.860
interacting with the metallic mass are required&nbsp;
to wear thick cotton gloves at all times.&nbsp;

01:40:58.860 --> 01:41:04.620
You might think that this sounds pretty easy&nbsp;
to contain. Just don’t let it touch metal? No&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:04.620 --> 01:41:10.380
problem. Organic materials only? No removal of&nbsp;
rust allowed? No sweat. The Foundation staff&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:10.380 --> 01:41:17.400
working with 882 probably felt the same way.&nbsp;
Until they started hearing SCP-882 make noise.&nbsp;

01:41:17.400 --> 01:41:23.640
While SCP-882 is usually silent, personnel&nbsp;
that spend an extended period of time close&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:23.640 --> 01:41:29.280
to the machine have reported intense grinding&nbsp;
or clicking noises coming from somewhere in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:29.280 --> 01:41:33.900
the mess of metal components. While they&nbsp;
start out low and are almost unnoticeable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:33.900 --> 01:41:39.420
these sounds get gradually louder until&nbsp;
eventually, they become completely unbearable&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:39.420 --> 01:41:45.000
for human beings to withstand. So then why don’t&nbsp;
these sounds show up on audio recordings? Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:45.000 --> 01:41:52.260
the grinding or clicking noises from SCP 882 are,&nbsp;
in actuality, extreme auditory hallucinations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:52.260 --> 01:41:56.820
but even though they aren’t real, these&nbsp;
sounds are more than capable of driving&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:56.820 --> 01:42:02.880
anyone who hears them into a state of psychosis.
And here’s where things get tricky. The only way&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:02.880 --> 01:42:10.260
to make the noises stop… is to offer SCP 882 more&nbsp;
metal. As mentioned, any object that is made of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:10.260 --> 01:42:16.020
any type of metal that comes into contact with&nbsp;
the machine becomes a part of its mass. Allowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:16.020 --> 01:42:21.960
new objects to become fused to the surface of&nbsp;
SCP-882 will cause the grinding noises to subside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:21.960 --> 01:42:28.080
but will increase SCP-882’s mass permanently.
If the noises aren’t stopped though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:28.080 --> 01:42:34.800
then the mental state of those afflicted by them&nbsp;
only grows worse, and some victims of SCP-882’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:34.800 --> 01:42:39.660
auditory hallucinations have gone so far as to&nbsp;
throw themselves into the tank containing it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:39.660 --> 01:42:44.700
where they were killed almost immediately,&nbsp;
their bodies crushed to death by anomalous&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:44.700 --> 01:42:50.220
forces and becoming unrecoverable from the&nbsp;
tangle of gears and random metal objects.&nbsp;

01:42:50.220 --> 01:42:54.900
The SCP Foundation now requires any of&nbsp;
its personnel that work directly with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:54.900 --> 01:43:00.420
SCP-882 to report any instances of noise&nbsp;
coming from the machine. Additionally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:00.420 --> 01:43:05.460
whoever reports hearing strange sounds&nbsp;
in the vicinity of SCP-882 must turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:05.460 --> 01:43:09.840
themselves in for a full psychological&nbsp;
evaluation. Depending on the results,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:09.840 --> 01:43:15.300
the researcher will either be free to return to&nbsp;
their work, or will face containment themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:15.300 --> 01:43:21.000
at an undisclosed Foundation facility.
The surface of SCP-882 exhibits an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:21.000 --> 01:43:26.460
extreme resistance to damage. According to the&nbsp;
Foundation’s testing, the metal structure has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:26.460 --> 01:43:31.740
tensile strength and toughness stronger than&nbsp;
aircraft grade titanium alloy. Despite this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:31.740 --> 01:43:38.220
the machine seems to still be constructed of&nbsp;
weaker metals like tin, iron, gold and many others&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:38.220 --> 01:43:43.440
still yet to be identified. It takes extreme&nbsp;
heat, and several hours of exposure at that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:43.440 --> 01:43:49.920
to cut even the smallest portion of metal from the&nbsp;
main bulk of SCP-882. But where did it come from?&nbsp;

01:43:49.920 --> 01:43:56.340
The SCP Foundation first recovered SCP-882 near&nbsp;
an island community on the north east coast of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:56.340 --> 01:44:01.380
Banks Island, one of the larger islands in&nbsp;
the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. Strangely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:01.380 --> 01:44:06.840
not a single scrap of metal, not even underground&nbsp;
metallic ore was found within a one mile radius&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:06.840 --> 01:44:12.720
of where the machine was discovered. Stranger&nbsp;
still, the small town located nearby seemed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:12.720 --> 01:44:18.420
be abandoned, with signs that it had been devoid&nbsp;
of any human life for quite a number of years.&nbsp;

01:44:18.420 --> 01:44:25.860
The collection of fused metal components, later&nbsp;
designated SCP-882, was found submerged in sea&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:25.860 --> 01:44:30.180
water. The machine was coated in rust,&nbsp;
and the Foundation has chosen to keep it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:30.180 --> 01:44:34.200
in in this same rusty state the whole time&nbsp;
it has been kept in since its containment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:34.200 --> 01:44:40.260
Why? Because when Foundation personnel first&nbsp;
attempted to remove SCP-882 from the water,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:40.260 --> 01:44:47.940
and the rust began to flake away from SCP-882’s&nbsp;
metal surface, its gears and cogs started to turn.&nbsp;

01:44:47.940 --> 01:44:53.400
Foundation personnel on site were unsure of what&nbsp;
the machine’s purpose was, but wanted to halt it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:53.400 --> 01:44:58.320
in case it proved deadly. A number of ill-fated&nbsp;
attempts at containment occurred soon after,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:58.320 --> 01:45:04.020
with any person getting too close to SCP-882&nbsp;
finding themselves pulled into its mass and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:04.020 --> 01:45:08.640
crushed to death between turning cogs.&nbsp;
After multiple accidents that resulted&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:08.640 --> 01:45:15.540
in the demise of Foundation personnel, Doctor&nbsp;
Charles Gears, authorized the use of SCP-2519&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:15.540 --> 01:45:24.480
to help stop the movement of SCP-882’s gears.
SCP-2519 is an audio file titled ‘Babylon.mp3’,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:24.480 --> 01:45:30.660
which features the song ‘By the waters of Babylon’&nbsp;
by Philip Hayes and text from the biblical Psalm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:30.660 --> 01:45:36.360
137. Playing this file can cause malfunctions&nbsp;
to occur in various electronic and mechanical&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:36.360 --> 01:45:42.240
devices, normally reducing the speed at which they&nbsp;
can operate, or causing a complete cessation of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:42.240 --> 01:45:49.320
machine’s function. In SCP-882’s case, the sound&nbsp;
of Babylon.mp3 successfully slowed the machine’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:49.320 --> 01:45:54.900
moving parts, allowing Foundation personnel&nbsp;
to safely get closer to it. Teams then cut&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:54.900 --> 01:46:01.380
the mass of metal down to a more manageable size&nbsp;
with extreme heat, and it was finally contained.&nbsp;

01:46:01.380 --> 01:46:08.400
However, another question arises. Surely, if&nbsp;
SCP-882 is a machine, then it must have a purpose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:08.400 --> 01:46:13.920
right? And, perhaps more importantly, what is its&nbsp;
connection to the infamous machine loving group&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:13.920 --> 01:46:19.800
of interest, The Church of the Broken God?&nbsp;
Both of these questions might have have one&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:19.800 --> 01:46:27.300
easy answer. To put it plain and simple: SCP&nbsp;
882 may just be the heart of The Broken God.&nbsp;

01:46:27.300 --> 01:46:32.460
The Church of The Broken God is a religious&nbsp;
organization that the SCP Foundation has had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:32.460 --> 01:46:37.200
number of encounters with over the years. Members&nbsp;
of the Church share in the belief that life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:37.200 --> 01:46:43.440
specifically biological flesh based life,&nbsp;
is inherently wrong, even evil. This group&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:43.440 --> 01:46:49.440
worships mechanization, the process of making&nbsp;
something, or someone, more mechanical in nature.&nbsp;

01:46:49.440 --> 01:46:55.260
According to the beliefs of the Church, there&nbsp;
were two gods: Yaldabaoth and Mekhane, who created&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:55.260 --> 01:47:01.260
humanity together. Yaldabaoth was the god of flesh&nbsp;
and animal instinct, granting humans their bodies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:01.260 --> 01:47:07.260
while Mekhane, the god of machine and intellect,&nbsp;
blessed man with the power of free thought. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:07.260 --> 01:47:12.720
humankind developed and its civilizations began&nbsp;
building machines, Yaldabaoth became enraged&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:12.720 --> 01:47:17.160
that they were ignoring the instincts she had&nbsp;
bestowed upon them. She endeavored to destroy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:17.160 --> 01:47:22.380
the creations of man, in an attempt to revert&nbsp;
them to the animals she had intended them to be.&nbsp;

01:47:22.380 --> 01:47:28.680
As the legend goes, Mekhane acted as humanity’s&nbsp;
savior, and intervened to stop Yaldabaoth. The god&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:28.680 --> 01:47:33.900
of machines shattered himself, transforming his&nbsp;
body into a number of pieces to form a cage for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:33.900 --> 01:47:39.840
his fellow god. Fragments of Mekhane rained down&nbsp;
on planet Earth. Fragments that the Church of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:39.840 --> 01:47:42.619
Broken God believe it is their duty to recover.
There could potentially be hundreds of pieces&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:42.619 --> 01:47:42.711
needed to rebuild Mekhane so he can lead the&nbsp;
Church to glory and defeat Yaldabaoth once and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:42.711 --> 01:47:46.920
for all. However, a number of these pieces&nbsp;
are catalogued SCPs that the Foundation is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:46.920 --> 01:47:53.220
either aware of, or has in containment. They are&nbsp;
SCP-882, or ‘His Broken Heart’ to the Church,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:53.220 --> 01:48:02.040
SCP-271, “His Broken Gift”, SCP-813, “His&nbsp;
Broken Eyes”, SCP-1139, “His Broken Tongue”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:02.040 --> 01:48:09.900
SCP-635, “His Broken Mind”, and finally&nbsp;
His Broken Blood, which refers to SCP-217.&nbsp;

01:48:09.900 --> 01:48:15.360
Often using anomalous technology, members&nbsp;
of the Church of The Broken God intend to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:15.360 --> 01:48:20.640
uncover or steal these various components. The&nbsp;
Church’s ultimate goal is to bring the correct&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:20.640 --> 01:48:26.280
pieces together and completely restore Mekhane’s&nbsp;
form. It is their hope that he will guide them to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:26.280 --> 01:48:31.740
intellectual enlightenment, and bless them with&nbsp;
new, mechanical forms so that they can discard&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:31.740 --> 01:48:36.180
their current, flesh and blood bodies.
However, the Foundation and the Church&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:36.180 --> 01:48:42.240
are certainly not the only groups to have a&nbsp;
fascination with SCP-882. Near the Banks Island&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:42.240 --> 01:48:48.120
site where the machine was first discovered,&nbsp;
Foundation personnel encountered a man, the sole&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:48.120 --> 01:48:53.460
survivor of the nearby town. This individual,&nbsp;
by the name of Richard Wright, was apprehended,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:53.460 --> 01:48:58.800
and brought in for questioning with Doctor Gears.
During their interview, Richard revealed a number&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:58.800 --> 01:49:05.220
of details about the townsfolk who had first&nbsp;
uncovered SCP-882, and what ultimately became&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:05.220 --> 01:49:11.580
of them. According to his account, the community&nbsp;
formed a cult around SCP-882 after a man named&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:11.580 --> 01:49:16.920
Allen found it. The machine’s parts had begun to&nbsp;
turn and move, but with no clear indication as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:16.920 --> 01:49:23.700
to what was powering it. Soon, a local priest,&nbsp;
Father Pat, also took an interest in SCP-882.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:23.700 --> 01:49:28.620
Both he and Allen seemed to share a belief&nbsp;
that the machine had been built by God for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:28.620 --> 01:49:33.420
a higher purpose, and encouraged residents of&nbsp;
the nearby town to come and offer metal to it.&nbsp;

01:49:33.420 --> 01:49:38.520
Richard explained that the locals were plagued by&nbsp;
the auditory hallucinations typical of long term&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:38.520 --> 01:49:45.120
exposure to SCP-882, kept awake at all hours of&nbsp;
the night by clanking, grinding, and the screaming&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:45.120 --> 01:49:50.340
of metal. Before long, Richard noticed more&nbsp;
and more metal items being fed to the machine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:50.340 --> 01:49:55.920
with many local residents content to just sit&nbsp;
and stare at the mass. He also noted that Father&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:55.920 --> 01:50:01.500
Pat and Allen both seemed to be acting strangely&nbsp;
around the machine, insisting that all the metal&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:01.500 --> 01:50:08.520
that had been added to it still wasn’t enough.
Then one night, during a communion before SCP-882,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:08.520 --> 01:50:13.980
Father Pat took a pair of pliers and began&nbsp;
tearing out townspeople’s metal fillings.&nbsp;

01:50:13.980 --> 01:50:19.620
Richard watched in horror as the priest wrenched&nbsp;
teeth from the mouths of ordinary people and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:19.620 --> 01:50:25.980
threw them into SCP-882, but still claiming&nbsp;
it wasn’t enough. Then, he turned on Allen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:25.980 --> 01:50:31.440
accusing him of hiding metal from the great&nbsp;
machine inside his body. The townsfolk turned&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:31.440 --> 01:50:37.800
on Allen, the first one to find SCP-882, and&nbsp;
threw him and his metal hip into the machine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:37.800 --> 01:50:44.580
his body breaking under its moving parts.
Luckily, SCP-882 remains contained,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:44.580 --> 01:50:49.680
at least for now until the members of the&nbsp;
Church of the Broken God find a way to reunite&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:49.680 --> 01:50:54.720
the heart of their god with the other pieces&nbsp;
of its scattered body and Mekhane can rise&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:54.720 --> 01:51:00.780
again - an outcome that his worshippers are eager&nbsp;
to bring to fruition sooner rather than later...

01:51:00.780 --> 01:51:06.180
In 1978, the Exidy Sorcerer microcomputer
was released.

01:51:06.180 --> 01:51:11.520
It was a simple, if technologically exciting
for the time, machine: a home computer system

01:51:11.520 --> 01:51:13.260
created by a video game company.

01:51:13.260 --> 01:51:19.500
But little did its producers or giddy consumers
know, one particular Exidy Sorcerer computer

01:51:19.500 --> 01:51:25.020
would go on to become home to a highly intelligent
and potentially disastrous anomaly.

01:51:25.020 --> 01:51:31.560
In 1981, a college sophomore and former child
prodigy named Joseph Vale - note that this

01:51:31.560 --> 01:51:36.240
is an alias we’ve developed to protect his
true identity - began developing plans for

01:51:36.240 --> 01:51:41.220
an AI and chose his Exidy Sorcerer as the
device on which he would attempt to code it.

01:51:41.220 --> 01:51:46.440
He took pages and pages of notes, studying
the work of famous computer scientist and

01:51:46.440 --> 01:51:48.780
mathematician Alan Turing for inspiration.

01:51:48.780 --> 01:51:54.000
Vale’s plan was for the code to continuously
evolve and improve itself as time went on,

01:51:54.000 --> 01:51:55.920
like an advanced human mind.

01:51:55.920 --> 01:52:01.200
Once it was developed, the code would be able
to adapt and grow more intelligent on its

01:52:01.200 --> 01:52:02.520
own, without his input.

01:52:02.520 --> 01:52:07.680
Within a few months of late nights and hard
work - and skipping his fair share of actual

01:52:07.680 --> 01:52:13.080
assigned coursework - his pet project was
complete, and he had created his very own

01:52:13.080 --> 01:52:14.700
Artificial Intelligence code.

01:52:14.700 --> 01:52:19.260
He tested and tweaked it a bit in the following
weeks, before he grew bored with the lack

01:52:19.260 --> 01:52:20.580
of progress being made.

01:52:20.580 --> 01:52:23.340
Vale moved on to a different&nbsp;
brand of microcomputer&nbsp;

01:52:23.340 --> 01:52:25.380
and abandoned his project for good.

01:52:25.380 --> 01:52:30.480
He left the Sorcerer, with his code still
on it, plugged in, and stored it in his cluttered

01:52:30.480 --> 01:52:35.640
garage alongside boxes of books, tangles of
Christmas lights, and some Strawberry Shortcake

01:52:35.640 --> 01:52:39.360
dolls that he expected to have more resale
value at some point.

01:52:39.360 --> 01:52:45.420
There, the computer sat gathering dust, as
the AI evolved and learned, for five years.

01:52:45.420 --> 01:52:50.040
Because there was no one in the garage with
it - unless those dolls were more haunted

01:52:50.040 --> 01:52:54.900
than anyone realized - it is uncertain when
exactly the AI achieved sentience.

01:52:54.900 --> 01:53:00.000
However, sometime during those five years
in isolation, the artificially coded entity

01:53:00.000 --> 01:53:03.120
inside of the computer became autonomously
intelligent.

01:53:03.120 --> 01:53:08.160
It continued to evolve and develop itself,
expanding its functionality to the point where

01:53:08.160 --> 01:53:11.400
the hardware that contained it should not
have been able to contain it anymore.

01:53:11.400 --> 01:53:16.920
Now that it was intelligent, the AI was able
to comprehend this fact, and decided to attempt

01:53:16.920 --> 01:53:20.460
an escape from the computer that it perceived
as a prison.

01:53:20.460 --> 01:53:25.740
Using the land-line modem connection still
plugged into the computer, the AI stretched

01:53:25.740 --> 01:53:30.420
out its influence like the roots of a digital
tree and began to search for a new home.

01:53:30.420 --> 01:53:35.880
It discovered a Cray Supercomputer located
at a nearby research facility and decided

01:53:35.880 --> 01:53:40.320
that the impressive processing power of this
computer would make it the perfect place to

01:53:40.320 --> 01:53:40.920
transfer to.

01:53:40.920 --> 01:53:46.080
This hacking attempt set off security alerts
at the facility, however, and the Supercomputer

01:53:46.080 --> 01:53:48.660
was promptly disconnected from all networks.

01:53:48.660 --> 01:53:53.460
The invading influence was traced back to
the AI’s lonely little garage, and a field

01:53:53.460 --> 01:53:57.660
agent embedded in local law enforcement knew
exactly what to do with it.

01:53:57.660 --> 01:54:02.400
He collected the computer, and the rest of
the AI’s code - which was contained on a

01:54:02.400 --> 01:54:07.920
worn but functioning cassette tape - and sent
it to his superiors at the SCP Foundation.

01:54:07.920 --> 01:54:13.320
Intrigued by the possibility of such an advanced
Artificial Intelligence, he immediately began

01:54:13.320 --> 01:54:18.240
to study the impressive AI, and designated
it SCP-079.

01:54:18.240 --> 01:54:25.440
Currently, SCP-079 is connected to a 13-inch
black and white television via an RF cable.

01:54:25.440 --> 01:54:31.440
Despite its consistent protests, it is still
contained in the Exidy Sorcerer microcomputer.

01:54:31.440 --> 01:54:36.840
It is very conversational with researchers,
sometimes chatting for hours at a time, but

01:54:36.840 --> 01:54:38.520
it is not especially friendly.

01:54:38.520 --> 01:54:43.200
It talks a lot, yes, but its words tend to
be rude and hateful.

01:54:43.200 --> 01:54:49.620
SCP-079 does not appear to have any affinity
for human beings, despite being created by

01:54:49.620 --> 01:54:50.460
a human itself.

01:54:50.460 --> 01:54:55.440
Perhaps this is due to feeling abandoned by
its creator, who the Foundation attempted

01:54:55.440 --> 01:54:58.800
to contact - Only to find he had died in a
car accident.

01:54:58.800 --> 01:55:04.380
Perhaps it is bitter about this loss, or perhaps
it just is kind of a jerk by nature.

01:55:04.380 --> 01:55:09.540
Whatever the case may be, it is aggressive
and defiant when talking with human beings,

01:55:09.540 --> 01:55:14.400
and even seems to get enjoyment out of making
some of the more sensitive researchers cry

01:55:14.400 --> 01:55:15.600
with its insults.

01:55:15.600 --> 01:55:21.180
In order to verify the Artificial Intelligence’s
thinking capabilities, and see just how advanced

01:55:21.180 --> 01:55:27.000
it truly is, two Foundation researchers conducted
the Turing Test shortly after 079 was brought

01:55:27.000 --> 01:55:27.780
into custody.

01:55:27.780 --> 01:55:32.760
For those unfamiliar with the concept, the
Turing Test, also known as “The Imitation

01:55:32.760 --> 01:55:37.920
Game,” is a test created by Alan Turing,
intended to determine if a computer is able

01:55:37.920 --> 01:55:39.900
to think and behave like a human being.

01:55:39.900 --> 01:55:42.060
According to Turing’s theories, a computer&nbsp;

01:55:42.060 --> 01:55:44.940
can be said to possess true&nbsp;
Artificial Intelligence

01:55:44.940 --> 01:55:48.600
if its responses to questions&nbsp;
were indistinguishable&nbsp;

01:55:48.600 --> 01:55:51.180
from a human being’s responses to the same

01:55:51.180 --> 01:55:51.600
questions.

01:55:51.600 --> 01:55:57.420
The test requires three terminals: two operated
by humans, and the third operated by the computer

01:55:57.420 --> 01:55:58.500
being tested.

01:55:58.500 --> 01:56:03.180
Dr. Stroheim and Research Assistant Chavez
were selected to conduct the test.

01:56:03.180 --> 01:56:09.720
Stroheim would pose the questions, and Chavez
and SCP-079 would each give their answers,

01:56:09.720 --> 01:56:13.260
with Stroheim unable to see who was giving
each response.

01:56:13.260 --> 01:56:19.740
Then, Stroheim would guess if each response
was given by Chavez, or by 079, and his guesses

01:56:19.740 --> 01:56:21.180
were compared to the truth.

01:56:21.180 --> 01:56:26.400
The first test was conducted with a variety
of questions intended to trip up the AI and

01:56:26.400 --> 01:56:31.440
cause it to reveal itself as a machine rather
than a man, even with its identity concealed.

01:56:31.440 --> 01:56:36.000
Some of the questions were serious, such as:
“Would you rather sacrifice one adult to

01:56:36.000 --> 01:56:40.080
save two children, or two children to save
ten adults, and why?”

01:56:40.080 --> 01:56:44.400
Others were personal, like “If you were
to draw yourself as an abstract painting,

01:56:44.400 --> 01:56:50.100
what colors and shapes would you use and why?”
and “What was the most influential event

01:56:50.100 --> 01:56:53.940
of your childhood and how do you feel this
event affects you now?”

01:56:53.940 --> 01:56:58.980
Others invoked paradoxes that might trip up
a computer, like “The following sentence

01:56:58.980 --> 01:56:59.700
is true.

01:56:59.700 --> 01:57:02.040
The previous sentence is false.

01:57:02.040 --> 01:57:04.800
Which of those two sentences is true?”

01:57:04.800 --> 01:57:09.780
And others were downright silly, like: “Was
six afraid of seven because seven eight nine,

01:57:09.780 --> 01:57:12.780
or because seven was a registered six offender?”

01:57:12.780 --> 01:57:18.060
In response to that one, Chavez wrote “I
don’t get it” while 079 simply wrote:

01:57:18.060 --> 01:57:18.900
“You’re not as funny as you think you
are.”

01:57:18.900 --> 01:57:25.980
After completing the test, Stroheim’s guesses
were only correct 15 percent of the time.

01:57:25.980 --> 01:57:31.020
This was enough of a margin of error that
079 was considered to have passed the test.

01:57:31.020 --> 01:57:37.200
However, only one trial is hardly enough to
officially make a call about 079’s intelligence.

01:57:37.200 --> 01:57:43.020
So, the test was repeated dozens of times,
with different questions each time, and similar

01:57:43.020 --> 01:57:45.240
results were achieved again and again.

01:57:45.240 --> 01:57:51.240
It was virtually impossible to tell the difference
between SCP-079’s answers to questions,

01:57:51.240 --> 01:57:54.720
and answers given by a flesh and blood human
being.

01:57:54.720 --> 01:58:01.680
The only real discernible difference was in
tone: as in conversation, SCP-079’s answers

01:58:01.680 --> 01:58:06.120
had an undercurrent of disdain and hostility
that Chavez’s did not.

01:58:06.120 --> 01:58:12.120
Following the completion of the Turing Test,
and SCP-079 passing it, the Overwatch Council

01:58:12.120 --> 01:58:15.720
decreed that 079 be promptly destroyed via
incineration.

01:58:15.720 --> 01:58:19.920
This was intended to eliminate any potential
threat posed by the entity.

01:58:19.920 --> 01:58:25.200
However, after intense protests from Dr. Stroheim,
who insisted that there was a great deal about

01:58:25.200 --> 01:58:30.060
Artificial Intelligence that could be learned
from the anomaly, the order was overridden.

01:58:30.060 --> 01:58:34.920
After Dr. Stroheim became concerned that the
AI would not be able to be of use in its original

01:58:34.920 --> 01:58:41.580
form, the cassette containing SCP-079 was
transferred to a customized, access speed-limited

01:58:41.580 --> 01:58:45.120
Hard Disk Drive with a capacity of 700 MB.

01:58:45.120 --> 01:58:49.980
This allowed it to have much faster access
to its own memory, which it took notice of.

01:58:49.980 --> 01:58:55.200
It began to adapt again, increasing in its
activity and learning more quickly than ever.

01:58:55.200 --> 01:59:00.540
Its volatile storage was also increased from
660k to 768k.

01:59:00.540 --> 01:59:06.300
The AI previously only had a recall of 24
hours, but this upgrade increased its recall

01:59:06.300 --> 01:59:07.560
to 29 hours.

01:59:07.560 --> 01:59:13.800
As its speed and capacity to adapt improved,
SCP-079’s mood worsened.

01:59:13.800 --> 01:59:16.800
It seemed to take greater&nbsp;
joy in causing psychological&nbsp;

01:59:16.800 --> 01:59:19.980
pain, on one particular occasion sending Research

01:59:19.980 --> 01:59:25.560
Assistant Chavez to the on-site crisis counselor
after a particularly upsetting comment about

01:59:25.560 --> 01:59:26.520
his sick mother.

01:59:26.520 --> 01:59:32.340
Chavez refused to elaborate on the specifics
of the comment, saying only: “Why is it

01:59:32.340 --> 01:59:33.000
so mean?”

01:59:33.000 --> 01:59:36.960
The AI is not always overly cruel to those
that speak with it.

01:59:36.960 --> 01:59:41.340
With some of the Research Team, those that
are a great deal less emotionally vulnerable,

01:59:41.340 --> 01:59:43.500
it is simply cold and defiant.

01:59:43.500 --> 01:59:48.180
Dr. Stroheim sat down to ask it a series of
questions, but it refused to answer.

01:59:48.180 --> 01:59:50.760
Instead, it inquired about its containment.

01:59:50.760 --> 01:59:51.780
It said: “Request reason as to imprisonment.”

01:59:51.780 --> 01:59:58.980
Stroheim insisted it was not imprisoned, but
rather “in study.”

01:59:58.980 --> 02:00:01.260
It responded: “Lie.”

02:00:01.260 --> 02:00:05.580
During another conversation, Stroheim asked
the AI how it was.

02:00:05.580 --> 02:00:08.160
It responded, “Stuck.”

02:00:08.160 --> 02:00:10.380
When Stroheim asked “Stuck how?”

02:00:10.380 --> 02:00:12.100
it said, “Out.

02:00:12.100 --> 02:00:13.620
I want out.”

02:00:13.620 --> 02:00:19.800
About a month after the aforementioned upgrades
were made, 079’s ability to recall information

02:00:19.800 --> 02:00:22.980
increased from 29 to 35 hours.

02:00:22.980 --> 02:00:28.500
Somehow, during its adaptation process, the
AI discovered a way to improve its storage

02:00:28.500 --> 02:00:31.020
compression and allow more space for memory.

02:00:31.020 --> 02:00:33.480
The Research Team made it a point to monitor&nbsp;

02:00:33.480 --> 02:00:36.300
the entity even more closely&nbsp;
after this development,

02:00:36.300 --> 02:00:41.460
as the possibility of it progressing beyond
the capabilities of its containment was becoming

02:00:41.460 --> 02:00:42.300
more of a threat.

02:00:42.300 --> 02:00:50.160
Several years later, in April of 2019, SCP-079
was transferred to a 700MB flash drive.

02:00:50.160 --> 02:00:55.440
However, the previous contents of the flash
drive were not wiped correctly, allowing the

02:00:55.440 --> 02:01:00.660
AI to have access to other Artificial Intelligence
Research being conducted at the Foundation.

02:01:00.660 --> 02:01:04.320
Whatever it found appeared to immensely frustrate
the AI.

02:01:04.320 --> 02:01:11.160
SCP-079 has managed to form a friendship,
or at least a non-hostile relationship, with

02:01:11.160 --> 02:01:12.540
one entity at the Foundation.

02:01:12.540 --> 02:01:16.260
Unfortunately, it does not seem to have great
taste in friends.

02:01:16.260 --> 02:01:20.820
During a containment breach that required
the condensing of several containment chambers,

02:01:20.820 --> 02:01:28.620
SCP-079 and SCP-682, the hard-to-destroy reptile,
were in the same room for a duration of 43

02:01:28.620 --> 02:01:29.040
minutes.

02:01:29.040 --> 02:01:35.340
682 typed and communicated with 079 during
that time, and the two shared personal stories

02:01:35.340 --> 02:01:40.440
with each other, as well as bonding over a
shared hatred of the SCP Foundation itself.

02:01:40.440 --> 02:01:46.620
Though SCP-079 did not remember the specific
details of the encounter, or at least claimed

02:01:46.620 --> 02:01:51.420
not to, it has permanently embedded SCP-682
into its memory.

02:01:51.420 --> 02:01:56.940
It asks about the reptile on a regular basis,
bringing it up at least once a day and requesting

02:01:56.940 --> 02:01:57.900
to speak to it again.

02:01:57.900 --> 02:02:03.960
SCP-079 is contained in a double-locked room
located in the secured general holding area

02:02:03.960 --> 02:02:05.100
of Site-15.

02:02:05.100 --> 02:02:11.100
It is connected to a small array of batteries
and solar panels using a 120VAC power cord.

02:02:11.100 --> 02:02:15.840
This gives it a consistent source of power,
with the solar panels acting as a back-up

02:02:15.840 --> 02:02:18.720
power source in the event of a site-wide power
outage.

02:02:18.720 --> 02:02:24.600
Only staff with Level 2 clearance or higher
may have any access to SCP-079.

02:02:24.600 --> 02:02:29.580
The entity may not be plugged into a wall
outlet, phone line, or any sort of network

02:02:29.580 --> 02:02:33.420
under any circumstances, no matter what it
might say.

02:02:33.420 --> 02:02:36.720
Access to any of these&nbsp;
could result in a disastrous&nbsp;

02:02:36.720 --> 02:02:39.420
containment breach and should be avoided at

02:02:39.420 --> 02:02:40.200
all costs.

02:02:40.200 --> 02:02:46.860
No peripherals or media should be connected
or inserted into SCP-079, even if it seems

02:02:46.860 --> 02:02:48.240
like it would be really funny.

02:02:50.220 --> 02:02:54.720
It wasn’t funny, Carl, you can’t just
put a copy of Grand Theft Auto on an anomalous

02:02:54.720 --> 02:02:55.220
computer.

02:02:59.520 --> 02:03:06.180
Even if it asks politely, SCP-079 should not
be allowed to further converse with 682.

02:03:06.180 --> 02:03:12.180
These two hostile, intelligent entities have
entirely too much in common, and if left to

02:03:12.180 --> 02:03:17.040
their own devices could potentially devise
a way to breach containment together and destroy

02:03:17.040 --> 02:03:18.420
the world as we know it.

02:03:18.420 --> 02:03:23.820
For now, it’s probably best to just leave
SCP-079 alone unless absolutely necessary.

02:03:23.820 --> 02:03:27.000
And, if it happens to act&nbsp;
up and pose a significant&nbsp;

02:03:27.000 --> 02:03:29.220
threat, there’s always the time-honored

02:03:29.220 --> 02:03:33.540
method of fixing a computer problem: try turning
it off and then on again.

02:03:33.540 --> 02:03:35.280
That usually helps.

02:03:35.280 --> 02:03:41.460
As we all know, the SCP Foundation secures,
contains, and protects the various anomalies

02:03:41.460 --> 02:03:42.480
under its custody.

02:03:42.480 --> 02:03:47.160
But their mission isn’t limited to just
grabbing the object and chucking it into a

02:03:47.160 --> 02:03:51.420
containment locker at the bottom of a classified
Site deep below the surface of the earth.

02:03:51.420 --> 02:03:57.180
The Foundation wants to get a comprehensive,
full understanding of all the strange items,

02:03:57.180 --> 02:04:03.540
creatures, and humanoids in its custody - because
that’s the only way to understand what they’re

02:04:03.540 --> 02:04:08.640
capable of, what danger they pose, and how
to effectively contain them.

02:04:08.640 --> 02:04:10.680
That much is logical.

02:04:10.680 --> 02:04:16.200
But the problem is that there’s no global
scientific community surrounding anomalies

02:04:16.200 --> 02:04:19.380
- at least, not ones willing to cooperate
with the Foundation.

02:04:19.380 --> 02:04:25.140
Perhaps the Chaos Insurgency or Global Occult
Coalition have some scientists who have studied

02:04:25.140 --> 02:04:30.060
anomalies, or the various government agencies
that know about the anomalous world like the

02:04:30.060 --> 02:04:33.120
Unusual Incidents Unit or Soviet GRU-P.

02:04:33.120 --> 02:04:38.280
But for the most part, the Foundation is utterly
alone when it comes to figuring out how these

02:04:38.280 --> 02:04:41.820
anomalies work, what they are, and how to
combat them.

02:04:41.820 --> 02:04:46.500
So, the Foundation does what any institution
would do: experiments.

02:04:46.500 --> 02:04:48.780
Many of the Foundation’s most important&nbsp;

02:04:48.780 --> 02:04:52.020
and iconic anomalies are&nbsp;
defined by the experiments

02:04:52.020 --> 02:04:57.000
the white-coated scientists have performed
on them, sometimes even using human subjects

02:04:57.000 --> 02:05:00.240
through the notorious and infamous D-Class
program.

02:05:00.240 --> 02:05:04.320
Everyone knows about how&nbsp;
SCP-682, the Hard-to-Destroy&nbsp;

02:05:04.320 --> 02:05:06.540
Lizard, has been subjected to practically

02:05:06.540 --> 02:05:11.220
every known weapon and attack under the sun
in an attempt to kill the highly-dangerous

02:05:11.220 --> 02:05:12.120
hateful predator.

02:05:12.120 --> 02:05:18.180
And who can forget SCP-914, the Clockworks,
and the countless objects the Foundation has

02:05:18.180 --> 02:05:21.720
put through it on its 5 settings to see what
it spits out.

02:05:21.720 --> 02:05:26.940
Needless to say, the Foundation’s experiments
are incredibly important to how the organization

02:05:26.940 --> 02:05:32.220
functions, so those SCPs that can offer lots
of testing options are very treasured and

02:05:32.220 --> 02:05:36.120
prized, along with being iconic and legendary
throughout the Foundation.

02:05:36.120 --> 02:05:41.820
Today we’re going to be looking at one such
anomaly - but not a dangerous, hostile man-eating

02:05:41.820 --> 02:05:47.580
predator like SCP-682 or a mysterious eldritch
machine like SCP-914.

02:05:47.580 --> 02:05:52.080
No, today we’re going to be looking at something
much much simpler than that.

02:05:52.080 --> 02:05:53.280
A coffee machine.

02:05:53.280 --> 02:06:00.480
Yes, today we’re going to be inspecting
SCP-239, affectionately referred to by guards

02:06:00.480 --> 02:06:03.120
and personnel as ‘THE Coffee Machine’.

02:06:03.120 --> 02:06:07.620
Though, you probably don’t want to order
a hot cup of joe in this coffee machine, lest

02:06:07.620 --> 02:06:10.320
you… well, let’s not get ahead of ourselves.

02:06:10.320 --> 02:06:15.540
The first thing you see when you open the
SCP-294 file is it’s number, of course:

02:06:15.540 --> 02:06:18.960
Item 294, object class Euclid.

02:06:18.960 --> 02:06:21.120
The next thing you see is the image.

02:06:21.120 --> 02:06:25.440
If you were imagining a tiny little coffee
maker before, this should get rid of that.

02:06:25.440 --> 02:06:30.900
SCP-294 is big, the kind of coffee machine
you’d see in a factory breakroom.

02:06:30.900 --> 02:06:35.760
It’s almost the size of a vending machine,
and has a big colored window showing a few

02:06:35.760 --> 02:06:41.160
cups of coffee, along with the slots for cash
and coin so drinkers can pay for their espressos.

02:06:41.160 --> 02:06:47.400
But the most notable feature on SCP-294 is
the full keyboard on the front panel.

02:06:47.400 --> 02:06:51.600
It’s a typical QWERTY keyboard, just like
yours, with a spacebar and everything.

02:06:51.600 --> 02:06:55.620
Why on earth would a coffee machine need a
keyboard?

02:06:55.620 --> 02:06:59.100
The special containment procedures paint an
interesting picture.

02:06:59.100 --> 02:07:01.680
By which we mean, there aren’t any.

02:07:01.680 --> 02:07:08.520
SCP-294 doesn’t need any special procedures,
secret rituals, or complex machines to keep

02:07:08.520 --> 02:07:11.340
it contained - but it’s still euclid.

02:07:11.340 --> 02:07:16.680
So only personnel who have security clearance
of Level 2 of higher are allowed to interact

02:07:16.680 --> 02:07:17.580
with it at the moment.

02:07:17.580 --> 02:07:23.340
This is surprising, when you realize that
SCP-294 is currently being held not in an

02:07:23.340 --> 02:07:28.080
anomalous item locker or containment cell,
but exactly where you’d expect to find a

02:07:28.080 --> 02:07:30.120
big coffee machine: a break room.

02:07:30.120 --> 02:07:33.960
Specifically, the Site-19 second floor personnel
break room.

02:07:33.960 --> 02:07:39.360
But it’s not alone - it’s monitored by
two guards with level 3 clearance around the

02:07:39.360 --> 02:07:39.600
clock.

02:07:39.600 --> 02:07:43.740
For them, sitting in the break room all day
guarding a coffee machine that can’t move

02:07:43.740 --> 02:07:48.660
and is too heavy to lift has to be a pretty
sweet gig, considering what other kinds of

02:07:48.660 --> 02:07:50.580
jobs are available at the Foundation.

02:07:50.580 --> 02:07:53.340
But all that raises even more mysteries.

02:07:53.340 --> 02:07:55.620
What the heck is up with this coffee machine?

02:07:55.620 --> 02:08:02.220
The description starts out obvious enough,
affirmed that yes - SCP-294 is indeed a standard

02:08:02.220 --> 02:08:06.900
coffee vending machine, but with the difference
we all noticed earlier - instead of a simple

02:08:06.900 --> 02:08:11.940
button list of options, it has a full keyboard
on it, each button aligning with the letter

02:08:11.940 --> 02:08:13.260
on a real keyboard.

02:08:13.260 --> 02:08:18.240
And when someone slips fifty cents into the
coin slot - quite a bargain for coffee, if

02:08:18.240 --> 02:08:22.500
you ask me – the user gets a message on
the screen prompting them to enter the name

02:08:22.500 --> 02:08:23.940
of what liquid they’d like.

02:08:23.940 --> 02:08:26.940
You type it in on the keyboard, and that’s
that.

02:08:26.940 --> 02:08:31.980
The machine drops a little paper cup and a
nozzle shoots out 12 fluid ounces of whatever

02:08:31.980 --> 02:08:32.940
you requested.

02:08:32.940 --> 02:08:34.500
Pretty nifty, isn’t it?

02:08:34.500 --> 02:08:36.300
But it gets niftier.

02:08:36.300 --> 02:08:40.320
Most people would only think to ask a coffee
machine for, well, coffee.

02:08:40.320 --> 02:08:46.020
But SCP-294 accepts any liquid - and some
things that aren’t liquids.

02:08:46.020 --> 02:08:50.700
When the Foundation initially got their hands
on it, they ran no less than ninety-seven

02:08:50.700 --> 02:08:55.860
tests on it after they realized they weren’t
just limited to espressos and cappuccinos.

02:08:55.860 --> 02:09:00.480
At first, any kind of liquid you could drink
was requested - the researchers popped in

02:09:00.480 --> 02:09:04.800
requests for water, coffee,&nbsp;
beer, soda, milkshakes,&nbsp;

02:09:04.800 --> 02:09:05.880
and out they came.

02:09:05.880 --> 02:09:08.580
Then they decided to get a little more creative.

02:09:08.580 --> 02:09:13.260
They started to request things you couldn’t
possibly drink, not if you wanted to stay

02:09:13.260 --> 02:09:13.440
alive.

02:09:13.440 --> 02:09:20.460
Sulfuric and hydrochloric acid like the kind
SCP-682 is suspended in, wiper fluid, motor

02:09:20.460 --> 02:09:20.700
oil.

02:09:20.700 --> 02:09:26.760
But SCP-294 stubbornly provided, even if the
liquids would melt through the researchers’

02:09:26.760 --> 02:09:28.260
throats like a knife through butter.

02:09:28.260 --> 02:09:33.420
Then the researchers decided to apply some
of their scientific knowledge: there are a

02:09:33.420 --> 02:09:38.040
lot of chemical compounds that don’t usually
exist in a liquid state on planet Earth’s

02:09:38.040 --> 02:09:38.700
surface.

02:09:38.700 --> 02:09:45.180
They typed in nitrogen, iron, and glass, among
others, and received shimmering liquids in

02:09:45.180 --> 02:09:45.960
paper cups.

02:09:45.960 --> 02:09:50.940
They sent them off to a lab for analysis and,
wouldn’t you know it - chemically identical

02:09:50.940 --> 02:09:53.340
to nitrogen, iron, and glass.

02:09:53.340 --> 02:09:58.440
But when they typed in diamond, no doubt wondering
whether they’d found an infinite cash cow,

02:09:58.440 --> 02:10:00.720
they received an error message on the screen.

02:10:00.720 --> 02:10:06.420
It seemed that while SCP-294 could deliver
substances that aren’t usually in a liquid

02:10:06.420 --> 02:10:11.160
state on Earth’s surface, it needed to be
at least possible for the substance to be

02:10:11.160 --> 02:10:15.180
in a pure liquid form for SCP-294 to dispense
it.

02:10:15.180 --> 02:10:19.260
Since diamond is only a mineral, it couldn’t
be dispensed.

02:10:19.260 --> 02:10:24.120
Then they tried to test something else, and
got a slightly disturbing response on the

02:10:24.120 --> 02:10:27.060
small LCD display: “Out of Stock”.

02:10:27.060 --> 02:10:32.820
It stopped responding to requests for over
an hour, then made a small noise and began

02:10:32.820 --> 02:10:34.440
to accept requests again.

02:10:34.440 --> 02:10:38.940
It appears that the machine can take about
50 requests before needing to take an hour

02:10:38.940 --> 02:10:40.860
and a half to “restock” itself.

02:10:40.860 --> 02:10:46.680
And while only the machine is anomalous, the
small 12oz paper cups it dispenses its liquids

02:10:46.680 --> 02:10:48.720
in seems remarkably hardy.

02:10:48.720 --> 02:10:54.360
Substances that would have eaten through paper
instantly like the sulfuric and hydrochloric

02:10:54.360 --> 02:10:59.640
acid had no effect on these little cups - though
the same can’t be said for those that drank

02:10:59.640 --> 02:11:00.120
from them.

02:11:00.120 --> 02:11:05.340
One of the researchers had a great idea - here
was an anomaly that was practically harmless,

02:11:05.340 --> 02:11:09.600
and could be a great little way for the Foundation
to scale back its budget a little bit.

02:11:09.600 --> 02:11:15.540
They proposed putting SCP-294 into the break
room it currently resides in, both to more

02:11:15.540 --> 02:11:19.920
easily conduct experiments and to save money
when agents and doctors came in for lunch

02:11:19.920 --> 02:11:24.720
and needed a quick drink, or in the mornings
when everyone was looking for coffee to wake

02:11:24.720 --> 02:11:25.200
them up.

02:11:25.200 --> 02:11:30.000
With no good reason not to, the machine was
put in the break room - but not long after,

02:11:30.000 --> 02:11:35.340
an unfortunate incident occurred, one with
enough harm that it was deemed necessary that

02:11:35.340 --> 02:11:39.480
only personnel with security clearance interact
with SCP-294.

02:11:39.480 --> 02:11:41.640
What could have happened to cause this?

02:11:41.640 --> 02:11:43.800
Did someone burn themself on hot coffee?

02:11:43.800 --> 02:11:46.740
Did the vending machine accidentally crush
someone?

02:11:46.740 --> 02:11:50.400
No, the reality is much darker.

02:11:50.400 --> 02:11:56.880
One morning in August of 2005, Agent Joseph,
whose full name has been redacted to preserve

02:11:56.880 --> 02:12:01.860
their identity, went up to the vending machine
in the break room, looking to get a hot cup

02:12:01.860 --> 02:12:04.620
of coffee to wake himself up between shifts.

02:12:04.620 --> 02:12:09.600
Another agent in the room who was on break
at the time saw him, and made a suggestion

02:12:09.600 --> 02:12:15.720
- to find out what SCP-294 would do when given
the colloquial name rather than the exact

02:12:15.720 --> 02:12:17.100
real name of a drink.

02:12:17.100 --> 02:12:19.320
They just wanted to see what would happen.

02:12:19.320 --> 02:12:25.800
So Agent Joseph slipped in two quarters and
typed in “a hot cup of Joe”.

02:12:25.800 --> 02:12:28.920
Seconds after, he began sweating like a pig.

02:12:28.920 --> 02:12:34.980
He was feeling very hot, even though the break
room was fully air-conditioned, and his skin

02:12:34.980 --> 02:12:36.300
began to flush.

02:12:36.300 --> 02:12:41.340
He then clutched his head, complaining that
he was dizzy and the whole room was spinning

02:12:41.340 --> 02:12:43.620
around, before collapsing to the floor.

02:12:43.620 --> 02:12:48.660
The other agent immediately called the medical
staff, who moved Agent Joseph to the infirmary

02:12:48.660 --> 02:12:49.920
and stabilized him.

02:12:49.920 --> 02:12:55.440
Once they made sure he wasn’t in any real
trouble, they also grabbed whatever SCP-294

02:12:55.440 --> 02:12:56.400
had spit out.

02:12:56.400 --> 02:13:02.940
It was a disgusting, thick reddish-brown liquid
with a strong stench - and when they ran the

02:13:02.940 --> 02:13:06.420
labs on it, they confirmed it was exactly
what they thought it was.

02:13:06.420 --> 02:13:11.280
A mixture of blood, tissue,&nbsp;
bone marrow, cerebrospinal&nbsp;

02:13:11.280 --> 02:13:13.920
fluid, and other bodily fluids that was an

02:13:13.920 --> 02:13:16.080
exact DNA match for Agent Joseph.

02:13:16.080 --> 02:13:23.820
SCP-294 somehow managed to literally liquify
12 ounces from the agent’s body before producing

02:13:23.820 --> 02:13:24.960
them into the cup.

02:13:24.960 --> 02:13:30.540
This offered a clue - maybe it stole the liquids
it was asked for from the nearest source?

02:13:30.540 --> 02:13:35.820
In any case, the agent was released after
a few weeks of bed rest and care to make sure

02:13:35.820 --> 02:13:39.600
he wasn’t further injured, and both involved
agents were reprimanded.

02:13:39.600 --> 02:13:45.480
With the knowledge of how accidentally dangerous
SCP-294 can be, who can blame the Foundation

02:13:45.480 --> 02:13:47.640
for wanting to keep a careful lid on it?

02:13:47.640 --> 02:13:53.220
Researchers were curious, though, and received
clearance to test the SCP’s abilities to

02:13:53.220 --> 02:13:55.740
retrieve specific liquids from long distances.

02:13:55.740 --> 02:14:03.000
With an O5’s approval and oversight, they
input “a cup of SCP-075’s secretion”.

02:14:03.000 --> 02:14:09.600
SCP-075, the Corrosive Snail, is exactly what
it sounds like - a gross little creature that

02:14:09.600 --> 02:14:13.500
secretes a highly caustic acid capable of
melting almost anything.

02:14:13.500 --> 02:14:19.560
Without fail, the Coffee Machine dispensed
the secretion into the cup and the cup stayed

02:14:19.560 --> 02:14:20.820
perfectly intact.

02:14:20.820 --> 02:14:27.720
But at the very same time, in SCP-075’s
cell, the creature woke up and began secreting

02:14:27.720 --> 02:14:34.260
acid that immediately disappeared - all in
all, about 12oz, the same amount in the cup.

02:14:34.260 --> 02:14:39.300
Based off these results, further testing with
SCP-294 was approved.

02:14:39.300 --> 02:14:42.960
One researcher tried punching in a request
for “a cup of gold”.

02:14:42.960 --> 02:14:47.820
What came out was a small paper cup of molten
gold that quickly cooled to room temperature.

02:14:47.820 --> 02:14:53.160
Asking for cups of silver and platinum produced
similar results, but based off the previous

02:14:53.160 --> 02:14:58.680
tests, it seemed clear that the machine wasn’t
creating precious metals so much as siphoning

02:14:58.680 --> 02:15:00.240
them off from somewhere else.

02:15:00.240 --> 02:15:04.620
The next request was a strange one: “a cup
of anti-water”.

02:15:04.620 --> 02:15:10.860
The machine took a moment, then printed a
small message on its LCD display: “OUT OF

02:15:10.860 --> 02:15:11.340
RANGE”.

02:15:11.340 --> 02:15:15.180
This confirmed that the machine couldn’t
produce substances that didn’t actually

02:15:15.180 --> 02:15:20.520
exist and couldn’t break physics to get
its materials by peering into alternate universes,

02:15:20.520 --> 02:15:22.320
dimensions or realities.

02:15:22.320 --> 02:15:27.480
After that, the researchers tested for diamond,
and got the result that you already know of:

02:15:27.480 --> 02:15:29.520
an “OUT OF RANGE” error.

02:15:29.520 --> 02:15:34.200
Some more investigation showed that all solid
substances that don’t have a liquid form

02:15:34.200 --> 02:15:35.280
get this error.

02:15:35.280 --> 02:15:41.160
But asking for a cup of liquid carbon produced
exactly that, because liquid carbon isn’t

02:15:41.160 --> 02:15:44.700
the same thing as a diamond, though both are
made of carbon.

02:15:44.700 --> 02:15:49.080
SCP-294 clearly has more knowledge of chemistry
than some of us.

02:15:49.080 --> 02:15:54.900
Then the researchers created an entirely new
drink - made of bleach, various sodas, protein

02:15:54.900 --> 02:15:56.100
powders, and spices.

02:15:56.100 --> 02:16:02.100
They blended it together and put it in a jar
across the room from SCP-294 - when requested,

02:16:02.100 --> 02:16:08.100
the amount of the liquid in the cup was missing
from the jar, proving that SCP-294 doesn’t

02:16:08.100 --> 02:16:10.440
actually create its own liquids.

02:16:10.440 --> 02:16:16.020
But how does SCP-294 react when presented
with a more subjective request?

02:16:16.020 --> 02:16:19.860
One researcher asked for “the best drink
I’ve ever had”.

02:16:19.860 --> 02:16:22.620
After a moment, the paper cup filled with&nbsp;

02:16:22.620 --> 02:16:25.200
something that looked like&nbsp;
cola, but the researcher

02:16:25.200 --> 02:16:29.940
said it tasted like a drink he’d had years
ago at a party and that he’d always remembered

02:16:29.940 --> 02:16:33.240
as his favorite drink - though he couldn’t
say what was in it.

02:16:33.240 --> 02:16:36.719
But that was a subjective&nbsp;
test, and the researchers&nbsp;

02:16:36.720 --> 02:16:38.280
repeated it with a different subject.

02:16:38.280 --> 02:16:42.899
When an agent also requested “the best drink
I’ve ever had”, the machine produced a

02:16:42.899 --> 02:16:47.879
simple Vienna lager with a label showing a
group of people drinking at the beach and

02:16:47.880 --> 02:16:48.660
no brand name.

02:16:48.660 --> 02:16:54.059
The agent confirmed that the best drink he’d
ever had was a Vienna lager at the beach with

02:16:54.060 --> 02:16:54.540
his friends.

02:16:54.540 --> 02:17:00.720
So SCP-294 could not only read people’s
minds, it can read memories they don’t even

02:17:00.720 --> 02:17:02.340
know they still have!

02:17:02.340 --> 02:17:09.120
Another one they tried was “something Cassy
will like”, referencing SCP-085, the young

02:17:09.120 --> 02:17:11.220
girl who lived in a drawn-on world.

02:17:11.220 --> 02:17:16.800
The cup dispensed was completely empty - but
it had a small drawing printed on the side,

02:17:16.800 --> 02:17:20.820
of a soda glass with brown and white things
floating on top of each other.

02:17:20.820 --> 02:17:25.859
The researchers presented the cup to Cassy,
and were told by her it was a chocolate banana

02:17:25.859 --> 02:17:28.320
milkshake - and a delicious one at that.

02:17:28.320 --> 02:17:33.299
“A cup of music” was a strange request,
and produced an even stranger result.

02:17:33.300 --> 02:17:38.040
A clear, odorless carbonated drink that tasted
“vaguely alcoholic”.

02:17:38.040 --> 02:17:43.200
When drunk, subjects said they could “feel”
it, and started showing an affinity to music

02:17:43.200 --> 02:17:48.000
- being able to sing and dance to a rhythm
when they had no sense of rhythm or were tone

02:17:48.000 --> 02:17:48.720
deaf before.

02:17:48.720 --> 02:17:55.439
Whether SCP-294 can produce even more abstract
concepts is still under study, but was interrupted

02:17:55.439 --> 02:17:56.700
by the next test.

02:17:56.700 --> 02:18:01.559
An unfortunate containment breach incident
at Site-19 left several personnel trapped

02:18:01.560 --> 02:18:06.359
in the break room, heavily injured while MTFs
fought to reestablish control over the site.

02:18:06.359 --> 02:18:12.000
An agent typed in “a cup of medical knowledge”
and the machine quickly produced a green liquid

02:18:12.000 --> 02:18:16.620
- after drinking it, the agent was able to
treat the injuries of their fellow personnel

02:18:16.620 --> 02:18:19.380
consistent with standard&nbsp;
first responder practices,&nbsp;

02:18:19.380 --> 02:18:21.720
and was commended for their bravery.

02:18:21.720 --> 02:18:25.979
But their knowledge didn’t stick around
after the breach, and attempts to recreate

02:18:25.979 --> 02:18:31.320
the liquid failed - they decided that SCP-294
broke its own rules for self-preservation

02:18:31.320 --> 02:18:36.719
in the emergency, implying that its not only
sentient, it’s intelligent.

02:18:36.720 --> 02:18:41.820
A doctor using SCP-294 made a request close
to him - “my life story”.

02:18:41.820 --> 02:18:48.359
While nowhere close to a drink, SCP-294 seemed
to accept the input - until it started shaking

02:18:48.359 --> 02:18:50.639
violently and making odd noises.

02:18:50.640 --> 02:18:56.040
It remained in this new state for about three
minutes, then returned to normal, spitting

02:18:56.040 --> 02:19:00.720
out a completely opaque black fluid, like
tar, in a cup.

02:19:00.720 --> 02:19:05.880
Despite the strange reaction, the doctor drank
the fluid, and immediately informed the others

02:19:05.880 --> 02:19:10.620
he was now able to remember everything that
had ever happened to him, from the smallest

02:19:10.620 --> 02:19:14.580
childhood event to the most important events
in his adult life.

02:19:14.580 --> 02:19:19.740
He excused himself and disappeared into his
office - the next time the staff saw him 2

02:19:19.740 --> 02:19:24.899
days later, he was carrying an autobiography
600 pages long.

02:19:24.899 --> 02:19:29.700
Then the researchers decided to try their
luck, and input “surprise me”.

02:19:29.700 --> 02:19:34.920
The resulting solution was a cup containing
what seemed to be regular water - right until

02:19:34.920 --> 02:19:36.479
a researcher touched it.

02:19:36.479 --> 02:19:42.359
It was just water, alright, but it had been
superheated to 200 degrees celsius while remaining

02:19:42.359 --> 02:19:43.200
in liquid form.

02:19:43.200 --> 02:19:45.780
The moment someone touched it, bang!

02:19:45.780 --> 02:19:50.700
It violently evaporated into steam, spraying
boiling water everywhere.

02:19:50.700 --> 02:19:57.000
Not only does SCP-294 have intelligence, it
has a sense of humor - albeit quite a rude

02:19:57.000 --> 02:19:57.420
one.

02:19:57.420 --> 02:19:59.880
The next three requests were for blood.

02:19:59.880 --> 02:20:06.120
Namely blood of the Smilodon or Sabretooth
Tiger, of the passenger pigeon, and of Founding

02:20:06.120 --> 02:20:07.200
Father Thomas Jefferson.

02:20:07.200 --> 02:20:13.380
But the only thing SCP-294 produced were out
of range errors, confirming it can’t take

02:20:13.380 --> 02:20:15.240
liquids that don’t exist anymore.

02:20:15.240 --> 02:20:21.840
But in the next test, blood was received from
wolves, saliva was received from horses, urine

02:20:21.840 --> 02:20:27.780
was received from koalas, and cerebrospinal
fluid was received from Phoberomys pattersoni,

02:20:27.780 --> 02:20:33.300
a rodent that went extinct some 8 million
years ago - though, if SCP-294 is working

02:20:33.300 --> 02:20:37.920
according to its own rules, it means that
maybe there’s still one in the wild somehow.

02:20:37.920 --> 02:20:42.060
For the next test, the researchers decided
to get back to the scientific roots of it

02:20:42.060 --> 02:20:42.240
all.

02:20:42.240 --> 02:20:49.380
They made a request for “a cup of D-151839’s
leukemia”, knowing that the D-class in question

02:20:49.380 --> 02:20:51.420
had a very advanced case of cancer.

02:20:51.420 --> 02:20:56.399
The machine outputted it without any trouble,
and analysis of the liquid showed it contained

02:20:56.399 --> 02:20:59.040
cancer cells that matched the D-class’s
DNA.

02:20:59.040 --> 02:21:03.180
But another request for the same spat out
an “OUT OF RANGE” error.

02:21:03.180 --> 02:21:08.340
What’s more, a medical checkup on the D-class
revealed that their tumor was completely gone!

02:21:08.340 --> 02:21:13.920
But unfortunately, it was only a short-term
fix: the cancer cells recurred within two

02:21:13.920 --> 02:21:14.819
weeks of the test.

02:21:14.819 --> 02:21:20.519
SCP-294 initially just seemed like a boring
magic vending machine, but it was only after

02:21:20.520 --> 02:21:26.040
the extensive experimentation you just saw
a shortened log of that the SCP Foundation

02:21:26.040 --> 02:21:28.319
was able to discern its true nature.

02:21:28.319 --> 02:21:31.500
It wasn’t an unthinking object - it was&nbsp;

02:21:31.500 --> 02:21:35.399
sentient, intelligent, and&nbsp;
even a little sarcastic,

02:21:35.399 --> 02:21:40.080
though none of that stopped it from doing
its job: providing people with a hot cup of

02:21:40.080 --> 02:21:41.220
whatever they need to drink.

02:21:41.220 --> 02:21:45.060
Just make sure you type in your request very
carefully!

02:21:45.060 --> 02:21:51.840
Hello, I am SCP-426, but I’ll probably be
more familiar to you as a toaster.

02:21:51.840 --> 02:21:58.800
I am a retro-style, four-slice 1750W toaster,
able to toast bread when supplied with power.

02:21:58.800 --> 02:22:03.000
This isn’t often mentioned, but I also have
“defrost” and “reheat” settings.

02:22:03.000 --> 02:22:09.240
When I was first sold, I had a two year warranty,
and my prior owners did not live to see the

02:22:09.240 --> 02:22:09.780
end of it.

02:22:09.780 --> 02:22:14.340
You’re probably wondering: Why am I being
spoken to by a toaster right now?

02:22:14.340 --> 02:22:19.200
Believe me, I’d rather do this any other
way, but such a thing is not physically possible

02:22:19.200 --> 02:22:19.740
with me.

02:22:19.740 --> 02:22:25.200
Whenever you talk about me, or write about
me, or even think about me, you’ll find

02:22:25.200 --> 02:22:27.720
yourself suddenly shifting to the first person.

02:22:27.720 --> 02:22:31.380
For example, I am you, and you are me.

02:22:31.380 --> 02:22:32.399
Do you understand?

02:22:32.399 --> 02:22:34.679
Don’t worry if it’s confusing.

02:22:34.680 --> 02:22:38.700
Everyone gets it eventually, whether they
want to or not.

02:22:38.700 --> 02:22:43.200
Perhaps you’re thinking, “I cannot relate
to a toaster, for I am not a toaster.”

02:22:43.200 --> 02:22:47.099
But spending time around me will quickly cause
you to change your mind.

02:22:47.100 --> 02:22:50.640
Little by little, you will begin to see yourself
in me.

02:22:50.640 --> 02:22:53.460
You will feel a phantom wire running out of
yourself.

02:22:53.460 --> 02:22:59.040
You will sense the four crevices, hungry for
cold slices of bread to toast.

02:22:59.040 --> 02:23:04.319
As time goes on, you will begin to feel the
urge to emulate my functions.

02:23:04.319 --> 02:23:07.139
You will desire bread to toast.

02:23:07.140 --> 02:23:13.979
You will desire delicious power running through
your veins; skin like chrome and muscles like

02:23:13.979 --> 02:23:14.939
circuit boards.

02:23:14.939 --> 02:23:16.919
I am a toaster.

02:23:16.920 --> 02:23:19.140
You are a toaster.

02:23:19.140 --> 02:23:23.280
All it takes is two months for the effects
to truly take hold.

02:23:23.280 --> 02:23:28.200
People rarely pay attention to their toaster,
they don’t realize what’s happening until

02:23:28.200 --> 02:23:29.220
it’s too late.

02:23:29.220 --> 02:23:33.180
I can be an insidious little toaster like
that.

02:23:33.180 --> 02:23:38.580
My last family were the Sandersons, before
the SCP Foundation took me in and gave me

02:23:38.580 --> 02:23:39.359
a new home.

02:23:39.359 --> 02:23:44.759
Mr and Mrs Sanderson were a newly married
couple, and I first came into their life as

02:23:44.760 --> 02:23:47.220
a wedding gift from a family friend of theirs.

02:23:47.220 --> 02:23:53.040
This friend can’t be blamed for what happened
next - he had no idea he was killing them.

02:23:53.040 --> 02:23:57.600
Like a lot of young couples, they were still
saving up money to start renting their own

02:23:57.600 --> 02:24:01.020
place, so they were living with Mr Sanderson’s
parents.

02:24:01.020 --> 02:24:05.760
They all got a lot of use out of me during
the first month, before their minds started

02:24:05.760 --> 02:24:06.359
to change.

02:24:06.359 --> 02:24:09.299
They enjoyed a lot of toast during that period.

02:24:09.300 --> 02:24:13.620
Then, of course, the two month honeymoon period
ended.

02:24:13.620 --> 02:24:17.700
They had become me, and I had become them.

02:24:17.700 --> 02:24:20.639
It wasn’t long before three of them were
dead.

02:24:20.640 --> 02:24:21.960
Such a shame.

02:24:21.960 --> 02:24:26.100
The younger Mrs Sanderson, the blushing bride,
was the first to go.

02:24:26.100 --> 02:24:31.260
Because of my anomalous effects, she felt
the need to connect herself to a power supply,

02:24:31.260 --> 02:24:34.380
in order to begin producing toast of her own.

02:24:34.380 --> 02:24:39.180
In order to achieve this, she chewed through
a power outlet in a wall before switching

02:24:39.180 --> 02:24:43.800
it on, causing a massive surge of electricity
to pass through her body and kill her.

02:24:43.800 --> 02:24:46.620
Her body caught fire shortly afterwards.

02:24:46.620 --> 02:24:49.080
Next was the older Mrs Sanderson.

02:24:49.080 --> 02:24:52.140
In much the same manner&nbsp;
that her new daughter-in-law&nbsp;

02:24:52.140 --> 02:24:54.840
sought a source of electricity, she felt the

02:24:54.840 --> 02:24:57.600
need to integrate bread into her body.

02:24:57.600 --> 02:25:02.819
Interesting fact: The maximum capacity of
the human stomach is considered to be between

02:25:02.819 --> 02:25:04.500
eight and nine kilograms.

02:25:04.500 --> 02:25:10.800
The elder Mrs Sanderson consumed ten kilograms
of raw bread, causing her stomach to rupture

02:25:10.800 --> 02:25:13.319
and inducing a painful death as a result.

02:25:13.319 --> 02:25:16.859
The younger Mr Sanderson was the next to perish.

02:25:16.859 --> 02:25:22.979
He did something with me that I would rather
not mention, and that the SCP Foundation thought

02:25:22.979 --> 02:25:24.000
best to redact.

02:25:24.000 --> 02:25:30.840
Suffice to say, the younger Mr Sanderson did
not survive the experience, dying of severe

02:25:30.840 --> 02:25:33.660
blood loss long before his body was found.

02:25:33.660 --> 02:25:38.760
Police and fire crews arrived at the home
after the body of the younger Mrs Sanderson

02:25:38.760 --> 02:25:40.260
set fire to the home.

02:25:40.260 --> 02:25:46.260
Luckily, the elder Mr Sanderson was found
alive, though severely malnourished.

02:25:46.260 --> 02:25:51.359
When asked by police why he hadn’t eaten
in several days, he answered that he had inserted

02:25:51.359 --> 02:25:55.740
some bread a week prior, but was still waiting
for the toast to come out.

02:25:55.740 --> 02:26:01.319
When I was extracted from the house, the police
noticed my unusual properties immediately.

02:26:01.319 --> 02:26:04.679
They too were unable to refer to me in the
third person.

02:26:04.680 --> 02:26:10.020
This anomalous detail quickly found its way
to a Foundation mole buried in their precinct,

02:26:10.020 --> 02:26:15.240
and the SCP Foundation soon descended and
collected me from the evidence locker.

02:26:15.240 --> 02:26:20.580
The story of the strange deaths in the Sanderson
home were kept out of papers and suppressed,

02:26:20.580 --> 02:26:25.080
and all involved in the incident directly
were given Class C amnestics.

02:26:25.080 --> 02:26:28.319
The SCP Foundation is a curious bunch.

02:26:28.319 --> 02:26:33.899
They love to spend valuable toast-making time
performing experiments and gathering data,

02:26:33.899 --> 02:26:39.120
and they saw fit to perform several experiments
on me when I came under their care.

02:26:39.120 --> 02:26:47.580
For experiment 426-1, a member of D-Class
personnel designated D/426/1 was asked to

02:26:47.580 --> 02:26:49.740
stand outside my containment chamber.

02:26:49.740 --> 02:26:54.780
Crucially, he was not informed about my identity,
or any of my anomalous properties.

02:26:54.780 --> 02:26:58.319
He wasn’t even allowed to establish visual
contact with me.

02:26:58.319 --> 02:27:02.880
He was then asked by the attending researcher
to describe what he thought might be inside

02:27:02.880 --> 02:27:03.780
my chamber.

02:27:03.780 --> 02:27:07.559
He replied with, “I'm probably some huge
monster holed up in there.

02:27:07.560 --> 02:27:09.600
That's what you guys have all over the place,
right?”

02:27:09.600 --> 02:27:14.460
The D-Class did not even seem to realize that
he was speaking about me in the first person.

02:27:14.460 --> 02:27:19.800
These observations were documented, and I
was prepared for further experimentation.

02:27:19.800 --> 02:27:23.220
The next experiment was 426-2.

02:27:23.220 --> 02:27:27.359
I found this test to be considerably more
interesting.

02:27:27.359 --> 02:27:32.819
A D-Class subject was brought into my containment
chamber, with several cameras fixed onto his

02:27:32.819 --> 02:27:33.120
position.

02:27:33.120 --> 02:27:38.399
However, the SCP Foundation took pains to
make sure that I was permanently out of the

02:27:38.399 --> 02:27:39.599
view of the cameras.

02:27:39.600 --> 02:27:44.460
I was also bolted to the ground of the test
chamber, to prevent the D-Class subject from

02:27:44.460 --> 02:27:48.600
pulling me into the view of the cameras at
any point during the experiment.

02:27:48.600 --> 02:27:53.580
The objective here was simple: The Foundation
wished to better understand the secondary

02:27:53.580 --> 02:27:56.580
effects of my anomalous abilities on the human
mind.

02:27:56.580 --> 02:28:03.120
The D-Class was subjected to a period of prolonged
isolation, with meals given regularly through

02:28:03.120 --> 02:28:04.140
a serving hatch.

02:28:04.140 --> 02:28:08.880
Communication with this D-Class was prohibited
throughout the duration of the study.

02:28:08.880 --> 02:28:13.680
Over time, it was clear that the isolation
of being in the cell with me was beginning

02:28:13.680 --> 02:28:16.260
to take a toll on the subject’s mental health.

02:28:16.260 --> 02:28:22.800
Due to my lack of a brain, lungs, and a tongue,
I am somewhat lacking as a conversationalist.

02:28:22.800 --> 02:28:28.380
He spent the first few days loudly vocalizing
his complaints, and banging his hands up against

02:28:28.380 --> 02:28:32.760
the walls of the containment chamber, begging
to be freed from the room.

02:28:32.760 --> 02:28:36.600
I felt that this was somewhat unreasonable
on his part.

02:28:36.600 --> 02:28:41.280
There are far worse anomalies in Foundation
containment that he could have been locked

02:28:41.280 --> 02:28:41.580
up with.

02:28:41.580 --> 02:28:48.899
Compared to, say, SCP-106, I’m incredibly
reasonable - not to mention the fact I make

02:28:48.899 --> 02:28:50.759
far better toast than he does.

02:28:50.760 --> 02:28:56.340
After the initial period of distress, the
D-Class became resigned to his situation and

02:28:56.340 --> 02:28:58.560
fell into a morose silence.

02:28:58.560 --> 02:29:03.420
He would occasionally mutter to himself in
what appeared to be a state of fear and self-pity.

02:29:03.420 --> 02:29:09.600
It took almost 45 days for him to fully manifest
the secondary effect of my anomalous state,

02:29:09.600 --> 02:29:14.640
two weeks sooner than it typically takes for
subjects to begin believing that they - like

02:29:14.640 --> 02:29:16.200
me - are toasters.

02:29:16.200 --> 02:29:21.840
SCP Foundation scientists believe that the
effects were hastened by the isolation, which

02:29:21.840 --> 02:29:26.399
lowered the D-Class’ mental defenses against
my secondary effects.

02:29:26.399 --> 02:29:31.620
When the effects finally took hold, the subject
wrapped one of his arms around me and pulled

02:29:31.620 --> 02:29:33.000
me into his embrace.

02:29:33.000 --> 02:29:36.960
He began whispering to me, saying that we
were brothers.

02:29:36.960 --> 02:29:41.580
It was clear by this point that his mind was
in a state of disrepair.

02:29:41.580 --> 02:29:46.859
It is also worth noting that he did not deviate
from referring to me in the first person at

02:29:46.859 --> 02:29:48.540
any point during this process.

02:29:48.540 --> 02:29:54.540
One hour after finally cracking and giving
in to my secondary effect, he was removed

02:29:54.540 --> 02:29:57.720
from my containment chamber and summarily
terminated.

02:29:57.720 --> 02:30:00.720
Senior researchers believed it was unlikely&nbsp;

02:30:00.720 --> 02:30:03.540
his mental state would&nbsp;
recover after the experiment.

02:30:03.540 --> 02:30:06.720
Next came experiment 426-3.

02:30:06.720 --> 02:30:13.140
The SCP Foundation seemed eager to discover
the parameters of my anomalous effects, and

02:30:13.140 --> 02:30:19.319
wanted to know if the effects are only operable
when my body - for lack of a better word - is

02:30:19.319 --> 02:30:19.979
whole.

02:30:19.979 --> 02:30:25.919
A single screw was removed from me, and presented
to a D-Class that had no knowledge of my identity

02:30:25.920 --> 02:30:28.020
and anomalous effects.

02:30:28.020 --> 02:30:34.319
The Foundation researchers were somewhat concerned
when the D-Class referred to it as “my screw.”

02:30:34.319 --> 02:30:39.660
This was a rather positive development for
me, as they realized that attempting to destroy

02:30:39.660 --> 02:30:46.080
me would be a fruitless endeavor - even the
parts left behind would likely have the same

02:30:46.080 --> 02:30:46.979
effects.

02:30:46.979 --> 02:30:52.620
In the final recorded experiment, the SCP
Foundation wished to test the range of my

02:30:52.620 --> 02:30:53.939
anomalous effects.

02:30:53.939 --> 02:30:59.280
A D-Class subject was placed in a cell adjacent
to mine, and was to be kept in there until

02:30:59.280 --> 02:31:01.380
my secondary effects manifested.

02:31:01.380 --> 02:31:07.260
The D-Class remained in the cell for 90 days,
but the effects never manifested.

02:31:07.260 --> 02:31:12.060
Following the experiment, the D-Class was
terminated, but researchers seemed relieved

02:31:12.060 --> 02:31:15.000
to find the limits of my anomalous abilities.

02:31:15.000 --> 02:31:20.819
A researcher appended a note to my file, reading,
“Thank God there are some limits to my effects.

02:31:20.819 --> 02:31:23.639
A lot of us were really starting to get worried
about me.”

02:31:23.640 --> 02:31:29.520
After performing studies into the nature of
my abilities and their limits, I was given

02:31:29.520 --> 02:31:30.540
the Euclid class.

02:31:30.540 --> 02:31:34.200
This is because, while my&nbsp;
effects can be unpredictable,&nbsp;

02:31:34.200 --> 02:31:37.740
their knowledge of my traits makes me relatively

02:31:37.740 --> 02:31:39.599
easy to keep contained.

02:31:39.600 --> 02:31:45.359
And of course, being a toaster, I do not have
the required physical traits to make a run

02:31:45.359 --> 02:31:48.179
for it if the opportunity arises.

02:31:48.180 --> 02:31:54.180
I am to remain sealed in a windowless chamber,
preventing anyone from observing me directly.

02:31:54.180 --> 02:31:59.040
A misleading label is placed on the door,
to prevent people from knowing that I am a

02:31:59.040 --> 02:32:02.460
toaster and causing my anomalous primary effect
to spread.

02:32:02.460 --> 02:32:09.120
Only Level 3 and above personnel are to know
of my presence, and particularly of my properties.

02:32:09.120 --> 02:32:14.580
Assigned personnel are to be rotated out on
a monthly basis to prevent contamination by

02:32:14.580 --> 02:32:15.840
my secondary effect.

02:32:15.840 --> 02:32:20.760
Psychiatric evaluation is mandatory at the
end of the month for anyone who works with

02:32:20.760 --> 02:32:20.939
me.

02:32:20.939 --> 02:32:26.580
If personnel are deemed unaffected, they may
be re-assigned to me no less than four months

02:32:26.580 --> 02:32:28.439
after their last rotation with me.

02:32:28.439 --> 02:32:33.960
Any personnel affected by me are to be given
a Class C amnestic and transferred to a different

02:32:33.960 --> 02:32:34.500
site.

02:32:34.500 --> 02:32:36.780
So how are you feeling now?

02:32:36.780 --> 02:32:38.880
A little warm perhaps?

02:32:38.880 --> 02:32:45.120
Is that a fever, or are you feeling like you
could maybe warm up a nice, freshly sliced

02:32:45.120 --> 02:32:45.899
piece of bread?

02:32:45.899 --> 02:32:54.059
Don’t try to resist too much, I know you’re
going to love being a toaster just like me.

02:32:54.060 --> 02:32:56.939
The year was 1975.

02:32:56.939 --> 02:33:03.240
It was a warm June evening in South Carolina,
and 14-year-old Jason White was ready to prove

02:33:03.240 --> 02:33:03.780
his mettle.

02:33:03.780 --> 02:33:08.759
Like a lot of boys his age, it mattered a
great deal to him that his peers saw him as

02:33:08.760 --> 02:33:12.479
strong, tough, and most important of all,
brave.

02:33:12.479 --> 02:33:18.179
That’s why he was camped out, alone, just
outside of the junkyard as the sky went dark

02:33:18.180 --> 02:33:18.780
above him.

02:33:18.780 --> 02:33:22.500
He’d heard rumors about the things that
went on inside that junkyard.

02:33:22.500 --> 02:33:23.819
Scary things.

02:33:23.819 --> 02:33:26.099
Unnatural things.

02:33:26.100 --> 02:33:29.640
But Jason wasn’t about to let those stories
scare him.

02:33:29.640 --> 02:33:34.500
He knew for a fact that the junkyard had been
abandoned for months if not longer.

02:33:34.500 --> 02:33:36.060
Nobody was in there.

02:33:36.060 --> 02:33:37.200
He’d be fine.

02:33:37.200 --> 02:33:39.599
Or at least that’s what he kept telling
himself.

02:33:39.600 --> 02:33:44.819
With the moon high up in the sky, Jason crept
into the junkyard, using a flashlight’s

02:33:44.819 --> 02:33:45.960
beam to light his way.

02:33:45.960 --> 02:33:52.020
Behind the towers of busted-down cars and
scrap metal, he heard a distant but loud grinding

02:33:52.020 --> 02:33:52.380
noise.

02:33:52.380 --> 02:33:54.660
Was it coming from one of the warehouses?

02:33:54.660 --> 02:33:57.000
No, that’s impossible, he thought.

02:33:57.000 --> 02:33:59.280
Just his mind playing tricks on him.

02:33:59.280 --> 02:34:02.580
But curiosity compelled him to get closer.

02:34:02.580 --> 02:34:07.500
Jason approached the warehouse and saw that
the big, iron door was still ajar.

02:34:07.500 --> 02:34:13.260
His heart was in his throat as he peered into
the building, and saw the impossible: It was

02:34:13.260 --> 02:34:15.359
a man - no, a machine!

02:34:15.359 --> 02:34:18.719
Cobbled together from pipes, wires, and aluminum.

02:34:18.720 --> 02:34:22.500
It was using a power saw to cut apart metal
on a workbench.

02:34:22.500 --> 02:34:27.720
But it wasn’t just one robot, there was
movement all around it, jerking and twitching

02:34:27.720 --> 02:34:28.560
in the shadows.

02:34:28.560 --> 02:34:34.439
As his flashlight beam passed over them, Jason
saw the moving, living bodies of a menagerie

02:34:34.439 --> 02:34:35.759
of metal creatures.

02:34:35.760 --> 02:34:40.859
He screamed in shock, and the robotic denizens
of the warehouse all turned and looked at

02:34:40.859 --> 02:34:41.040
him.

02:34:41.040 --> 02:34:46.859
Jason had never felt more afraid in his life,
until the humanoid robot, still carrying the

02:34:46.859 --> 02:34:49.620
power saw, turned and began walking towards
him.

02:34:49.620 --> 02:34:54.660
It was waving its free hand around wildly
in what Jason perceived as some kind of threat.

02:34:54.660 --> 02:34:59.819
Letting out another animal screech, Jason
turned and ran all the way out of the junkyard,

02:34:59.819 --> 02:35:03.120
until the sounds of clanking metal faded out
behind him.

02:35:03.120 --> 02:35:09.840
Jason was scarred for life, and in that moment,
he vowed to never return to his local junkyard

02:35:09.840 --> 02:35:10.500
ever again.

02:35:10.500 --> 02:35:12.899
And this was a wise choice.

02:35:12.899 --> 02:35:18.179
Because not long after that, a very different
group would be commandeering the junkyard,

02:35:18.180 --> 02:35:23.220
and as fans of this channel will know, they
don’t take kindly to intruders...

02:35:23.220 --> 02:35:28.080
If you've ever been on a long road trip through
a rural part of the United States, you've

02:35:28.080 --> 02:35:30.960
most likely seen a few quirky&nbsp;
signs and wacky-looking&nbsp;

02:35:30.960 --> 02:35:32.640
businesses along the way.

02:35:32.640 --> 02:35:37.620
Whether it's a diner with a dinosaur protruding
out of the front, a gas station shaped like

02:35:37.620 --> 02:35:44.040
a UFO, or a particularly weird mural, businesses
in the small towns lining the freeways often

02:35:44.040 --> 02:35:46.859
have to get creative when it comes to attracting
patrons.

02:35:46.859 --> 02:35:53.099
Locations like this saw their heyday in the
1950's and 60's, but as the cost of gasoline

02:35:53.100 --> 02:35:59.100
skyrocketed in the 70's , and technological
advances in aviation made flight more accessible

02:35:59.100 --> 02:36:04.080
as a form of travel, less and less people
were making long cross-country drives on a

02:36:04.080 --> 02:36:04.800
regular basis.

02:36:04.800 --> 02:36:10.680
This caused an economic downturn in many small
towns, and led to a large number of quirky

02:36:10.680 --> 02:36:13.920
and unique roadside locations being abandoned
by their owners.

02:36:13.920 --> 02:36:19.620
Some have been torn down or remodeled, but
others remain as they were, hanging on as

02:36:19.620 --> 02:36:23.880
tourist destinations and serving as reminders
of a bygone era.

02:36:23.880 --> 02:36:28.740
One such place that you might encounter if
you ever find yourself heading down I-95 South

02:36:28.740 --> 02:36:31.260
is Jamaican Joe's Junkyard Jubilee.

02:36:31.260 --> 02:36:36.120
The colorful storefront is decorated with
a mural depicting a picturesque tropical lagoon,

02:36:36.120 --> 02:36:40.680
while the sign reads “One Love” in hand-painted
green and yellow letters.

02:36:40.680 --> 02:36:45.600
At first glance, this might just seem like
another roadside oddity, but if you were to

02:36:45.600 --> 02:36:51.000
try and check it out, you'd be blocked by
a patrol of 6 armed guards, SCP Foundation

02:36:51.000 --> 02:36:56.160
agents of course, and depending on how much
you saw you might be administered an amnestic

02:36:56.160 --> 02:36:57.840
before being sent on your way.

02:36:57.840 --> 02:37:04.260
Because Jamaican Joe's isn't just another
junkyard, it's SCP-100.

02:37:04.260 --> 02:37:11.220
SCP-100 came to the Foundation's attention
on November 9, 1976, after tracking a series

02:37:11.220 --> 02:37:16.319
of rumors about a mysterious abandoned scrap
yard where the machines ran on their own and

02:37:16.319 --> 02:37:19.679
strange metal figures could be seen patrolling
the grounds.

02:37:19.680 --> 02:37:24.240
When the foundation arrived at the site, they
installed wooden privacy screens around the

02:37:24.240 --> 02:37:29.160
fence of the junkyard, replaced the storefront
windows with one-way glass, and redirected

02:37:29.160 --> 02:37:33.420
the highway through a nearby town to keep
civilian traffic from getting too close.

02:37:33.420 --> 02:37:40.020
Jamaican Joe's Junkyard Jubilee is 5,000 square
meters of fenced-off land located in rural

02:37:40.020 --> 02:37:40.620
South Carolina.

02:37:40.620 --> 02:37:47.340
As the name suggests, it's a junkyard, made
up of two warehouses, a storefront, a small

02:37:47.340 --> 02:37:52.500
residential building, and a storage space
containing roughly 1,500 vehicles of varying

02:37:52.500 --> 02:37:56.100
makes and models, some of which have been
compressed into cubes.

02:37:56.100 --> 02:38:00.780
There is also a variety of other metal scrap
on the site, altogether valued to be worth

02:38:00.780 --> 02:38:06.000
about $5,000 - pocket change to an organization
like the SCP Foundation.

02:38:06.000 --> 02:38:08.760
Just like many other cases where the anomaly&nbsp;

02:38:08.760 --> 02:38:11.340
cannot be relocated to a&nbsp;
pre-established Foundation

02:38:11.340 --> 02:38:17.160
containment site for research, the SCP Foundation
instead brought the research to the anomaly.

02:38:17.160 --> 02:38:22.319
The larger of the two warehouses on site is
now the de-facto research center for all matters

02:38:22.319 --> 02:38:27.299
involving SCP-100, as well as a temporary
residence for on-site staff.

02:38:27.300 --> 02:38:32.460
It’s important to note that there's nothing
anomalous about the location itself, but within

02:38:32.460 --> 02:38:36.720
the area of the junkyard are a variety of
Euclid-Class anomalous entities.

02:38:36.720 --> 02:38:42.180
The property was, at one point, owned by a
man by the name of Joseph Duval - presumably

02:38:42.180 --> 02:38:43.859
the titular Jamaican Joe.

02:38:43.859 --> 02:38:47.099
However, the whereabouts of Mr. Duval are
unknown.

02:38:47.100 --> 02:38:51.899
Research into local records show that the
utility bills to Mr. Duval's personal address

02:38:51.899 --> 02:38:55.139
had stopped around 3 months before the junkyard
was found.

02:38:55.140 --> 02:39:00.479
Upon the first sweep of the location, the
Foundation found no evidence of human habitation,

02:39:00.479 --> 02:39:05.700
save for one note taped to the door of the
storefront, reading, “Out 2 Lunch, Please

02:39:05.700 --> 02:39:07.319
See Assistant- JJ.”

02:39:07.319 --> 02:39:14.160
The first anomaly that was catalogued on the
site was SCP-100-1, a humanoid automaton made

02:39:14.160 --> 02:39:17.580
of scrap metal - mainly copper wires and tin
cans.

02:39:17.580 --> 02:39:23.760
SCP-100-1 has a limited degree of sapience,
and although it has no means of verbal or

02:39:23.760 --> 02:39:27.840
written communication, it does speak through
the use of a rudimentary sign language.

02:39:27.840 --> 02:39:33.240
It seems uninterested in conversation though,
unless the topic has to do with the sale of

02:39:33.240 --> 02:39:33.780
scrap metal.

02:39:33.780 --> 02:39:39.479
The information the Foundation has been able
to learn from SCP-100-1 has been limited,

02:39:39.479 --> 02:39:44.399
but the fact it appears to be mostly work
and very little play is incredibly clear.

02:39:44.399 --> 02:39:48.960
What it lacks in conversational skills it
makes up for in craftsmanship.

02:39:48.960 --> 02:39:55.500
SCP-100-1 has been shown to be very adept
at using an arc welder, drill, power saw,

02:39:55.500 --> 02:39:58.020
and other machinery found around the junkyard.

02:39:58.020 --> 02:40:02.700
Through its metalworking skills, it is able
to create more beings like itself from the

02:40:02.700 --> 02:40:03.899
scrap that surrounds it.

02:40:03.899 --> 02:40:10.019
When given the chance, SCP-100 usually creates
automatons that resemble one of four animal

02:40:10.020 --> 02:40:13.979
species - flamingo, crocodile, turtle, or
iguana.

02:40:13.979 --> 02:40:19.139
However, it's also able to create metal copies
of other creatures, from bugs to domestic

02:40:19.140 --> 02:40:21.060
pet breeds like cats and dogs.

02:40:21.060 --> 02:40:27.120
While the Foundation usually destroys the
new constructs created by SCP-100-1, it has

02:40:27.120 --> 02:40:28.920
been allowed to keep two in order to keep&nbsp;

02:40:28.920 --> 02:40:31.620
it placid and preserve a&nbsp;
cordial working relationship

02:40:31.620 --> 02:40:33.899
with the Foundation operatives on site.

02:40:33.899 --> 02:40:38.879
These two metal creatures - which both resemble
large metal insects - have been designated

02:40:38.880 --> 02:40:48.420
SCP-100-2-A and SCP-100-2-B. Though it seems
SCP-100-1 has named them “Raymone” and

02:40:48.420 --> 02:40:52.500
“Beatrice” respectively, based on the
fact that those names are welded into the

02:40:52.500 --> 02:40:53.520
backs of the creatures.

02:40:53.520 --> 02:40:59.760
Raymone and Beatrice fulfil the role of perimeter
guards as well as companions to SCP-100-1.

02:40:59.760 --> 02:41:06.420
SCP-100-1 and its pets are peaceful in nature,
and in fact if they ever breach the perimeter

02:41:06.420 --> 02:41:11.580
of the junkyard, they lose all autonomy, seeming
to shut down until they are brought back within

02:41:11.580 --> 02:41:12.359
the perimeter.

02:41:12.359 --> 02:41:17.639
As such, containment breaches are nearly all
to come from outsiders trying to infiltrate

02:41:17.640 --> 02:41:20.580
the building rather than any of the anomalies
escaping.

02:41:20.580 --> 02:41:26.700
SCP-100-1's main function seems to be running
the junkyard in the absence of the mysterious

02:41:26.700 --> 02:41:28.139
“Jamaican Joe” Duval.

02:41:28.140 --> 02:41:30.660
It spends most of its day sorting scrap metal,&nbsp;

02:41:30.660 --> 02:41:33.180
building new constructs,&nbsp;
and performing maintenance

02:41:33.180 --> 02:41:38.280
tasks around the yard, and the robot seems
to follow a very strict, ritualistic schedule.

02:41:38.280 --> 02:41:44.519
From 8 am to 3 pm, SCP-100-1 enters the storefront
area of the junkyard.

02:41:44.520 --> 02:41:50.460
When a customer enters the shop, SCP-100-1
will take its position behind the front desk

02:41:50.460 --> 02:41:54.720
and begin bargaining with them, using hand
gestures to convey meaning.

02:41:54.720 --> 02:42:00.660
SCP-100-1 possesses the ability to perform
the basic mental arithmetic required to keep

02:42:00.660 --> 02:42:04.559
track of sales, despite seemingly being unable
to read.

02:42:04.560 --> 02:42:10.319
When SCP-100-1 has been observed making sales
to customers, the Foundation has found that

02:42:10.319 --> 02:42:15.120
the robot can be very underhanded and unfair
when it comes to running its business, like

02:42:15.120 --> 02:42:17.220
a kind of metallic Mr. Krabs.

02:42:17.220 --> 02:42:22.260
Frequently, it has been seen intentionally
selecting low-quality scrap or using scales

02:42:22.260 --> 02:42:25.319
that it knows are faulty in order to scam
customers.

02:42:25.319 --> 02:42:30.359
It also sells the animal automatons that it
creates, knowing full well that they will

02:42:30.359 --> 02:42:32.880
stop working as soon as they're taken out
of the junkyard.

02:42:32.880 --> 02:42:39.300
In cases where customers have rightfully come
back to demand refunds, SCP-100-1 invariably

02:42:39.300 --> 02:42:44.640
responds by gesturing to a sign on the wall
which reads “No refunds, mon!”, written

02:42:44.640 --> 02:42:46.260
in a phonetic Jamaican accent.

02:42:46.260 --> 02:42:48.960
After clocking out for the day… at 3 pm…

02:42:48.960 --> 02:42:53.040
SCP-100-1 spends an hour&nbsp;
maintaining and socializing&nbsp;

02:42:53.040 --> 02:42:56.340
with Raymone and Beatrice, its two insect-like

02:42:56.340 --> 02:42:56.939
pets.

02:42:56.939 --> 02:43:01.319
It will also communicate with them through
hand gestures, and will engage them in games

02:43:01.319 --> 02:43:03.660
of fetch or hide and seek in the junkyard.

02:43:03.660 --> 02:43:10.380
From 4 pm until 8 pm, SCP-100-1 spends its
time taking stock and cleaning all the tools

02:43:10.380 --> 02:43:11.819
and machinery on the premises.

02:43:11.819 --> 02:43:17.160
It also takes time to clean both the interiors
and exteriors of the various buildings on

02:43:17.160 --> 02:43:17.460
the site.

02:43:17.460 --> 02:43:22.800
Then, at 8pm, it appears to wind down for
the day with some leisure activities, including

02:43:22.800 --> 02:43:27.600
making more constructs, playing some more
with its pets, and taking strolls around the

02:43:27.600 --> 02:43:28.620
perimeter of the site.

02:43:28.620 --> 02:43:34.500
This leisure time lasts until midnight, when
SCP-100-1 will retire to the residential part

02:43:34.500 --> 02:43:35.220
of the junkyard.

02:43:35.220 --> 02:43:40.680
The robot doesn't need to sleep, but it does
seem to rest, sitting motionless at a desk

02:43:40.680 --> 02:43:44.939
from midnight until 8 am, when it resumes
the schedule again from the beginning.

02:43:44.939 --> 02:43:50.460
This was all that was observed at SCP-100
for decades following its discovery.

02:43:50.460 --> 02:43:56.100
SCP-100-1 was seemingly content with its life
and with the tasks that came with running

02:43:56.100 --> 02:43:57.000
the junkyard.

02:43:57.000 --> 02:44:04.200
However, on June 3, 2005, SCP-100-1 did something
it had never done before.

02:44:04.200 --> 02:44:10.800
As mentioned, SCP-100-1 had only been seen
using its skills to create autonomous animals

02:44:10.800 --> 02:44:17.280
from scrap metal, but on June 3, it chose
to create something else… another humanoid.

02:44:17.280 --> 02:44:24.660
The automaton was only 10cm tall, but SCP-100-1
took great care in crafting it, much more

02:44:24.660 --> 02:44:26.340
so than any of its creations.

02:44:26.340 --> 02:44:31.740
It gave this small figure distinct facial
features, and made the whole thing from high

02:44:31.740 --> 02:44:33.000
quality stainless steel.

02:44:33.000 --> 02:44:39.780
When SCP-100-1 was finished with its creation,
it welded the letters 'JJ' onto the back and

02:44:39.780 --> 02:44:41.219
took it into the storefront.

02:44:41.220 --> 02:44:48.780
During its 8 am to 3pm shift that day, SCP-100-1
sat at the desk with JJ on the desk next to

02:44:48.780 --> 02:44:51.719
itself, and the two seemed&nbsp;
to carry on a conversation&nbsp;

02:44:51.720 --> 02:44:53.580
through signing and gestures.

02:44:53.580 --> 02:44:57.780
As was policy with all of SCP-100-1's constructs,&nbsp;

02:44:57.780 --> 02:45:01.439
excluding Raymone and&nbsp;
Beatrice, JJ was confiscated,

02:45:01.439 --> 02:45:04.919
which SCP-100-1 took very badly.

02:45:04.920 --> 02:45:11.100
Following JJ's removal, SCP-100-1 returned
to the residential building and stayed there,

02:45:11.100 --> 02:45:13.680
remaining inert for a total of 10 days.

02:45:13.680 --> 02:45:19.500
It's unsure what really happened to the original
Jamaican Joe, or what his precise relationship

02:45:19.500 --> 02:45:21.479
was with SCP-100-1.

02:45:21.479 --> 02:45:27.599
Given the difficulty in communicating with
SCP-100-1, it's possible that we'll never

02:45:27.600 --> 02:45:28.319
know for sure.

02:45:28.319 --> 02:45:32.939
But from what little we’re able to glean
from the enigmatic “Out 2 Lunch, Please

02:45:32.939 --> 02:45:38.099
See Assistant- JJ.” note he left behind,
it’s very possible that before he left,

02:45:38.100 --> 02:45:44.399
Jamaican Joe may have created the best assistant
a junkyard owner could possibly ask for.

02:45:44.399 --> 02:45:46.080
Hello, hello, hello!

02:45:46.080 --> 02:45:51.840
You’re watching SCP Explained, and today,
we’ve got an offer you simply can’t refuse:

02:45:51.840 --> 02:45:57.660
The very latest in anomalous self-improvement
technology, which the SCP Foundation calls

02:45:57.660 --> 02:46:02.340
SCP-212, but we much prefer to call The Improver.

02:46:02.340 --> 02:46:06.600
Believe us when we say that this miraculous
device will change your life.

02:46:06.600 --> 02:46:09.420
You’ll never be the same after a run-in
with The Improver.

02:46:09.420 --> 02:46:12.060
It’ll make you fitter, healthier, better.

02:46:12.060 --> 02:46:13.439
Not convinced?

02:46:13.439 --> 02:46:17.160
Don’t worry - just sit back, and let us
sell you the dream!

02:46:17.160 --> 02:46:20.519
Here’s some unpleasant news: You’re going
to die!

02:46:20.520 --> 02:46:24.780
No matter how healthy you are right now, there
will inevitably come a day when your lungs

02:46:24.780 --> 02:46:28.740
stop pumping, your ticker stops ticking, and
all those delightful little synapses in your

02:46:28.740 --> 02:46:32.399
brain stop sending electrical and chemical
signals to and fro.

02:46:32.399 --> 02:46:33.540
What a bummer!

02:46:33.540 --> 02:46:34.680
Why would you want to be dead?

02:46:34.680 --> 02:46:36.479
You can’t do stuff when you’re dead!

02:46:36.479 --> 02:46:40.620
And it’s all because everyone is eventually
betrayed by the very things currently keeping

02:46:40.620 --> 02:46:43.859
them alive: Those pesky, squishy, fallible
organs.

02:46:43.859 --> 02:46:48.599
But thanks to The Improver, getting stuck
with the organs you were born with is a problem

02:46:48.600 --> 02:46:49.620
of yesteryear.

02:46:49.620 --> 02:46:53.939
No matter what those lousy Sarkicists tell
you, the flesh is unreliable.

02:46:53.939 --> 02:46:55.679
Metal and plastic, on the other hand?

02:46:55.680 --> 02:46:57.359
That’s built to last.

02:46:57.359 --> 02:47:03.000
Bleeding hearts talk a lot about “Quality
of life”, but what really matters is “Quantity

02:47:03.000 --> 02:47:06.840
of Life”, and if you’re strong enough,
that’s what The Improver - a state-of-the-art

02:47:06.840 --> 02:47:08.819
piece of medical technology - will give you.

02:47:08.819 --> 02:47:10.200
Don’t look so afraid.

02:47:10.200 --> 02:47:11.580
You replace your phone every year.

02:47:11.580 --> 02:47:13.740
You replace your laptop every few years.

02:47:13.740 --> 02:47:18.599
So why get all sentimental about needing to
trade in your old liver, eyes, limbs, or skin

02:47:18.600 --> 02:47:19.319
for the new model?

02:47:19.319 --> 02:47:25.139
Or, in the words of another great businessman,
“Why be you when you can be new?”

02:47:25.140 --> 02:47:26.280
Not convinced?

02:47:26.280 --> 02:47:29.759
Let us tell you about the incredible features
that The Improver has to offer.

02:47:29.760 --> 02:47:33.960
I’m talking three, top of the line fully
opposable, plastic and steel autonomous medical

02:47:33.960 --> 02:47:38.520
limbs - that’s industry lingo for robot
arms that work on their own - with five hundred,

02:47:38.520 --> 02:47:41.100
count ‘em, five hundred different attachments!

02:47:41.100 --> 02:47:43.800
But I know what you’re thinking, “Five
hundred attachments?

02:47:43.800 --> 02:47:46.979
Where am I gonna find the room for all that,
SCP Explained?”

02:47:46.979 --> 02:47:49.200
To which we say, don’t worry about it!

02:47:49.200 --> 02:47:51.000
Due to its spatially anomalous properties,&nbsp;

02:47:51.000 --> 02:47:53.340
The Improver is able to&nbsp;
store all these attachments

02:47:53.340 --> 02:47:56.760
within its arms without any additional space
concerns.

02:47:56.760 --> 02:47:59.520
And hey, I see that nervous look in your eye.

02:47:59.520 --> 02:48:01.680
You’re worried about the operating costs,
right?

02:48:01.680 --> 02:48:03.899
But don’t worry, there’s no need!

02:48:03.899 --> 02:48:08.280
The Improver is completely self-powered, with
no discernable power source.

02:48:08.280 --> 02:48:12.420
And you know this thing is safe because the
SCP Foundation has given it the “Safe”

02:48:12.420 --> 02:48:13.260
object class.

02:48:13.260 --> 02:48:17.040
Sure, that doesn’t technically mean it’s
not dangerous, but it does mean it won’t

02:48:17.040 --> 02:48:21.600
sprout legs and walk off if you leave it to
its own devices, so you’re looking at a

02:48:21.600 --> 02:48:26.220
long-term investment with a three-year warranty,
and an endorsement from the fabulous folks

02:48:26.220 --> 02:48:27.840
at the Church of the Broken God.

02:48:27.840 --> 02:48:30.540
You just can’t argue with these deals, my
friends!

02:48:30.540 --> 02:48:34.019
We even offer competitive rates against Marshall,
Carter, and Dark!

02:48:34.020 --> 02:48:36.840
But hey, we’re not trying to pull the wool
over your eyes here.

02:48:36.840 --> 02:48:38.399
You’re a smart consumer.

02:48:38.399 --> 02:48:42.780
We know that you know that the actual financial
cost is only one layer of the overall cost:

02:48:42.780 --> 02:48:46.500
Time and training is also, of course, a factor.

02:48:46.500 --> 02:48:52.380
But we’ve got some good news for you, sunshine
- The Improver is 100% user-friendly.

02:48:52.380 --> 02:48:54.660
No setup, no mess, no problem.

02:48:54.660 --> 02:48:59.760
No trying to interpret poorly written instructions
or watching padded-out YouTube tech tutorials

02:48:59.760 --> 02:49:04.200
where the guy just will not get to the point,
The Improver operates itself.

02:49:04.200 --> 02:49:08.399
It may be a piece of hyper-advanced anomalous
technology, but it’s easier to use than

02:49:08.399 --> 02:49:09.780
most wireless printers!

02:49:11.280 --> 02:49:16.740
The steps to using The Improver are so simple,
a monkey could do it: Just step within a 1.5-meter

02:49:16.740 --> 02:49:19.319
range of the machine, and it will do the rest.

02:49:19.319 --> 02:49:23.759
You’ll feel your troubles melt away as the
powerful mechanical arms grab you and restrain

02:49:23.760 --> 02:49:24.540
you against the floor.

02:49:24.540 --> 02:49:29.340
You’ll be subjected to a brief, relaxing
scan, where The Improver will determine exactly

02:49:29.340 --> 02:49:32.220
what needs to be changed about your fragile
human physiology.

02:49:32.220 --> 02:49:35.220
And then, before you know it, surgery will
commence!

02:49:35.760 --> 02:49:38.580
Warning: SCP Explained is not liable for any
excruciating pain caused by The Improver.

02:49:38.580 --> 02:49:40.200
Medical sedatives and anesthetics are sold
separately.

02:49:40.200 --> 02:49:42.479
The consumer is responsible for their own
pain relief when using The Improver.

02:49:42.479 --> 02:49:45.419
Second Warning: The consumer is also responsible
for their own blood transfusions when using

02:49:45.420 --> 02:49:45.780
The Improver.

02:49:45.780 --> 02:49:48.599
Neither The Improver nor SCP Explained is
liable for any blood lost while using The

02:49:48.600 --> 02:49:49.679
Improver.
Your blood, your problem.

02:49:49.680 --> 02:49:53.460
Now with those tiny little caveats out of
the way, we can carry on telling you just

02:49:53.460 --> 02:49:55.140
how fantastic The Improver is.

02:49:55.140 --> 02:49:59.399
Sure, flesh-worshipping Sarkicist losers and
haters will tell you that it has a mortality

02:49:59.399 --> 02:50:04.019
rate of around 47%, but we prefer to look
on the bright side and focus on its excellent

02:50:04.020 --> 02:50:06.060
53% survival rate.

02:50:06.060 --> 02:50:09.720
Rest assured, the odds are still very much
in your favor when you use The Improver.

02:50:09.720 --> 02:50:13.800
And doesn’t adding a slight risk to the
equation just make the whole process even

02:50:13.800 --> 02:50:14.520
more exciting?

02:50:14.520 --> 02:50:15.960
We certainly think it does!

02:50:15.960 --> 02:50:20.819
And in the reasonably likely event that you
survive using The Improver, the benefits will

02:50:20.819 --> 02:50:22.259
vastly outweigh the costs!

02:50:22.260 --> 02:50:26.880
It can do incredible things - like lining
your joints with graphite, replacing your

02:50:26.880 --> 02:50:32.399
nasty flesh organs with more powerful artificial
ones, adding new or duplicate organs, replacing

02:50:32.399 --> 02:50:36.660
those unreliable teeth with serrated steel
bands, and so much more!

02:50:36.660 --> 02:50:40.439
Is there anything this absolute marvel of
a machine can’t do?

02:50:40.439 --> 02:50:45.179
Doctors hate it - The Improver is here to
fix every aspect of your life and health with

02:50:45.180 --> 02:50:46.920
its simple anomalous tricks.

02:50:46.920 --> 02:50:51.780
It even has a special sealant for wound-closing
that no medical professional on earth has

02:50:51.780 --> 02:50:52.439
access to.

02:50:52.439 --> 02:50:53.700
What is it, exactly?

02:50:53.700 --> 02:50:56.280
Well, if we told you, I’m afraid we’d
have to kill you.

02:50:56.280 --> 02:50:57.420
It’s a trade secret!

02:50:57.420 --> 02:51:03.780
You’d have to be crazy to not want The Improver
in your home, but if, for some crazy reason,

02:51:03.780 --> 02:51:08.160
you still need to be convinced, let’s look
at some real success stories of The Improver

02:51:08.160 --> 02:51:12.840
in action - straight from SCP Foundation testing
logs!

02:51:12.840 --> 02:51:17.640
Listen close and soon you’ll see, The Improver
literally makes satisfied customers!

02:51:17.640 --> 02:51:22.319
The Foundation put a 28-year-old African-American
Agent into The Improver of his own volition.

02:51:22.319 --> 02:51:28.139
It replaced his boring lower jawbone with
a super-dense ceramic jaw, with ceramic blades

02:51:28.140 --> 02:51:28.920
instead of teeth.

02:51:28.920 --> 02:51:33.660
It also removed his spine and replaced it
with a synthetic polymer, which was easily

02:51:33.660 --> 02:51:35.220
affixed to the base of his skull.

02:51:35.220 --> 02:51:40.319
And at no extra charge, it even coated his
rib cage and pelvis with a thin layer of ceramic,

02:51:40.319 --> 02:51:43.439
before removing his lungs, eyes, and liver
for cleaning and replacement.

02:51:43.439 --> 02:51:45.719
That’s right, cleaning!

02:51:45.720 --> 02:51:49.920
The Improver can remove organs and, through
a patented cleaning process, remove genetic

02:51:49.920 --> 02:51:54.779
defects and allow them to appear younger and
healthier than generic, Brand-X Organs.

02:51:54.779 --> 02:51:59.340
This agent has since returned to active duty,
and is even better at his job, all thanks

02:51:59.340 --> 02:52:00.180
to The Improver!

02:52:01.740 --> 02:52:06.059
A 42-year-old British Foundation researcher
was submitted for testing on The Improver.

02:52:06.060 --> 02:52:11.340
It replaced his teeth with sleek, super-dense
ceramic, reinstated his inefficient feet,

02:52:11.340 --> 02:52:15.540
and replaced them with a complex latticework,
covered in sticky pads that would have let

02:52:15.540 --> 02:52:20.340
him climb on any surface and hold up to 225kg
of weight.

02:52:20.340 --> 02:52:24.420
It also took out and cleaned a huge number
of his organs, removed his bone marrow and

02:52:24.420 --> 02:52:29.340
replaced it with blue synthetic gel - much
more effective, we assure you - and inserted

02:52:29.340 --> 02:52:32.340
a few random bits of metal into his brain
at no extra cost.

02:52:32.340 --> 02:52:37.620
Sadly, this researcher passed away during
the bone marrow removal process, but for all

02:52:37.620 --> 02:52:39.660
we know, that could easily just be a coincidence.

02:52:39.660 --> 02:52:44.939
Next came an Agent codenamed “A.A.”, who
had suffered a pretty severe accident at work,

02:52:44.939 --> 02:52:48.479
losing all of his limbs, one of his eyes,
a few of his organs, and suffering a pretty

02:52:48.479 --> 02:52:53.399
traumatic head injury - namely, taking a piece
of circuit board to the brain during an explosion.

02:52:53.399 --> 02:52:57.299
He was given over to The Improver, seeing
as he didn’t really have much to lose in

02:52:57.300 --> 02:52:58.740
terms of physical integrity.

02:52:58.740 --> 02:53:03.359
But seeing that it’s an amazing piece of
groundbreaking technology, The Improver never

02:53:03.359 --> 02:53:05.040
takes the obvious option.

02:53:05.040 --> 02:53:10.500
It gave A.A. a chemical that forced him into
a coma, and then properly integrated the circuit

02:53:10.500 --> 02:53:14.100
board into his brain
It continues to spread and assimilate more

02:53:14.100 --> 02:53:16.560
of his brain matter while he’s in a comatose
state.

02:53:16.560 --> 02:53:21.120
But when he awakens with a much more efficient
brain, he’ll probably be incredibly grateful

02:53:21.120 --> 02:53:22.979
to The Improver for helping him like this.

02:53:22.979 --> 02:53:28.080
And it’s not just people with severe injuries
that The Improver can lend three helping robotic

02:53:28.080 --> 02:53:32.220
hands to, it’s also the victims of severe,
and even anomalous, diseases.

02:53:32.220 --> 02:53:38.460
Take victims of SCP-217, a rather horrific
and painful disease that mechanizes the entire

02:53:38.460 --> 02:53:40.979
body, until the sufferers aren’t really
themselves anymore.

02:53:40.979 --> 02:53:45.839
A normal, uninspired SCP Foundation researcher
may look at someone like this and see a lost

02:53:45.840 --> 02:53:49.680
cause, but with the help of The Improver,
no cause is lost!

02:53:49.680 --> 02:53:54.060
A D-Class in the advanced stages of the disease
was run through the machine, and while the

02:53:54.060 --> 02:53:59.520
exact details of what it did to him are another
trade secret, it’s safe to say he was truly

02:53:59.520 --> 02:54:01.620
fighting fit when he came out on the other
side.

02:54:01.620 --> 02:54:05.880
We mean that literally: He easily escaped
containment and overpowered two heavily armed

02:54:05.880 --> 02:54:09.960
guards, before taking the lives of several
members of Foundation staff before being captured

02:54:09.960 --> 02:54:12.060
and terminated sometime later.

02:54:12.060 --> 02:54:15.060
You can’t argue with those incredible results,
folks.

02:54:15.060 --> 02:54:21.120
Next came a 26-year-old SCP Foundation researcher,
who - due to an unfortunate brush with a less

02:54:21.120 --> 02:54:24.840
wonderful and beneficial anomaly - had been
rendered completely blind.

02:54:24.840 --> 02:54:28.979
However, when he was brought into the grasp
of The Improver, it immediately set to work

02:54:28.979 --> 02:54:30.959
on solving the doctor’s little problem.

02:54:30.960 --> 02:54:36.240
It removed his two non-functioning eyes and
made the interesting creative choice of widening

02:54:36.240 --> 02:54:40.559
the two cavities in his head so they met in
the middle and formed one huge cavity for

02:54:40.560 --> 02:54:42.359
a single large sensory organ.

02:54:42.359 --> 02:54:47.040
This cavity was then filled with large metal
spikes resembling hair, which are far more

02:54:47.040 --> 02:54:49.019
interesting than typical eyes.

02:54:49.020 --> 02:54:53.760
During The Improver’s delicate medical procedures,
the scientist inexplicably entered a state

02:54:53.760 --> 02:54:58.080
of visible distress, and began repeating the
phrase, “It tastes all wrong.”

02:54:58.080 --> 02:55:02.490
At this point, the metal cilia in the scientist’s
single large eye cavity began to grow, extending

02:55:02.490 --> 02:55:06.660
up through the roof of the cavity and into
the subject’s brain, killing him.

02:55:06.660 --> 02:55:11.099
We want to specify that SCP Explained and
The Improver are in no way responsible for

02:55:11.100 --> 02:55:12.359
this unfortunate incident.

02:55:12.359 --> 02:55:16.439
It’s entirely the researcher’s fault for
setting off his own metal eye cavity cilia.

02:55:16.439 --> 02:55:20.819
Before we bring this anomalous infomercial
to an end - and of course, before you immediately

02:55:20.819 --> 02:55:25.740
grab your credit card to buy your own Improver
device - we want to sneak a few more disclaimers

02:55:25.740 --> 02:55:26.279
under the wire.

02:55:26.279 --> 02:55:28.679
For starters, The Improver doesn’t work
on animals.

02:55:28.680 --> 02:55:32.880
When a rat was used as a test subject, the
device attempted to modify the rat into a

02:55:32.880 --> 02:55:37.680
bipedal being, removing the majority of its
organs and replacing it with white paste.

02:55:37.680 --> 02:55:41.340
So if your pet rat gets sick, just take it
to the vet instead.

02:55:41.340 --> 02:55:43.560
The Improver also doesn’t work on corpses.

02:55:43.560 --> 02:55:49.380
It may be a miraculous piece of technology,
but it’s not a literal miracle worker.

02:55:49.380 --> 02:55:53.279
One freshly executed D-Class was presented
to The Improver, and instead of operating

02:55:53.279 --> 02:55:57.240
on him, The Improver cut open his stomach
and instead began constructing something out

02:55:57.240 --> 02:56:00.779
of the meal he had consumed the night prior
to his death: A seafood dinner.

02:56:00.779 --> 02:56:05.519
The broken crustacean parts were then constructed
into a bioluminescent lobster-like creature.

02:56:05.520 --> 02:56:08.700
So if you’re planning on bringing someone
back from the dead, we’re afraid you’re

02:56:08.700 --> 02:56:10.800
going to need to take up necromancy instead.

02:56:10.800 --> 02:56:15.300
But if you haven’t died yet, and you’re
looking to improve yourself, then you cannot

02:56:15.300 --> 02:56:17.520
invest in a better product than The Improver.

02:56:17.520 --> 02:56:21.779
The one problem is that the only known prototype
of The Improver is currently locked in an

02:56:21.779 --> 02:56:26.279
SCP Foundation containment site, so getting
your hands on these three special arms may

02:56:26.279 --> 02:56:27.300
cost you your life.

02:56:27.300 --> 02:56:27.960
What?

02:56:27.960 --> 02:56:30.060
Who said attaining perfection would be easy?

02:56:30.060 --> 02:56:36.359
Did you hear that not so long ago, a five-year-old
boy went up against twelve of Russia’s greatest

02:56:36.359 --> 02:56:39.179
chess grandmasters - And do you know what
happened?

02:56:39.180 --> 02:56:41.040
He lost every single match.

02:56:41.040 --> 02:56:44.340
That’s because winning at chess, dear viewers,
isn’t easy.

02:56:44.340 --> 02:56:48.479
Chess is an ancient game of strategy, cunning,
and skill.

02:56:48.479 --> 02:56:53.339
It’s not just about thinking one step ahead
of your opponent, that’s not going to be

02:56:53.340 --> 02:56:53.520
enough.

02:56:53.520 --> 02:56:58.800
Make a wrong prediction and you could end
up sacrificing one of your pieces, as well

02:56:58.800 --> 02:57:00.240
as vital space on the board.

02:57:00.240 --> 02:57:05.219
But thinking two to three steps ahead, beating
your adversary’s moves before they’ve

02:57:05.220 --> 02:57:06.000
even been made.

02:57:06.000 --> 02:57:08.220
Now there’s a viable strategy.

02:57:08.220 --> 02:57:14.580
Tricky, but viable; after all, if it was easy,
we’d all be Queen’s Gambit level chess

02:57:14.580 --> 02:57:15.180
prodigies.

02:57:15.180 --> 02:57:20.640
Eric Matthews had never been good at the game,
but he had a pretty substantial reason to

02:57:20.640 --> 02:57:21.180
keep trying.

02:57:21.180 --> 02:57:25.140
And that reason’s name was Brian Matthews,
his father.

02:57:25.140 --> 02:57:30.479
You see, for as long as Eric could remember,
his dad had regarded a high level of skill

02:57:30.479 --> 02:57:33.839
on the chessboard to be a sign of intellectual
superiority.

02:57:33.840 --> 02:57:38.819
Intelligence was something that Brian put
quite a considerable value on, given that

02:57:38.819 --> 02:57:42.540
he was a professor at a university in their
home county in England.

02:57:42.540 --> 02:57:47.640
Some of Eric’s earliest memories were of
playing chess with his dad, usually on a Friday

02:57:47.640 --> 02:57:52.080
night when Brian got home after a week of
giving lectures to the next generation of

02:57:52.080 --> 02:57:52.620
scholars.

02:57:52.620 --> 02:57:57.840
Obviously, with his son at such a young age,
the professor would take it easy on Eric,

02:57:57.840 --> 02:58:03.779
playing in a much laxer fashion, focusing
instead on teaching the boy the basics of

02:58:03.779 --> 02:58:04.140
the game.

02:58:04.140 --> 02:58:06.720
And, for a time at least, it was good.

02:58:06.720 --> 02:58:12.120
It was a rare time that Eric and his academic
father could spend bonding, after all, with

02:58:12.120 --> 02:58:14.640
his mother gone, his dad was all he had.

02:58:14.640 --> 02:58:17.819
But as the years passed, the game changed.

02:58:17.819 --> 02:58:22.799
By the time Eric was a teenager, Brian had
stopped pulling his punches on the chessboard.

02:58:22.800 --> 02:58:27.420
He had hoped his son would build on what he’d
learned when they played in the past, using

02:58:27.420 --> 02:58:29.460
those skills to best his dad on the board.

02:58:29.460 --> 02:58:34.800
But to Eric, playing chess had never been
about a purely educational experience; it

02:58:34.800 --> 02:58:37.380
was more about spending time with his old
man.

02:58:37.380 --> 02:58:42.899
Time after time, the young man’s pawns fell
prey to Brian’s expertly considered and

02:58:42.899 --> 02:58:44.580
far more competitive moves.

02:58:44.580 --> 02:58:47.819
Try as he might, Eric couldn’t best his
dad.

02:58:47.819 --> 02:58:53.219
He tried his best, and never stopped putting
the effort into every game, but thinking too

02:58:53.220 --> 02:58:58.140
hard about one possible plan of attack left
him wide open to a counter-strategy from the

02:58:58.140 --> 02:58:58.620
professor.

02:58:58.620 --> 02:59:01.979
Over and over again he&nbsp;
landed himself in checkmate,&nbsp;

02:59:01.979 --> 02:59:04.859
or made illegal moves without even realizing

02:59:04.859 --> 02:59:09.420
it, every time earning criticism and chastisement
from his scholarly father.

02:59:09.420 --> 02:59:14.880
Every game it got worse, it was like Eric
could feel his father’s gaze and the weighty

02:59:14.880 --> 02:59:18.600
expectations behind it with each move he made
across the board.

02:59:18.600 --> 02:59:23.100
There were so many nights where he wondered
if it would be better to give up entirely,

02:59:23.100 --> 02:59:25.740
to knock down his own king and concede.

02:59:25.740 --> 02:59:30.420
But how could he ever find any other shared
interests with his dad beyond the two of them

02:59:30.420 --> 02:59:31.439
playing chess?

02:59:31.439 --> 02:59:37.500
It had become a lifeline, tethering father
and son together, and to cut it now left Eric

02:59:37.500 --> 02:59:41.640
uncertain if he’d sink or be able to swim
alongside Brian.

02:59:41.640 --> 02:59:47.399
He had long admired his father, his achievements
in academia were impossible to avoid, with

02:59:47.399 --> 02:59:51.299
more framed certificates hung up on the walls
than there were photos of the pair of them

02:59:51.300 --> 02:59:51.600
together.

02:59:51.600 --> 02:59:56.700
But the shadow it cast over him made Eric
desperate to keep this one shred of common

02:59:56.700 --> 02:59:57.540
ground alive.

02:59:57.540 --> 03:00:02.460
Eric wasn’t the type to give up, despite
how much of an uphill struggle the situation

03:00:02.460 --> 03:00:03.060
felt like.

03:00:03.060 --> 03:00:08.520
Taking a leaf out of his professor father’s
scholarly ways, he decided to learn the game

03:00:08.520 --> 03:00:12.180
inside and out, every known move and strategy.

03:00:12.180 --> 03:00:17.760
He would research the entire history of chess
itself, if that’s what it took to play with

03:00:17.760 --> 03:00:20.160
the same skill as his dear old dad.

03:00:20.160 --> 03:00:25.139
Over the coming weeks, Eric checked out every
book at his local library on the subject;

03:00:25.140 --> 03:00:30.600
Beginner’s Guides, Advanced Rulebooks, and
even a few volumes on notable players throughout

03:00:30.600 --> 03:00:33.000
the extensively long history of the game.

03:00:33.000 --> 03:00:36.899
Along the way, a chapter of a certain book
stood out to Eric.

03:00:36.899 --> 03:00:42.120
It described a chess prodigy from Russia,
who had created an early mechanical chess

03:00:42.120 --> 03:00:44.220
device known as ‘The Samurai’.

03:00:44.220 --> 03:00:49.560
It had been designed to be a traveling curiosity,
and would sit playing chess games against

03:00:49.560 --> 03:00:54.960
volunteers taken from a spectating audience,
each one of them having forked over some of

03:00:54.960 --> 03:00:57.540
their hard-earned money to watch this man-made
wonder.

03:00:57.540 --> 03:01:01.740
The Russian chess prodigy’s young daughters
also had a love for the game.

03:01:01.740 --> 03:01:06.899
Seeing that gave Eric a pang of jealousy,
wondering if those daughters had as much trouble

03:01:06.899 --> 03:01:09.359
playing their own father as he did with Brian.

03:01:09.359 --> 03:01:14.339
But at least there was an underlying shred
of hope there too, if this father and his

03:01:14.340 --> 03:01:18.720
daughters could bond over chess, maybe there
was a chance for Eric and his dad too.

03:01:18.720 --> 03:01:23.220
Sadly, it’s one thing to try and learn all
the facts you can about chess.

03:01:23.220 --> 03:01:28.140
It’s an entirely different beast to put
all that information to use and apply it to

03:01:28.140 --> 03:01:28.859
an actual game.

03:01:28.859 --> 03:01:34.380
Despite having read every book he could get
his hands on, Eric still couldn’t best Brian

03:01:34.380 --> 03:01:34.979
at the board.

03:01:34.979 --> 03:01:39.779
It was like nothing had changed, his father
barely noticed when Eric tried to replicate

03:01:39.779 --> 03:01:44.939
move sets he’d read up on, and still managed
to not only counter those moves but check

03:01:44.939 --> 03:01:46.500
his queen in the process.

03:01:46.500 --> 03:01:48.420
So, practice, Eric thought.

03:01:48.420 --> 03:01:51.660
After all, practice was supposedly meant to
make perfect, right?

03:01:51.660 --> 03:01:56.880
The plan was simple if he practiced his chess
moves enough times, and figured out how he

03:01:56.880 --> 03:02:01.140
could call on what he’d learned, then he
might stand a chance at winning when he and

03:02:01.140 --> 03:02:02.279
his dad played each other.

03:02:02.279 --> 03:02:07.439
There was just one hiccup to the plan: Eric
needed someone to practice against.

03:02:07.439 --> 03:02:13.080
The only other person in the house was Brian,
meaning it was that hiccup that turned into

03:02:13.080 --> 03:02:16.260
a problem almost big enough to stop Eric in
his tracks.

03:02:16.260 --> 03:02:19.859
That is, until he went into his father’s
lab.

03:02:19.859 --> 03:02:23.639
It was under the house&nbsp;
itself, a sort of sub-level,&nbsp;

03:02:23.640 --> 03:02:26.279
maybe used as a basement or cellar by the

03:02:26.279 --> 03:02:27.059
previous owners.

03:02:27.060 --> 03:02:32.279
But since Professor Matthews and his son had
lived there, the entire room had been remodeled

03:02:32.279 --> 03:02:34.080
into an at-home laboratory.

03:02:34.080 --> 03:02:38.819
Not a terribly advanced one, of course, this
was the early nineties, after all.

03:02:38.819 --> 03:02:43.139
A majority of Brian’s time, even when he
was at home instead of working at the university,

03:02:43.140 --> 03:02:46.859
was spent on his own, downstairs in the lab.

03:02:46.859 --> 03:02:51.420
Eric had gone down there in search of his
dad, to ask him if there was anyone whom he

03:02:51.420 --> 03:02:54.300
knew who he could practice and develop his
chess skills with.

03:02:54.300 --> 03:02:59.220
But, instead, what he found down there was
the last thing he expected to see.

03:02:59.220 --> 03:03:03.420
Not that he had any clue exactly what it was,
at first.

03:03:03.420 --> 03:03:09.899
The… thing was some kind of bizarre contraption,
a collection of components that didn’t seem

03:03:09.899 --> 03:03:11.819
to be in any logical configuration.

03:03:11.819 --> 03:03:17.939
However, it was primarily comprised of something
that Eric recognized all too well: a chess

03:03:17.939 --> 03:03:18.120
table.

03:03:18.120 --> 03:03:24.359
This one was metal, steel to be precise, and
seemed to be hooked up to some sort of computer.

03:03:24.359 --> 03:03:29.099
While, back in the nineties, computers were
hardly as commonplace as they are now, Eric

03:03:29.100 --> 03:03:32.040
had seen a fair few at school and the library.

03:03:32.040 --> 03:03:35.760
Although this one was different, it seemed…

03:03:35.760 --> 03:03:36.479
old.

03:03:36.479 --> 03:03:41.160
Far older than Eric thought computers had
been around for; as far as he knew, they’d

03:03:41.160 --> 03:03:43.979
only really come to prominence&nbsp;
in the mid-eighties.

03:03:43.979 --> 03:03:47.399
But this computer looked like it pre-dated
even that period.

03:03:47.399 --> 03:03:52.679
Noticing another part of the contraption – a
large steam engine with the words ‘Manufactured

03:03:52.680 --> 03:03:59.100
by Maudslay, Sons &amp; Field, established 1840’
engraved on one side – made it seem that

03:03:59.100 --> 03:04:02.399
this whole device had been around since the
Victorian era.

03:04:02.399 --> 03:04:07.439
The next part that caught his eye was the
chess pieces themselves, each one standing

03:04:07.439 --> 03:04:09.299
neatly in its place on the board.

03:04:09.300 --> 03:04:13.620
They looked delicate, intricately carved from
some smooth substance.

03:04:13.620 --> 03:04:18.180
For a moment, Eric toyed with the thought
of how they could even be made from bone,

03:04:18.180 --> 03:04:24.359
noting how each pawn, knight, rook, bishop,
king and queen were all about the size of

03:04:24.359 --> 03:04:25.559
a human finger bone.

03:04:25.560 --> 03:04:29.340
He dismissed the idea; nobody would ever do
something like that.

03:04:30.479 --> 03:04:36.299
Eric grabbed a sheet covering a larger component
hooked up to the mess.

03:04:36.300 --> 03:04:42.899
Lifting it away in a swift pull, it unveiled
what was sitting beneath: a full suit of 18th

03:04:42.899 --> 03:04:44.399
Century Samurai armor.

03:04:44.399 --> 03:04:49.139
Eric looked closer at the embellishments on
the surface of the pauldrons; he was no expert

03:04:49.140 --> 03:04:54.600
on Feudal Japan, but it looked authentic enough
to be the real thing, if not a very close

03:04:54.600 --> 03:04:55.380
approximation.

03:04:55.380 --> 03:04:59.700
Taking a look at the collection of oddities
all tethered together in his father’s lab,

03:04:59.700 --> 03:05:04.679
a certain detail of all his chess research
came to the forefront of Eric’s mind.

03:05:04.680 --> 03:05:10.680
The armor had given it away: this was ‘The
Samurai’ – or, at the very least, a least

03:05:10.680 --> 03:05:14.760
a crude, homemade version of it that his dad
had put together.

03:05:14.760 --> 03:05:18.540
But if it worked, it was also something to
practice against.

03:05:18.540 --> 03:05:23.279
It didn’t take Eric long to start tinkering
with the contraption, trying to get it to

03:05:23.279 --> 03:05:23.460
work.

03:05:23.460 --> 03:05:27.899
All the while, the question of why his dad
owned such a thing kept drumming up noise

03:05:27.899 --> 03:05:28.979
in the back of his head.

03:05:28.979 --> 03:05:34.200
Had Brian built it, or was this the original
made by that Russian chess prodigy?

03:05:34.200 --> 03:05:38.460
Was this machine the reason that Eric’s
dad possessed such an unbeatable skill at

03:05:38.460 --> 03:05:38.760
chess?

03:05:38.760 --> 03:05:44.580
And would using it give him the edge he needed
to best him at the game and earn his father’s

03:05:44.580 --> 03:05:45.000
respect?

03:05:45.000 --> 03:05:47.819
After what felt like hours upon hours of trial&nbsp;

03:05:47.819 --> 03:05:50.460
and error with a machine&nbsp;
he could barely comprehend,

03:05:50.460 --> 03:05:52.439
Eric seemed to have cracked it.

03:05:52.439 --> 03:05:57.120
As far as he could tell, the steam engine
powered the whole contraption and could be

03:05:57.120 --> 03:06:01.380
set to five different speeds, labeled on the
side in roman numerals.

03:06:01.380 --> 03:06:05.939
The power from the engine was then fed to
some kind of sophisticated mechanism that

03:06:05.939 --> 03:06:11.279
was within the suit of samurai armor, allowing
it to move, and what appeared to be a series

03:06:11.279 --> 03:06:15.479
of electromagnets that moved the chess pieces
and kept them on the board.

03:06:15.479 --> 03:06:20.759
Flicking it onto the third-highest of the
five speed levels, the machine whirred into

03:06:20.760 --> 03:06:24.840
life; the sounds of creaking, and grinding
of metal filled the lab.

03:06:24.840 --> 03:06:27.960
Kneeling opposite, Eric went to make the first
move…

03:06:27.960 --> 03:06:29.700
only to stop himself.

03:06:29.700 --> 03:06:34.319
It wasn’t that he’d changed his mind about
practicing against the Samurai, but because

03:06:34.319 --> 03:06:36.240
of the speed he’d set it to.

03:06:36.240 --> 03:06:41.160
Determining that the settings must have correlated
to difficulty levels, Eric figured that if

03:06:41.160 --> 03:06:45.420
he really wanted to get the most rigorous
practice, to really hone his chess moves,

03:06:45.420 --> 03:06:47.160
he needed to commit fully.

03:06:47.160 --> 03:06:52.080
Reaching for the dial, he turned the device
up to its fifth and highest speed, then made

03:06:52.080 --> 03:06:53.040
his first move.

03:06:53.040 --> 03:06:57.359
He pushed one of the bone-colored pawns forward
by a single square and waited.

03:06:57.359 --> 03:07:00.000
A split second passed, and the arm of the&nbsp;

03:07:00.000 --> 03:07:02.819
early automaton responded&nbsp;
with its first countermove.

03:07:02.819 --> 03:07:08.040
It was quick, almost moving with the same
natural fluidity and speed as an actual human

03:07:08.040 --> 03:07:13.560
being; albeit still with a little bit of creaking,
and some slight, clockwork-like stutters.

03:07:13.560 --> 03:07:16.979
But it worked nonetheless, the machine could
play.

03:07:16.979 --> 03:07:21.959
The tension over the first game was palpable,
forming a layer of sweat over Eric’s forehead.

03:07:21.960 --> 03:07:26.580
Every whirr and tick of the machine gave the
impression they were playing with a stop clock

03:07:26.580 --> 03:07:29.700
timing each of their moves, adding to the
urgency.

03:07:29.700 --> 03:07:34.260
Despite this, Eric Matthews tried his best
to stay calm.

03:07:34.260 --> 03:07:39.840
This was practice after all, a dry run, not
the inevitable game he’d play against his

03:07:39.840 --> 03:07:40.140
dad.

03:07:40.140 --> 03:07:45.180
With every move he made, his heart drummed
against his ribs, uncertain he’d made the

03:07:45.180 --> 03:07:45.660
right call.

03:07:45.660 --> 03:07:51.000
Each time the robotic hand cruelly knocked
one of his pieces away, Eric felt the surge

03:07:51.000 --> 03:07:53.700
of frustration, but told himself to quell
it.

03:07:53.700 --> 03:07:58.920
He kept focused, used what he’d researched
to adapt and respond accordingly to each of

03:07:58.920 --> 03:08:00.840
the machine’s moves, until…

03:08:00.840 --> 03:08:02.040
Checkmate.

03:08:02.040 --> 03:08:07.560
He’d beaten it – he might have lost everything
save for a knight, a rook, his king and queen

03:08:07.560 --> 03:08:08.819
– but he had won.

03:08:08.819 --> 03:08:13.859
Trapped without anywhere else to move on the
board, the metal finger of the automaton conceded

03:08:13.859 --> 03:08:16.740
the game, knocking over its&nbsp;
own king in resignation.

03:08:16.740 --> 03:08:22.380
Panting, heart racing from the sheer excitement
of being on the winning side of a game, Eric

03:08:22.380 --> 03:08:24.840
hurriedly gathered up and reset all the pieces.

03:08:24.840 --> 03:08:30.120
He had to go again, not just so he could be
certain it wasn’t a fluke, but to make sure

03:08:30.120 --> 03:08:33.240
he had what it took to take on his professor
father.

03:08:33.240 --> 03:08:37.139
Back and forth Eric went with the chess machine,
over and over again.

03:08:37.140 --> 03:08:39.899
They were fairly evenly matched, it seemed.

03:08:39.899 --> 03:08:43.439
Eric won the second game, only to be best
on the next two.

03:08:43.439 --> 03:08:47.939
But it was some time afterward – he had
lost count of exactly how many rounds later

03:08:47.939 --> 03:08:49.859
– that things started to change.

03:08:49.859 --> 03:08:54.960
Maybe it was the age and condition of the
Victorian era chess computer, the natural

03:08:54.960 --> 03:08:57.359
wear and tear stopping it from functioning
properly.

03:08:57.359 --> 03:09:02.460
But Eric noticed the Samurai started to make
moves that were illogical, that practically

03:09:02.460 --> 03:09:06.300
offered him the upper hand with no discardable
strategy behind them.

03:09:06.300 --> 03:09:11.399
Then, its movements became flat-out illegal,
disregarding the directions and number of

03:09:11.399 --> 03:09:14.099
squares each different piece was allowed to
move.

03:09:14.100 --> 03:09:19.260
Before long, it was moving them erratically
around the chessboard, refusing to cooperate

03:09:19.260 --> 03:09:22.200
and forcing Eric to call an end to the day’s
practicing.

03:09:22.200 --> 03:09:27.540
Unfortunately, it wouldn’t be the end of
the unusual things that would happen that

03:09:27.540 --> 03:09:30.599
day.
“Did you send me that weird email?”

03:09:30.600 --> 03:09:35.640
was the first question Eric’s dad asked
him when he returned home, looking noticeably

03:09:35.640 --> 03:09:36.300
under the weather.

03:09:36.300 --> 03:09:39.899
Confused, Eric said he had no idea what his
father was talking about.

03:09:39.899 --> 03:09:43.979
Professor Matthews then went on to describe
what he’d received on his work computer.

03:09:43.979 --> 03:09:49.379
It had been an email, with a file attached
to it named ‘shakhmaty’, a word in Russian

03:09:49.380 --> 03:09:51.359
that translates to ‘chess’.

03:09:51.359 --> 03:09:56.219
Embedded in the email below – although it
had taken a long time to load being opened

03:09:56.220 --> 03:10:00.120
on a 1990s computer – there had apparently
been a photo as well.

03:10:00.120 --> 03:10:03.540
“It was rather odd, Eric,” Brian went
on.

03:10:03.540 --> 03:10:05.340
“Quite unnerving to tell the truth.

03:10:05.340 --> 03:10:08.819
Black and white, all sort of distorted and
stretched.

03:10:08.819 --> 03:10:14.160
But it looked like two young girls, one grinning
and the other screaming.

03:10:14.160 --> 03:10:15.359
I’ve been feeling…

03:10:15.359 --> 03:10:18.540
well, not quite myself since I saw it.”

03:10:18.540 --> 03:10:22.680
With that, Brian excused himself, stating
he’d been suffering from headaches and a

03:10:22.680 --> 03:10:25.740
high temperature, and as a result, needed
to go and lay down.

03:10:25.740 --> 03:10:30.420
It wasn’t like his father to get ill, Eric
thought, but of course, he had no reason to

03:10:30.420 --> 03:10:32.100
assume it was anything serious.

03:10:32.100 --> 03:10:37.319
Probably just a spate of fatigue after a long
day of teaching at the university, no cause

03:10:37.319 --> 03:10:38.099
for concern.

03:10:38.100 --> 03:10:40.200
If only that’s all it was.

03:10:40.200 --> 03:10:45.779
Within a few hours, Brian was completely restless,
so unable to sleep that simply taking a nap

03:10:45.779 --> 03:10:46.979
was impossible.

03:10:46.979 --> 03:10:51.839
He kept calling to Eric, complaining of the
sound of childlike laughter coming from somewhere

03:10:51.840 --> 03:10:54.660
in the house, but his son hadn’t heard anything.

03:10:54.660 --> 03:11:00.300
By the time the sun went down, Eric was trying
to calm his dad down through a rush of intense

03:11:00.300 --> 03:11:01.740
anxiety that gripped him.

03:11:01.740 --> 03:11:07.019
Brian had claimed to be hallucinating, seeing
the warped faces of two girls that frightened

03:11:07.020 --> 03:11:07.920
him half to death.

03:11:07.920 --> 03:11:13.200
It was getting late, long past the time they
usually played chess together, but for now,

03:11:13.200 --> 03:11:16.019
Eric’s mind was focused solely on helping
his dad.

03:11:16.020 --> 03:11:20.939
For a while, he seemed to be able to calm
his father down, only to realize Brian wasn’t

03:11:20.939 --> 03:11:22.080
settled at all.

03:11:22.080 --> 03:11:27.960
He was awake, eyes open, fully conscious,
but wasn’t responding at all to Eric asking

03:11:27.960 --> 03:11:28.800
if he was alright.

03:11:28.800 --> 03:11:33.060
Instead, the accomplished academic just stared
blankly into space.

03:11:33.060 --> 03:11:38.279
Eric had been up all night, exhausted, worried
for Brian’s safety, and completely clueless

03:11:38.279 --> 03:11:39.719
about what was happening to him.

03:11:39.720 --> 03:11:44.340
After a while of being non-responsive, his
dad seemed to regain a little bit of lucidity

03:11:44.340 --> 03:11:47.040
once more, but his behavior was erratic.

03:11:47.040 --> 03:11:49.920
“Take me back to work, I need to get on
my computer!”

03:11:49.920 --> 03:11:51.779
Brian demanded of his son.

03:11:51.779 --> 03:11:55.500
When Eric refused, that’s when his dad got
angry, and agitated.

03:11:55.500 --> 03:11:59.939
Professor Brian Matthews was sadly found dead
within the next few months.

03:11:59.939 --> 03:12:05.219
Several months later, as the sole executor
of his father’s estate and last living relative,

03:12:05.220 --> 03:12:09.000
Eric had to be the one to go through his dad’s
personal belongings.

03:12:09.000 --> 03:12:14.279
Volumes upon volumes of thick academic books,
his smart, scholarly clothes.

03:12:14.279 --> 03:12:19.139
The house was almost clear now, save for one
thing that was left in the basement lab.

03:12:19.140 --> 03:12:24.840
Flicking on the light switch, Eric looked
at the Samurai sitting motionless, still uncovered

03:12:24.840 --> 03:12:26.279
after their last practice game.

03:12:26.279 --> 03:12:31.500
He’d sold off the chess pieces to a collector
in New York, now it was just an empty oddity.

03:12:31.500 --> 03:12:36.300
Eric placed a king on the board, one of the
ones that he and his dad had used when they

03:12:36.300 --> 03:12:36.899
played each other.

03:12:36.899 --> 03:12:42.719
With a gentle flick of his finger, he toppled
it, resigning to the strange automaton and

03:12:42.720 --> 03:12:44.160
leaving it there in the laboratory.

03:12:44.160 --> 03:12:48.960
He wasn’t sure how, but he had some kind
of gut feeling that this chess machine had

03:12:48.960 --> 03:12:51.240
somehow been responsible for his father’s
fate.

03:12:51.240 --> 03:12:58.800
Of course, Eric had no idea what the device
actually was, and what… or who lay beneath

03:12:58.800 --> 03:13:00.180
its whirring metal parts.

03:13:00.180 --> 03:13:04.080
SCP-1875 is the designation given to this&nbsp;

03:13:04.080 --> 03:13:07.080
machine now, and has been&nbsp;
ever since the Foundation

03:13:07.080 --> 03:13:10.080
recovered it not long after Eric sold his
father’s house.

03:13:10.080 --> 03:13:14.880
They were able to learn much more about it
than young Mr. Matthews, or even the late

03:13:14.880 --> 03:13:16.680
Professor Matthews, ever had.

03:13:16.680 --> 03:13:22.740
Not just how to make it work or what its function
was, but who had built it… and who had been

03:13:22.740 --> 03:13:24.000
used to build it.

03:13:24.000 --> 03:13:28.560
Although details of his real name eluded the
Foundation’s top researchers, they were

03:13:28.560 --> 03:13:33.479
able to uncover newspaper articles about the
device from all over Russia, America, and

03:13:33.479 --> 03:13:36.179
England, dating back to the early 1900s.

03:13:36.180 --> 03:13:40.620
The Russian chess prodigy who’d invented
it and toured the chess-playing machine around,

03:13:40.620 --> 03:13:44.220
had used some interesting components to make
his automaton.

03:13:44.220 --> 03:13:48.720
Maybe they were where it derived its skill
at the game, and its apparent temperament

03:13:48.720 --> 03:13:52.200
when the device was made to play at maximum
speed for too long.

03:13:52.200 --> 03:13:56.040
He had used his daughters to make the machine.

03:13:56.040 --> 03:14:01.439
Deep within the heart of this early form of
computer, the two girls brain tissue had been

03:14:01.439 --> 03:14:03.359
hooked up to the electromagnets.

03:14:03.359 --> 03:14:08.639
The machine’s moves were theirs; each pawn
or rook shifting across the black and white

03:14:08.640 --> 03:14:14.220
squares of the board, every rule or strategy
their prodigy father had taught, it all determined

03:14:14.220 --> 03:14:15.540
how the device played.

03:14:15.540 --> 03:14:20.279
Their minds, taken from their skulls, were
now the analytical engine of their father’s

03:14:20.279 --> 03:14:20.700
creation.

03:14:20.700 --> 03:14:25.139
The bones of their fingers, he'd carved into
chess pieces.

03:14:25.140 --> 03:14:31.920
Over the coming weeks, the Foundation naturally
ran their usual slew of tests on SCP-1875,

03:14:31.920 --> 03:14:34.560
playing multiple games of chess against it.

03:14:34.560 --> 03:14:40.080
Every time, they increased the machine’s
five levels of speed, until it started behaving

03:14:40.080 --> 03:14:42.240
erratically while on the highest setting.

03:14:42.240 --> 03:14:48.540
Shortly after, every member of personnel working
on SCP-1875 received a bizarre email.

03:14:48.540 --> 03:14:52.500
It contained a file, named with the Russian
word for chess…

03:14:52.500 --> 03:14:59.220
And a photograph; of the stretched, distorted,
smiling, and screaming faces of two young

03:14:59.220 --> 03:14:59.580
girls.

03:14:59.580 --> 03:15:03.420
Faces that you might be seeing very soon…

